      {
        "id": 561,
        "course": "C02",
        "seq": 65,
        "acip": "DGAG BYA NGOS 'DZIN DNGOS BSHAD PA LA, GZUGS SOGS KYI CHOS RNAMS KUN RDZOB TU YOD TSUL LAS BZLOG PA GCIG BYUNG NA, DE NYID DON DAM LA DPYOD PA'I RTAGS KYI DGAG BYA MTHAR THUG TU 'GYUR BAS, THOG MAR GZUGS SOGS KYI CHOS RNAMS KUN RDZOB TU YOD TSUL BSHAD PA,",
        "wylie": "dgag bya ngos 'dzin dngos bshad pa la, gzugs sogs kyi chos rnams kun rdzob tu yod tshul las bzlog pa gcig byung na, de nyid don dam la dpyod pa'i rtags kyi dgag bya mthar thug tu 'gyur bas, thog mar gzugs sogs kyi chos rnams kun rdzob tu yod tshul bshad pa",
        "english": "Here now is the actual identification of the object we deny. Suppose something were to occur in some way that was opposite to the way that all the phenomena of physical form and so on exist deceptively. Anything that could occur this way would be precisely the final object we deny with reasoning that treats the ultimate. Therefore we must first explain how it is that all the phenomena of physical form and the rest exist deceptively.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C02\", \"acip\": \"DGAG BYA NGOS 'DZIN DNGOS BSHAD PA LA, GZUGS SOGS KYI CHOS RNAMS KUN RDZOB TU YOD TSUL LAS BZLOG PA GCIG BYUNG NA, DE NYID DON DAM LA DPYOD PA'I RTAGS KYI DGAG BYA MTHAR THUG TU 'GYUR BAS, THOG MAR GZUGS SOGS KYI CHOS RNAMS KUN RDZOB TU YOD TSUL BSHAD PA,\", \"wylie\": \"dgag bya ngos 'dzin dngos bshad pa la, gzugs sogs kyi chos rnams kun rdzob tu yod tshul las bzlog pa gcig byung na, de nyid don dam la dpyod pa'i rtags kyi dgag bya mthar thug tu 'gyur bas, thog mar gzugs sogs kyi chos rnams kun rdzob tu yod tshul bshad pa\", \"english\": \"Here now is the actual identification of the object we deny. Suppose something were to occur in some way that was opposite to the way that all the phenomena of physical form and so on exist deceptively. Anything that could occur this way would be precisely the final object we deny with reasoning that treats the ultimate. Therefore we must first explain how it is that all the phenomena of physical form and the rest exist deceptively.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 676,
        "course": "C03",
        "seq": 112,
        "acip": ",SKAD CIG TZAM YANG BZLOG PAR MA GYUR CIG,",
        "wylie": "skad cig tsam yang bzlog par ma gyur cig",
        "english": "kechik tsamyang dokpar magyur chik. Through all the string of my future lives May Tsongkapa the Victor come to me And serve himself as my spiritual guide In the teachings of the Greater Way.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C03\", \"acip\": \",SKAD CIG TZAM YANG BZLOG PAR MA GYUR CIG,\", \"wylie\": \"skad cig tsam yang bzlog par ma gyur cig\", \"english\": \"kechik tsamyang dokpar magyur chik. Through all the string of my future lives May Tsongkapa the Victor come to me And serve himself as my spiritual guide In the teachings of the Greater Way.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 863,
        "course": "C03",
        "seq": 299,
        "acip": "THOG MAR RJE NYID NAS DBU BSKUL GYIS SHER SNYING DANG SENG GDONG BZLOG PA NAS, BDE LEGS SHOG CES PA'I BAR SPYI LTAR DANG ,",
        "wylie": "thog mar rje nyid nas dbu bskul gyis sher snying dang seng gdong bzlog pa nas, bde legs shog ces pa'i bar spyi ltar dang",
        "english": "As he began the teaching, the Lord himself led us in reciting the Essence of Wisdom, and then the prayer of Simhamukhi--the Angel with the Face of a Lion, from the part where we ward off any evil that might disrupt the teaching, all the way up to the words \"May goodness come to be,\" in the way we usually do them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C03\", \"acip\": \"THOG MAR RJE NYID NAS DBU BSKUL GYIS SHER SNYING DANG SENG GDONG BZLOG PA NAS, BDE LEGS SHOG CES PA'I BAR SPYI LTAR DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"thog mar rje nyid nas dbu bskul gyis sher snying dang seng gdong bzlog pa nas, bde legs shog ces pa'i bar spyi ltar dang\", \"english\": \"As he began the teaching, the Lord himself led us in reciting the Essence of Wisdom, and then the prayer of Simhamukhi--the Angel with the Face of a Lion, from the part where we ward off any evil that might disrupt the teaching, all the way up to the words \\\"May goodness come to be,\\\" in the way we usually do them.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 873,
        "course": "C03",
        "seq": 309,
        "acip": "PHYIN CHAD MI KHOM PA'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I THABS NI DA RES CHOS RNAM DAG CIG BSGRUB PA LAS LHAG PA BYAR MED LA, CHOS DE YANG DA LTA NYID NAS MA BSGRUBS NA, RANG NYID 'CHI BAR NGES SHING NAM 'CHI CHA MED PA DANG , 'CHI BA'I 'OG TU JI LTAR 'GYUR SNYAM NA,",
        "wylie": "phyin chad mi khom pa'i skye ba bzlog pa'i thabs ni da res chos rnam dag cig bsgrub pa las lhag pa byar med la, chos de yang da lta nyid nas ma bsgrubs na, rang nyid 'chi bar nges shing nam 'chi cha med pa dang , 'chi ba'i 'og tu ji ltar 'gyur snyam na",
        "english": "The way to avoid ever taking another birth where we have no chance for Dharma is to practice the Dharma purely now; there is no better way at all. And our practice must start this minute; you will die, you must die, it is certain you will die. But you've no idea when. And what will happen after that?",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C03\", \"acip\": \"PHYIN CHAD MI KHOM PA'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I THABS NI DA RES CHOS RNAM DAG CIG BSGRUB PA LAS LHAG PA BYAR MED LA, CHOS DE YANG DA LTA NYID NAS MA BSGRUBS NA, RANG NYID 'CHI BAR NGES SHING NAM 'CHI CHA MED PA DANG , 'CHI BA'I 'OG TU JI LTAR 'GYUR SNYAM NA,\", \"wylie\": \"phyin chad mi khom pa'i skye ba bzlog pa'i thabs ni da res chos rnam dag cig bsgrub pa las lhag pa byar med la, chos de yang da lta nyid nas ma bsgrubs na, rang nyid 'chi bar nges shing nam 'chi cha med pa dang , 'chi ba'i 'og tu ji ltar 'gyur snyam na\", \"english\": \"The way to avoid ever taking another birth where we have no chance for Dharma is to practice the Dharma purely now; there is no better way at all. And our practice must start this minute; you will die, you must die, it is certain you will die. But you've no idea when. And what will happen after that?\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 875,
        "course": "C03",
        "seq": 311,
        "acip": "RE ZHIG NGAN 'GRO'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I SLAD DU DKON MCHOG GSUM LA SKYABS SU 'GRO ZHING , SNGON BYAS KYI SDIG PA LA GNYEN PO STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BSHAGS BSDAMS SHUGS DRAG DANG , DGE MI DGE'I BLANG DOR PHRA ZHING PHRA BA LA 'BAD,",
        "wylie": "re zhig ngan 'gro'i skye ba bzlog pa'i slad du dkon mchog gsum la skyabs su 'gro zhing , sngon byas kyi sdig pa la gnyen po stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bshags bsdams shugs drag dang , dge mi dge'i blang dor phra zhing phra ba la 'bad",
        "english": "We must then for the short term go for shelter to the Three Jewels, to keep ourselves from a birth in the lower realms. We must as well open up and admit the things we have done wrong before, and restrain ourselves in the future. To do so we must make strong and heartfelt use of the four forces9 that counteract the power of the wrong. We must too put all our effort into taking up even the smallest virtue, and giving up even the slightest harms we commit against others.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C03\", \"acip\": \"RE ZHIG NGAN 'GRO'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I SLAD DU DKON MCHOG GSUM LA SKYABS SU 'GRO ZHING , SNGON BYAS KYI SDIG PA LA GNYEN PO STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BSHAGS BSDAMS SHUGS DRAG DANG , DGE MI DGE'I BLANG DOR PHRA ZHING PHRA BA LA 'BAD,\", \"wylie\": \"re zhig ngan 'gro'i skye ba bzlog pa'i slad du dkon mchog gsum la skyabs su 'gro zhing , sngon byas kyi sdig pa la gnyen po stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bshags bsdams shugs drag dang , dge mi dge'i blang dor phra zhing phra ba la 'bad\", \"english\": \"We must then for the short term go for shelter to the Three Jewels, to keep ourselves from a birth in the lower realms. We must as well open up and admit the things we have done wrong before, and restrain ourselves in the future. To do so we must make strong and heartfelt use of the four forces9 that counteract the power of the wrong. We must too put all our effort into taking up even the smallest virtue, and giving up even the slightest harms we commit against others.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1466,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 84,
        "acip": ",DGOS SOGS CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA'I DGOS PA LA, SLOB DPON CHOS MCHOG NI, BSTAN BCOS 'DI LA DGOS PA YOD DAM MED, YOD KYANG SGRUB PAR MI NUS SAM, NUS KYANG 'DOD BYA MA YIN NAM, 'DOD BYA YIN YANG 'BREL PA MED DAM SNYAM PA'I DOGS PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU DGOS SOGS CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA YIN ZHES 'CHAD,",
        "wylie": "dgos sogs chos bzhi bstan pa'i dgos pa la, slob dpon chos mchog ni, bstan bcos 'di la dgos pa yod dam med, yod kyang sgrub par mi nus sam, nus kyang 'dod bya ma yin nam, 'dod bya yin yang 'brel pa med dam snyam pa'i dogs pa bzlog pa'i phyir du dgos sogs chos bzhi bstan pa yin zhes 'chad",
        "english": "Demonstrating that the work possesses the four attributes serves its own purpose. Master Dharmottara explains that It functions to satisfy any doubts that a student might have about whether the commentary has any purpose; if so, whether one could ever achieve that purpose; if so, whether anyone would want to; and, if so, whether there exists any relationship between the text and the realization of its proclaimed purpose.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \",DGOS SOGS CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA'I DGOS PA LA, SLOB DPON CHOS MCHOG NI, BSTAN BCOS 'DI LA DGOS PA YOD DAM MED, YOD KYANG SGRUB PAR MI NUS SAM, NUS KYANG 'DOD BYA MA YIN NAM, 'DOD BYA YIN YANG 'BREL PA MED DAM SNYAM PA'I DOGS PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU DGOS SOGS CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA YIN ZHES 'CHAD,\", \"wylie\": \"dgos sogs chos bzhi bstan pa'i dgos pa la, slob dpon chos mchog ni, bstan bcos 'di la dgos pa yod dam med, yod kyang sgrub par mi nus sam, nus kyang 'dod bya ma yin nam, 'dod bya yin yang 'brel pa med dam snyam pa'i dogs pa bzlog pa'i phyir du dgos sogs chos bzhi bstan pa yin zhes 'chad\", \"english\": \"Demonstrating that the work possesses the four attributes serves its own purpose. Master Dharmottara explains that It functions to satisfy any doubts that a student might have about whether the commentary has any purpose; if so, whether one could ever achieve that purpose; if so, whether anyone would want to; and, if so, whether there exists any relationship between the text and the realization of its proclaimed purpose.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1506,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 124,
        "acip": "THAR PA, ,DAM PA'I DON DU DGE RTZA BA, ,NGO TSA KHREL YOD BDAG NYID KYIS, ,DE DAG DANG LDAN MTSUNGS LDAN GYIS, ,BYA BA LA SOGS KUN SLONG BAS, ,BZLOG PA MI DGE DON DAM PAR, ,LUNG MA BSTAN PA BSTAN PA DAG,",
        "wylie": "thar pa, ,dam pa'i don du dge rtsa ba, ,ngo tsha khrel yod bdag nyid kyis, ,de dag dang ldan mtshungs ldan gyis, ,bya ba la sogs kun slong bas, ,bzlog pa mi dge don dam par, ,lung ma bstan pa bstan pa dag",
        "english": "...Freedom Is the ultimate virtue. The roots as well as Shame and a conscience are so in themselves. Those that are linked with them, by a mental link; Actions and the like, by motivation. Their opposites, non-virtue. The ultimate In the ethically neutral, those described.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"THAR PA, ,DAM PA'I DON DU DGE RTZA BA, ,NGO TSA KHREL YOD BDAG NYID KYIS, ,DE DAG DANG LDAN MTSUNGS LDAN GYIS, ,BYA BA LA SOGS KUN SLONG BAS, ,BZLOG PA MI DGE DON DAM PAR, ,LUNG MA BSTAN PA BSTAN PA DAG,\", \"wylie\": \"thar pa, ,dam pa'i don du dge rtsa ba, ,ngo tsha khrel yod bdag nyid kyis, ,de dag dang ldan mtshungs ldan gyis, ,bya ba la sogs kun slong bas, ,bzlog pa mi dge don dam par, ,lung ma bstan pa bstan pa dag\", \"english\": \"...Freedom Is the ultimate virtue. The roots as well as Shame and a conscience are so in themselves. Those that are linked with them, by a mental link; Actions and the like, by motivation. Their opposites, non-virtue. The ultimate In the ethically neutral, those described.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1511,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 129,
        "acip": "DE DAG LAS BZLOG PA MI DGE BA YIN NO, , JI LTAR NA, 'KHOR BA CHOS CAN, DON DAM PAR MI DGE BA YIN TE, SDUG BSNGAL GYIS BCINGS PAS MCHOG TU MI BDE BA'I PHYIR, NAD BZHIN,",
        "wylie": "de dag las bzlog pa mi dge ba yin no, , ji ltar na, 'khor ba chos can, don dam par mi dge ba yin te, sdug bsngal gyis bcings pas mchog tu mi bde ba'i phyir, nad bzhin",
        "english": "The opposites of each of the above are what we call \"non-virtue\"; the process is as follows. First take the cycle of life. It is the ultimate non-virtue, for it is the highest form of unhappiness--total bondage in suffering. It is like an illness.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"DE DAG LAS BZLOG PA MI DGE BA YIN NO, , JI LTAR NA, 'KHOR BA CHOS CAN, DON DAM PAR MI DGE BA YIN TE, SDUG BSNGAL GYIS BCINGS PAS MCHOG TU MI BDE BA'I PHYIR, NAD BZHIN,\", \"wylie\": \"de dag las bzlog pa mi dge ba yin no, , ji ltar na, 'khor ba chos can, don dam par mi dge ba yin te, sdug bsngal gyis bcings pas mchog tu mi bde ba'i phyir, nad bzhin\", \"english\": \"The opposites of each of the above are what we call \\\"non-virtue\\\"; the process is as follows. First take the cycle of life. It is the ultimate non-virtue, for it is the highest form of unhappiness--total bondage in suffering. It is like an illness.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1623,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 241,
        "acip": "YAN LAG BCU GNYIS PO THAMS CAD KYANG SEMS GCIG LA BRTEN PAR GSUNGS PA LA BRTEN NAS, TZAM GYI SGRAS SEMS GTZO BOR GYUR PAR BSTAN TE, BSHAD PA SNGA MA DGAG PHYOGS DANG PHYI MA SGRUB PHYOGS NAS YIN NO, , DE'I PHYIR 'GRO BA RAB TU 'JUG PA LA SEMS NYID GTZO BO'I RGYU YIN GYI, SEMS LAS GZHAN PA NI GTZO BO'I RGYU MIN PAS, MDO LAS SEMS TZAM ZHIG GTZO BOR BZHAG GI GZUGS NI MA YIN NO, , GAL TE GZUGS YOD PAR 'DOD MOD KYI STE KYANG GZUGS DE LA NI SEMS BZHIN DU 'GRO BA'I BYED PA PO NYID NI YOD PA MA YIN NO, , DES NA SEMS LAS GZHAN PA'I BYED PA PO BZLOG PA STE BKAG GI, PHYI ROL GYI GZUGS NI BKAG PA MA YIN NO, ,",
        "wylie": "yan lag bcu gnyis po thams cad kyang sems gcig la brten par gsungs pa la brten nas, tsam gyi sgras sems gtso bor gyur par bstan te, bshad pa snga ma dgag phyogs dang phyi ma sgrub phyogs nas yin no, , de'i phyir 'gro ba rab tu 'jug pa la sems nyid gtso bo'i rgyu yin gyi, sems las gzhan pa ni gtso bo'i rgyu min pas, mdo las sems tsam zhig gtso bor bzhag gi gzugs ni ma yin no, , gal te gzugs yod par 'dod mod kyi ste kyang gzugs de la ni sems bzhin du 'gro ba'i byed pa po nyid ni yod pa ma yin no, , des na sems las gzhan pa'i byed pa po bzlog pa ste bkag gi, phyi rol gyi gzugs ni bkag pa ma yin no,",
        "english": "It is stated in scripture as well that each and every one of the twelve links in the chain of interdependence depends on a single thing: the mind. These references convey yet another meaning of the word \"only\" in the expression \"mind-only.\" Here the point is that mind is the main thing. The former scriptural references are putting their point in a negative way, and the latter references are putting their point in a positive way. Thus we can see that it's the mind which is the one single principal cause that sets all life into motion. The principal cause is not something other than the mind. Therefore when the sutras speak of \"mind only,\" they are making the point that mind is the main thing, and not matter. Although we do of course admit that physical matter exists, this matter is not the one prime creator of sentient beings in the way that mind is. Therefore all these scriptural references are denying that the opposite of the mind, something other than the mind, could be the creator. They are not though saying that there are no outside physical objects at all. 93 The Asian Classics Institute Course V: How Karma Works Reading Six: How Emptiness Allows Karma to Work, According to the Middle-Way School",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"YAN LAG BCU GNYIS PO THAMS CAD KYANG SEMS GCIG LA BRTEN PAR GSUNGS PA LA BRTEN NAS, TZAM GYI SGRAS SEMS GTZO BOR GYUR PAR BSTAN TE, BSHAD PA SNGA MA DGAG PHYOGS DANG PHYI MA SGRUB PHYOGS NAS YIN NO, , DE'I PHYIR 'GRO BA RAB TU 'JUG PA LA SEMS NYID GTZO BO'I RGYU YIN GYI, SEMS LAS GZHAN PA NI GTZO BO'I RGYU MIN PAS, MDO LAS SEMS TZAM ZHIG GTZO BOR BZHAG GI GZUGS NI MA YIN NO, , GAL TE GZUGS YOD PAR 'DOD MOD KYI STE KYANG GZUGS DE LA NI SEMS BZHIN DU 'GRO BA'I BYED PA PO NYID NI YOD PA MA YIN NO, , DES NA SEMS LAS GZHAN PA'I BYED PA PO BZLOG PA STE BKAG GI, PHYI ROL GYI GZUGS NI BKAG PA MA YIN NO, ,\", \"wylie\": \"yan lag bcu gnyis po thams cad kyang sems gcig la brten par gsungs pa la brten nas, tsam gyi sgras sems gtso bor gyur par bstan te, bshad pa snga ma dgag phyogs dang phyi ma sgrub phyogs nas yin no, , de'i phyir 'gro ba rab tu 'jug pa la sems nyid gtso bo'i rgyu yin gyi, sems las gzhan pa ni gtso bo'i rgyu min pas, mdo las sems tsam zhig gtso bor bzhag gi gzugs ni ma yin no, , gal te gzugs yod par 'dod mod kyi ste kyang gzugs de la ni sems bzhin du 'gro ba'i byed pa po nyid ni yod pa ma yin no, , des na sems las gzhan pa'i byed pa po bzlog pa ste bkag gi, phyi rol gyi gzugs ni bkag pa ma yin no,\", \"english\": \"It is stated in scripture as well that each and every one of the twelve links in the chain of interdependence depends on a single thing: the mind. These references convey yet another meaning of the word \\\"only\\\" in the expression \\\"mind-only.\\\" Here the point is that mind is the main thing. The former scriptural references are putting their point in a negative way, and the latter references are putting their point in a positive way. Thus we can see that it's the mind which is the one single principal cause that sets all life into motion. The principal cause is not something other than the mind. Therefore when the sutras speak of \\\"mind only,\\\" they are making the point that mind is the main thing, and not matter. Although we do of course admit that physical matter exists, this matter is not the one prime creator of sentient beings in the way that mind is. Therefore all these scriptural references are denying that the opposite of the mind, something other than the mind, could be the creator. They are not though saying that there are no outside physical objects at all. 93 The Asian Classics Institute Course V: How Karma Works Reading Six: How Emptiness Allows Karma to Work, According to the Middle-Way School\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1786,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 404,
        "acip": ",DES NA 'PHEN BYED DGE BA LA RDZOGS BYED MI DGE BA DANG , DE LAS BZLOG PA DANG , GNYIS KA DGE BA DANG , MI DGE BA STE MU BZHI LAS, DANG PO NI 'DOD PA'I LHA MI SDUG BSNGAL CAN LTA BU DANG ,",
        "wylie": "des na 'phen byed dge ba la rdzogs byed mi dge ba dang , de las bzlog pa dang , gnyis ka dge ba dang , mi dge ba ste mu bzhi las, dang po ni 'dod pa'i lha mi sdug bsngal can lta bu dang",
        "english": "Thus we can say that all four combinations between the two are possible: projection of a life by virtue but finishing by non-virtue, the reverse, a case where both are virtue, and a case where both are non-virtue. An example of the first would be persons born as pleasure beings or humans but who possessed certain sufferings.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \",DES NA 'PHEN BYED DGE BA LA RDZOGS BYED MI DGE BA DANG , DE LAS BZLOG PA DANG , GNYIS KA DGE BA DANG , MI DGE BA STE MU BZHI LAS, DANG PO NI 'DOD PA'I LHA MI SDUG BSNGAL CAN LTA BU DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"des na 'phen byed dge ba la rdzogs byed mi dge ba dang , de las bzlog pa dang , gnyis ka dge ba dang , mi dge ba ste mu bzhi las, dang po ni 'dod pa'i lha mi sdug bsngal can lta bu dang\", \"english\": \"Thus we can say that all four combinations between the two are possible: projection of a life by virtue but finishing by non-virtue, the reverse, a case where both are virtue, and a case where both are non-virtue. An example of the first would be persons born as pleasure beings or humans but who possessed certain sufferings.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 2471,
        "course": "C08",
        "seq": 126,
        "acip": "DES MA NGOMS PAR SHAR LUS 'PHAGS PO, DES MA NGOMS PAR NUB BA LANG SPYOD, DES MA NGOMS PAR BYANG SGRA MI SNYAN, DE THAMS CAD KYIS MA NGOMS PAR RGYAL CHEN BZHI RIGS KYI LHA GNAS, DES KYANG MA NGOMS PAR SUM CU RTZA GSUM DU PHYIN TE BRGYA BYIN GYI STAN PHYED LA 'DUG STE DE DANG MNYAM POR LO BRGYA DANG SO BDUN BAR DU DBANG BSGYUR, DE NAS SANGS RGYAS 'OD SRUNG GI DUS SU LHA MA YIN GYI DMAG BYUNG BA BRGYA BYIN GYIS KYANG MA BZLOG PA DE SPYI BO SKYES KYIS BZLOG PA NA BRGYA BYIN DANG MTHUN MONG DU DBANG BSKUR BAS MA TSIMS PAR RIL PO 'DOD NAS BRGYA BYIN BSAD DGOS BSAM PA'I NGAN SEMS SKYES PA NA, DES BSOD NAMS ZAD DE 'DZAM BU GLING DU LHUNG ZHING , DE NAS KYANG MI RING BAR 'JIG RTEN GZHAN DU GSHEGS PAR PHYOGS PA NA BLON PO DAG GIS LHA GSHEGS PA'I RGYU DRIS PAS, 'CHI BA'I RGYU NI NGAN SEMS KYIS BYAS PAR SMRA BA SOGS SO,",
        "wylie": "des ma ngoms par shar lus 'phags po, des ma ngoms par nub ba lang spyod, des ma ngoms par byang sgra mi snyan, de thams cad kyis ma ngoms par rgyal chen bzhi rigs kyi lha gnas, des kyang ma ngoms par sum cu rtsa gsum du phyin te brgya byin gyi stan phyed la 'dug ste de dang mnyam por lo brgya dang so bdun bar du dbang bsgyur, de nas sangs rgyas 'od srung gi dus su lha ma yin gyi dmag byung ba brgya byin gyis kyang ma bzlog pa de spyi bo skyes kyis bzlog pa na brgya byin dang mthun mong du dbang bskur bas ma tshims par ril po 'dod nas brgya byin bsad dgos bsam pa'i ngan sems skyes pa na, des bsod nams zad de 'dzam bu gling du lhung zhing , de nas kyang mi ring bar 'jig rten gzhan du gshegs par phyogs pa na blon po dag gis lha gshegs pa'i rgyu dris pas, 'chi ba'i rgyu ni ngan sems kyis byas par smra ba sogs so",
        "english": "Dissatisfied with only this much, he then went to the eastern continent of Greatbody, and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he then went to the western continent of Cattle-Users, and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he hen went to the northern continent of Unpleasant Sound and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he went to the Heaven of the Thirty-Three, and appropriated half the throne of Shakra, the king of the full pleasure beings. He reigned there with this being, for the length of 137 years. Then, during the time of the Buddha named Kashyapa, a war with the lesser pleasure beings broke out. Even Shakra found himself unable to repulse them, but Headborn was successful. Then an evil thought began to form in his mind: dissatisfied with the idea of continuing to share his power with Shakra, he decided that he would have to murder the being. This single thought erased all the good energy that was keeping Headborn in this realm, and he fell back to Dzambu Continent. Not long after that he was on his deathbed, ready to pass on to the next world. His ministers crowded around him, and asked the mighty being what it was that could force him to leave them so. And then it was that he gave his famous reply, describing how his bad thoughts had brought him to death, and so on.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C08\", \"acip\": \"DES MA NGOMS PAR SHAR LUS 'PHAGS PO, DES MA NGOMS PAR NUB BA LANG SPYOD, DES MA NGOMS PAR BYANG SGRA MI SNYAN, DE THAMS CAD KYIS MA NGOMS PAR RGYAL CHEN BZHI RIGS KYI LHA GNAS, DES KYANG MA NGOMS PAR SUM CU RTZA GSUM DU PHYIN TE BRGYA BYIN GYI STAN PHYED LA 'DUG STE DE DANG MNYAM POR LO BRGYA DANG SO BDUN BAR DU DBANG BSGYUR, DE NAS SANGS RGYAS 'OD SRUNG GI DUS SU LHA MA YIN GYI DMAG BYUNG BA BRGYA BYIN GYIS KYANG MA BZLOG PA DE SPYI BO SKYES KYIS BZLOG PA NA BRGYA BYIN DANG MTHUN MONG DU DBANG BSKUR BAS MA TSIMS PAR RIL PO 'DOD NAS BRGYA BYIN BSAD DGOS BSAM PA'I NGAN SEMS SKYES PA NA, DES BSOD NAMS ZAD DE 'DZAM BU GLING DU LHUNG ZHING , DE NAS KYANG MI RING BAR 'JIG RTEN GZHAN DU GSHEGS PAR PHYOGS PA NA BLON PO DAG GIS LHA GSHEGS PA'I RGYU DRIS PAS, 'CHI BA'I RGYU NI NGAN SEMS KYIS BYAS PAR SMRA BA SOGS SO,\", \"wylie\": \"des ma ngoms par shar lus 'phags po, des ma ngoms par nub ba lang spyod, des ma ngoms par byang sgra mi snyan, de thams cad kyis ma ngoms par rgyal chen bzhi rigs kyi lha gnas, des kyang ma ngoms par sum cu rtsa gsum du phyin te brgya byin gyi stan phyed la 'dug ste de dang mnyam por lo brgya dang so bdun bar du dbang bsgyur, de nas sangs rgyas 'od srung gi dus su lha ma yin gyi dmag byung ba brgya byin gyis kyang ma bzlog pa de spyi bo skyes kyis bzlog pa na brgya byin dang mthun mong du dbang bskur bas ma tshims par ril po 'dod nas brgya byin bsad dgos bsam pa'i ngan sems skyes pa na, des bsod nams zad de 'dzam bu gling du lhung zhing , de nas kyang mi ring bar 'jig rten gzhan du gshegs par phyogs pa na blon po dag gis lha gshegs pa'i rgyu dris pas, 'chi ba'i rgyu ni ngan sems kyis byas par smra ba sogs so\", \"english\": \"Dissatisfied with only this much, he then went to the eastern continent of Greatbody, and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he then went to the western continent of Cattle-Users, and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he hen went to the northern continent of Unpleasant Sound and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he went to the Heaven of the Thirty-Three, and appropriated half the throne of Shakra, the king of the full pleasure beings. He reigned there with this being, for the length of 137 years. Then, during the time of the Buddha named Kashyapa, a war with the lesser pleasure beings broke out. Even Shakra found himself unable to repulse them, but Headborn was successful. Then an evil thought began to form in his mind: dissatisfied with the idea of continuing to share his power with Shakra, he decided that he would have to murder the being. This single thought erased all the good energy that was keeping Headborn in this realm, and he fell back to Dzambu Continent. Not long after that he was on his deathbed, ready to pass on to the next world. His ministers crowded around him, and asked the mighty being what it was that could force him to leave them so. And then it was that he gave his famous reply, describing how his bad thoughts had brought him to death, and so on.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 2684,
        "course": "C08",
        "seq": 339,
        "acip": ",'CHI BDAG LA BROS PAS MI THAR ZHING SNGAGS LA SOGS PAS BZLOG TU MED PA NI, RGYAL PO LA GDAMS PA LAS, DPER NA, PHYOGS BZHI NAS RI CHEN PO SRA BA BRTAN PA SNYING PO DANG LDAN PA MA ZHIG PA MA GAS PA 'PHAN MED PA SHIN TU MKHREGS PA GCIG TU STUG PO BZHI GNAM LA REG CING SA BRLOG STE, RTZVA DANG SHING DANG SHING GEL PA DANG YAL GA DANG LO MA THAMS CAD DANG SEMS CAN SROG CHAGS 'BYUNG PO THAMS CAD PHYE MAR 'THAG CING 'ONG BA LA NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO,",
        "wylie": "'chi bdag la bros pas mi thar zhing sngags la sogs pas bzlog tu med pa ni, rgyal po la gdams pa las, dper na, phyogs bzhi nas ri chen po sra ba brtan pa snying po dang ldan pa ma zhig pa ma gas pa 'phan med pa shin tu mkhregs pa gcig tu stug po bzhi gnam la reg cing sa brlog ste, rtsva dang shing dang shing gel pa dang yal ga dang lo ma thams cad dang sems can srog chags 'byung po thams cad phye mar 'thag cing 'ong ba la ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no",
        "english": "Beyond this is the fact that no one can free themselves from the Lord of Death by outrunning him, nor turn him back with magic words or the like. As the scripture known as Advice to the King states, Suppose for example that you were surrounded on all four sides by four great mountains, solid, firm, hard to the core, indestructible, unbreakable, indomitable, adamantine and massive, stretching to the highest limits of the sky. And then suppose suddenly that they caved in on you. Every trace of vegetation--from the roots, to the stalks, to the branches, to the twigs, and out to the leaves, would be crushed to powder. And every living creature there, every sentient being, every spirit too, would be smashed to dust. And as the mountains fell in it would happen in a minute, and no one would be able to outrun them, or force them back, or buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C08\", \"acip\": \",'CHI BDAG LA BROS PAS MI THAR ZHING SNGAGS LA SOGS PAS BZLOG TU MED PA NI, RGYAL PO LA GDAMS PA LAS, DPER NA, PHYOGS BZHI NAS RI CHEN PO SRA BA BRTAN PA SNYING PO DANG LDAN PA MA ZHIG PA MA GAS PA 'PHAN MED PA SHIN TU MKHREGS PA GCIG TU STUG PO BZHI GNAM LA REG CING SA BRLOG STE, RTZVA DANG SHING DANG SHING GEL PA DANG YAL GA DANG LO MA THAMS CAD DANG SEMS CAN SROG CHAGS 'BYUNG PO THAMS CAD PHYE MAR 'THAG CING 'ONG BA LA NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO,\", \"wylie\": \"'chi bdag la bros pas mi thar zhing sngags la sogs pas bzlog tu med pa ni, rgyal po la gdams pa las, dper na, phyogs bzhi nas ri chen po sra ba brtan pa snying po dang ldan pa ma zhig pa ma gas pa 'phan med pa shin tu mkhregs pa gcig tu stug po bzhi gnam la reg cing sa brlog ste, rtsva dang shing dang shing gel pa dang yal ga dang lo ma thams cad dang sems can srog chags 'byung po thams cad phye mar 'thag cing 'ong ba la ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no\", \"english\": \"Beyond this is the fact that no one can free themselves from the Lord of Death by outrunning him, nor turn him back with magic words or the like. As the scripture known as Advice to the King states, Suppose for example that you were surrounded on all four sides by four great mountains, solid, firm, hard to the core, indestructible, unbreakable, indomitable, adamantine and massive, stretching to the highest limits of the sky. And then suppose suddenly that they caved in on you. Every trace of vegetation--from the roots, to the stalks, to the branches, to the twigs, and out to the leaves, would be crushed to powder. And every living creature there, every sentient being, every spirit too, would be smashed to dust. And as the mountains fell in it would happen in a minute, and no one would be able to outrun them, or force them back, or buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 2685,
        "course": "C08",
        "seq": 340,
        "acip": ",RGYAL PO CHEN PO, DE BZHIN DU 'JIGS PA CHEN PO BZHI 'DI DAG 'ONG STE DE DAG LAS MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN GYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO,",
        "wylie": "rgyal po chen po, de bzhin du 'jigs pa chen po bzhi 'di dag 'ong ste de dag las mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman gyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no",
        "english": "Here, great King, our life is the same. When the four great terrors make their appearance, no one can outrun them, nor force them back, nor buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C08\", \"acip\": \",RGYAL PO CHEN PO, DE BZHIN DU 'JIGS PA CHEN PO BZHI 'DI DAG 'ONG STE DE DAG LAS MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN GYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO,\", \"wylie\": \"rgyal po chen po, de bzhin du 'jigs pa chen po bzhi 'di dag 'ong ste de dag las mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman gyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no\", \"english\": \"Here, great King, our life is the same. When the four great terrors make their appearance, no one can outrun them, nor force them back, nor buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 2686,
        "course": "C08",
        "seq": 341,
        "acip": ",BZHI GANG ZHE NA RGA BA DANG NA BA DANG 'CHI BA DANG RGUD PA'O, , RGYAL PO CHEN PO, RGA BA NI DAR LA BAB PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , NA BA NI NAD MED PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , RGUD PA NI PHUN SUM TSOGS PA THAMS CAD 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , 'CHI BA NI SROG 'JOMS SHING 'ONG STE, DE DAG LAS NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS ZHI BAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO, , ZHES SO,",
        "wylie": "bzhi gang zhe na rga ba dang na ba dang 'chi ba dang rgud pa'o, , rgyal po chen po, rga ba ni dar la bab pa 'joms shing 'ong , na ba ni nad med pa 'joms shing 'ong , rgud pa ni phun sum tshogs pa thams cad 'joms shing 'ong , 'chi ba ni srog 'joms shing 'ong ste, de dag las ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis zhi bar sla ba ni ma yin no, , zhes so",
        "english": "And what are these four terrors? They are aging, and sickness, and death, and trouble. Great King, I say to you: aging is the fact that our vitality is inevitably obliterated; sickness is the fact that our health is inevitably obliterated; trouble is the fact that any good thing that ever comes to us is inevitably obliterated; and death is the fact that our very life is inevitably obliterated. All four of these come to us, and we cannot outrun them, nor force them back, nor buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C08\", \"acip\": \",BZHI GANG ZHE NA RGA BA DANG NA BA DANG 'CHI BA DANG RGUD PA'O, , RGYAL PO CHEN PO, RGA BA NI DAR LA BAB PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , NA BA NI NAD MED PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , RGUD PA NI PHUN SUM TSOGS PA THAMS CAD 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , 'CHI BA NI SROG 'JOMS SHING 'ONG STE, DE DAG LAS NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS ZHI BAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO, , ZHES SO,\", \"wylie\": \"bzhi gang zhe na rga ba dang na ba dang 'chi ba dang rgud pa'o, , rgyal po chen po, rga ba ni dar la bab pa 'joms shing 'ong , na ba ni nad med pa 'joms shing 'ong , rgud pa ni phun sum tshogs pa thams cad 'joms shing 'ong , 'chi ba ni srog 'joms shing 'ong ste, de dag las ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis zhi bar sla ba ni ma yin no, , zhes so\", \"english\": \"And what are these four terrors? They are aging, and sickness, and death, and trouble. Great King, I say to you: aging is the fact that our vitality is inevitably obliterated; sickness is the fact that our health is inevitably obliterated; trouble is the fact that any good thing that ever comes to us is inevitably obliterated; and death is the fact that our very life is inevitably obliterated. All four of these come to us, and we cannot outrun them, nor force them back, nor buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 3115,
        "course": "C10",
        "seq": 141,
        "acip": ",DE YANG LTUNG BA PHYIR BCOS NI SDOM PA GONG 'OG SO SO NAS GSUNGS PA LTAR BYA LA SDIG PA PHYIR BCOS PA NI STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BYA STE, CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA'I MDO LAS, BYAMS PA BYANG CHUB SEMS DPA' SEMS DPA' CHEN PO CHOS BZHI DANG LDAN NA SDIG PA BYAS SHING BSAGS PA YANG ZIL GYIS GNON PAR 'GYUR RO, , BZHI GANG ZHE NA, 'DI LTA STE, RNAM PAR SUN 'BYIN PA KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG , GNYEN PO KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG , NYES PA LAS BZLOG PA'I STOBS DANG , RTEN GYI STOBS SO ZHES GSUNGS TE BYAS SHING BSAGS PA'I LAS NI MYONG NGES YIN PAS DE ZIL GYIS GNON NUS NA MA NGES PA LTA CI SMOS,",
        "wylie": "de yang ltung ba phyir bcos ni sdom pa gong 'og so so nas gsungs pa ltar bya la sdig pa phyir bcos pa ni stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bya ste, chos bzhi bstan pa'i mdo las, byams pa byang chub sems dpa' sems dpa' chen po chos bzhi dang ldan na sdig pa byas shing bsags pa yang zil gyis gnon par 'gyur ro, , bzhi gang zhe na, 'di lta ste, rnam par sun 'byin pa kun tu spyod pa dang , gnyen po kun tu spyod pa dang , nyes pa las bzlog pa'i stobs dang , rten gyi stobs so zhes gsungs te byas shing bsags pa'i las ni myong nges yin pas de zil gyis gnon nus na ma nges pa lta ci smos",
        "english": "Instances in which you have committed some transgression of your vows, at whichever level of vows you may have, should be repaired according to the methods prescribed for those particular vows. For repairing the damage caused by other kinds of bad deeds you should utilize the four powers. As the Sutra on the Four Practices states, If, oh Loving One, some great bodhisattva has succeeded in the four practices, then even those bad deeds which have been both committed and collected can be overcome. What are the four practices? They are as follows: the activity of destruction; the activity of applying the antidote; the force of restraining from the particular bad deed; and the force of the foundation. The expression \"committed and collected\" refers to actions whose karmic results are \"certain to be experienced.\" If even these types of karmic results can be overcome by the four practices, then there is no need to mention those which are not certain to be experienced.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C10\", \"acip\": \",DE YANG LTUNG BA PHYIR BCOS NI SDOM PA GONG 'OG SO SO NAS GSUNGS PA LTAR BYA LA SDIG PA PHYIR BCOS PA NI STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BYA STE, CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA'I MDO LAS, BYAMS PA BYANG CHUB SEMS DPA' SEMS DPA' CHEN PO CHOS BZHI DANG LDAN NA SDIG PA BYAS SHING BSAGS PA YANG ZIL GYIS GNON PAR 'GYUR RO, , BZHI GANG ZHE NA, 'DI LTA STE, RNAM PAR SUN 'BYIN PA KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG , GNYEN PO KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG , NYES PA LAS BZLOG PA'I STOBS DANG , RTEN GYI STOBS SO ZHES GSUNGS TE BYAS SHING BSAGS PA'I LAS NI MYONG NGES YIN PAS DE ZIL GYIS GNON NUS NA MA NGES PA LTA CI SMOS,\", \"wylie\": \"de yang ltung ba phyir bcos ni sdom pa gong 'og so so nas gsungs pa ltar bya la sdig pa phyir bcos pa ni stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bya ste, chos bzhi bstan pa'i mdo las, byams pa byang chub sems dpa' sems dpa' chen po chos bzhi dang ldan na sdig pa byas shing bsags pa yang zil gyis gnon par 'gyur ro, , bzhi gang zhe na, 'di lta ste, rnam par sun 'byin pa kun tu spyod pa dang , gnyen po kun tu spyod pa dang , nyes pa las bzlog pa'i stobs dang , rten gyi stobs so zhes gsungs te byas shing bsags pa'i las ni myong nges yin pas de zil gyis gnon nus na ma nges pa lta ci smos\", \"english\": \"Instances in which you have committed some transgression of your vows, at whichever level of vows you may have, should be repaired according to the methods prescribed for those particular vows. For repairing the damage caused by other kinds of bad deeds you should utilize the four powers. As the Sutra on the Four Practices states, If, oh Loving One, some great bodhisattva has succeeded in the four practices, then even those bad deeds which have been both committed and collected can be overcome. What are the four practices? They are as follows: the activity of destruction; the activity of applying the antidote; the force of restraining from the particular bad deed; and the force of the foundation. The expression \\\"committed and collected\\\" refers to actions whose karmic results are \\\"certain to be experienced.\\\" If even these types of karmic results can be overcome by the four practices, then there is no need to mention those which are not certain to be experienced.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 3321,
        "course": "C10",
        "seq": 347,
        "acip": ",GSUM PA NI, DPE DE BZHIN DU PHYI ROL GYI GNOD BYED KYI DNGOS PO YANG MTHA' DAG BDAG GIS PHYIR BZLOG PAR BYED PAS MI LANG GIS, BDAG GI SEMS 'DI YUL DE DAG LA DMIGS NAS KHONG KHRO BSKYED PA LAS PHYIR BZLOG PAR BYA'I DE GOMS PA LAS BZOD PA RDZOGS PAR 'GYUR GYI, KHRO BA'I YUL GZHAN RNAMS BDAG GIS CI ZHIG BZLOG STE BZLOG MI NUS SHING DGOS PA MED PA'I PHYIR RO,",
        "wylie": "gsum pa ni, dpe de bzhin du phyi rol gyi gnod byed kyi dngos po yang mtha' dag bdag gis phyir bzlog par byed pas mi lang gis, bdag gi sems 'di yul de dag la dmigs nas khong khro bskyed pa las phyir bzlog par bya'i de goms pa las bzod pa rdzogs par 'gyur gyi, khro ba'i yul gzhan rnams bdag gis ci zhig bzlog ste bzlog mi nus shing dgos pa med pa'i phyir ro",
        "english": "Here is the third part. My present case is the same as this metaphor. It would be an impossibility for me to stop every single outer object that might bring me any kind of pain. Rather I should stop this tendency of my own mind to focus on these same objects and then feel anger. It is through the constant practice of this action that I could eventually bring patience to its culmination. Why though should I try to stop all the other objects, everything that could ever make me angry? I never could, and there is no need even to try.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C10\", \"acip\": \",GSUM PA NI, DPE DE BZHIN DU PHYI ROL GYI GNOD BYED KYI DNGOS PO YANG MTHA' DAG BDAG GIS PHYIR BZLOG PAR BYED PAS MI LANG GIS, BDAG GI SEMS 'DI YUL DE DAG LA DMIGS NAS KHONG KHRO BSKYED PA LAS PHYIR BZLOG PAR BYA'I DE GOMS PA LAS BZOD PA RDZOGS PAR 'GYUR GYI, KHRO BA'I YUL GZHAN RNAMS BDAG GIS CI ZHIG BZLOG STE BZLOG MI NUS SHING DGOS PA MED PA'I PHYIR RO,\", \"wylie\": \"gsum pa ni, dpe de bzhin du phyi rol gyi gnod byed kyi dngos po yang mtha' dag bdag gis phyir bzlog par byed pas mi lang gis, bdag gi sems 'di yul de dag la dmigs nas khong khro bskyed pa las phyir bzlog par bya'i de goms pa las bzod pa rdzogs par 'gyur gyi, khro ba'i yul gzhan rnams bdag gis ci zhig bzlog ste bzlog mi nus shing dgos pa med pa'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"Here is the third part. My present case is the same as this metaphor. It would be an impossibility for me to stop every single outer object that might bring me any kind of pain. Rather I should stop this tendency of my own mind to focus on these same objects and then feel anger. It is through the constant practice of this action that I could eventually bring patience to its culmination. Why though should I try to stop all the other objects, everything that could ever make me angry? I never could, and there is no need even to try.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 3817,
        "course": "C11",
        "seq": 398,
        "acip": ",CI PHYIR KUN GYI SDUG BSNGAL NI, ,BZLOG PAR BYA ZHES BRTZAD DU MED, ,GAL TE BZLOG NA'ANG THAMS CAD BZLOG,DE MIN BDAG KYANG SEMS CAN BZHIN,",
        "wylie": "ci phyir kun gyi sdug bsngal ni, ,bzlog par bya zhes brtsad du med, ,gal te bzlog na'ang thams cad bzlog,de min bdag kyang sems can bzhin",
        "english": "You can't continue your argument Of asking why the pains of all Are something you must stop. If you're going to stop it, then You must stop all of it; if not, Then mine's like other beings'.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C11\", \"acip\": \",CI PHYIR KUN GYI SDUG BSNGAL NI, ,BZLOG PAR BYA ZHES BRTZAD DU MED, ,GAL TE BZLOG NA'ANG THAMS CAD BZLOG,DE MIN BDAG KYANG SEMS CAN BZHIN,\", \"wylie\": \"ci phyir kun gyi sdug bsngal ni, ,bzlog par bya zhes brtsad du med, ,gal te bzlog na'ang thams cad bzlog,de min bdag kyang sems can bzhin\", \"english\": \"You can't continue your argument Of asking why the pains of all Are something you must stop. If you're going to stop it, then You must stop all of it; if not, Then mine's like other beings'.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 5289,
        "course": "C14",
        "seq": 26,
        "acip": "SHOG,SPYOD LAM KUN TU RANG RGYUD LA, ,RTOG CING NYON MONGS SKYES MA THAG,BDAG GZHAN MA RUNGS BYED PAS NA, ,BTZAN THABS GDONG NAS BZLOG PAR",
        "wylie": "shog,spyod lam kun tu rang rgyud la, ,rtog cing nyon mongs skyes ma thag,bdag gzhan ma rungs byed pas na, ,btsan thabs gdong nas bzlog par",
        "english": "(3) As I go through the day may I watch my mind, To see if a negative thought has come; If it does may I stop it right there, with force, Since it hurts myself and others.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C14\", \"acip\": \"SHOG,SPYOD LAM KUN TU RANG RGYUD LA, ,RTOG CING NYON MONGS SKYES MA THAG,BDAG GZHAN MA RUNGS BYED PAS NA, ,BTZAN THABS GDONG NAS BZLOG PAR\", \"wylie\": \"shog,spyod lam kun tu rang rgyud la, ,rtog cing nyon mongs skyes ma thag,bdag gzhan ma rungs byed pas na, ,btsan thabs gdong nas bzlog par\", \"english\": \"(3) As I go through the day may I watch my mind, To see if a negative thought has come; If it does may I stop it right there, with force, Since it hurts myself and others.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 5303,
        "course": "C14",
        "seq": 40,
        "acip": ",'KHOR BA'I SKYON TSANG SOMS DANG , ,ZHEN PA RTING NAS BZLOG PA'I GNAD YIN,",
        "wylie": "'khor ba'i skyon tshang soms dang , ,zhen pa rting nas bzlog pa'i gnad yin",
        "english": "Think of the viper's nest of the problems of this life; This is the key for stopping Attachment at the bottom of your heart.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C14\", \"acip\": \",'KHOR BA'I SKYON TSANG SOMS DANG , ,ZHEN PA RTING NAS BZLOG PA'I GNAD YIN,\", \"wylie\": \"'khor ba'i skyon tshang soms dang , ,zhen pa rting nas bzlog pa'i gnad yin\", \"english\": \"Think of the viper's nest of the problems of this life; This is the key for stopping Attachment at the bottom of your heart.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 5306,
        "course": "C14",
        "seq": 43,
        "acip": ",SNOD BCUD YI DAM DU SBYONGS DANG , ,THA MAL GYI ZHEN PA BZLOG PA'I GNAD YIN,",
        "wylie": "snod bcud yi dam du sbyongs dang , ,tha mal gyi zhen pa bzlog pa'i gnad yin",
        "english": "See the world and people as Angels; This is the key for stopping The idea that life is ordinary.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C14\", \"acip\": \",SNOD BCUD YI DAM DU SBYONGS DANG , ,THA MAL GYI ZHEN PA BZLOG PA'I GNAD YIN,\", \"wylie\": \"snod bcud yi dam du sbyongs dang , ,tha mal gyi zhen pa bzlog pa'i gnad yin\", \"english\": \"See the world and people as Angels; This is the key for stopping The idea that life is ordinary.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 5348,
        "course": "C14",
        "seq": 85,
        "acip": ",DKA' BA GSUM NYON MONGS PA SKYES PA NA, DRAN PA, BZLOG PA, RGYUN CHOD PA GSUM, LA BSLAB PAR BYA, , RGYU YI GTZO BO RNAM GSUM BLA MA MJAL, CHOS NYAMS SU LEN, CHOS KYI MTHUN RKYEN 'DZOM PA STE, BLANG , , NYAMS PA MED PA RNAM GSUM MOS GUS, SPRO BA, BSLAB BYA, BSGOM, LUS NGAG YID GSUM DGE BA DANG , 'BRAL MED GSUM DANG LDAN PAR BYA,",
        "wylie": "dka' ba gsum nyon mongs pa skyes pa na, dran pa, bzlog pa, rgyun chod pa gsum, la bslab par bya, , rgyu yi gtso bo rnam gsum bla ma mjal, chos nyams su len, chos kyi mthun rkyen 'dzom pa ste, blang , , nyams pa med pa rnam gsum mos gus, spro ba, bslab bya, bsgom, lus ngag yid gsum dge ba dang , 'bral med gsum dang ldan par bya",
        "english": "Train yourself in the three kinds of hardship. Take to yourself the three main causes. Live in three ways that never get weaker. Keep the three that should never be lost. Note by the Tibetan commentator: The \"three hardships\" are learning to detect the start of a mental affliction; to stop it; and to stop the habit. The \"three main causes\" are finding a Lama, practicing the Dharma, and having all the support you need to practice the Dharma come together. The \"three ways\" are feeling devotion [for your Lama], joy [for practicing the lojong or instructions for developing a good heart], and keeping your various vows and commitments [through strong awareness and recollection]. The \"three that should never be lost\" are virtuous actions in your actions, your words, and your thoughts.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C14\", \"acip\": \",DKA' BA GSUM NYON MONGS PA SKYES PA NA, DRAN PA, BZLOG PA, RGYUN CHOD PA GSUM, LA BSLAB PAR BYA, , RGYU YI GTZO BO RNAM GSUM BLA MA MJAL, CHOS NYAMS SU LEN, CHOS KYI MTHUN RKYEN 'DZOM PA STE, BLANG , , NYAMS PA MED PA RNAM GSUM MOS GUS, SPRO BA, BSLAB BYA, BSGOM, LUS NGAG YID GSUM DGE BA DANG , 'BRAL MED GSUM DANG LDAN PAR BYA,\", \"wylie\": \"dka' ba gsum nyon mongs pa skyes pa na, dran pa, bzlog pa, rgyun chod pa gsum, la bslab par bya, , rgyu yi gtso bo rnam gsum bla ma mjal, chos nyams su len, chos kyi mthun rkyen 'dzom pa ste, blang , , nyams pa med pa rnam gsum mos gus, spro ba, bslab bya, bsgom, lus ngag yid gsum dge ba dang , 'bral med gsum dang ldan par bya\", \"english\": \"Train yourself in the three kinds of hardship. Take to yourself the three main causes. Live in three ways that never get weaker. Keep the three that should never be lost. Note by the Tibetan commentator: The \\\"three hardships\\\" are learning to detect the start of a mental affliction; to stop it; and to stop the habit. The \\\"three main causes\\\" are finding a Lama, practicing the Dharma, and having all the support you need to practice the Dharma come together. The \\\"three ways\\\" are feeling devotion [for your Lama], joy [for practicing the lojong or instructions for developing a good heart], and keeping your various vows and commitments [through strong awareness and recollection]. The \\\"three that should never be lost\\\" are virtuous actions in your actions, your words, and your thoughts.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 5427,
        "course": "C14",
        "seq": 164,
        "acip": ",RIM GRO BYAS TSAD LOG PAR SONG BA'I TSE, ,NAG PO'I PHYOGS LA RE LTOS BYAS PA YIS, ,LAS NGAN MTSON CHA RANG LA 'KHOR BA YIN, ,DA NI NAG PO'I PHYOGS LAS BZLOG PAR BYA,",
        "wylie": "rim gro byas tshad log par song ba'i tshe, ,nag po'i phyogs la re ltos byas pa yis, ,las ngan mtshon cha rang la 'khor ba yin, ,da ni nag po'i phyogs las bzlog par bya",
        "english": "When all the attempts I make To honor and offer to holy ones Seem to go awry, It's because the wheel of knives Has turned on me again: The karma of putting my hopes In the negative side of things. From now on then I'll reject All negativity.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C14\", \"acip\": \",RIM GRO BYAS TSAD LOG PAR SONG BA'I TSE, ,NAG PO'I PHYOGS LA RE LTOS BYAS PA YIS, ,LAS NGAN MTSON CHA RANG LA 'KHOR BA YIN, ,DA NI NAG PO'I PHYOGS LAS BZLOG PAR BYA,\", \"wylie\": \"rim gro byas tshad log par song ba'i tshe, ,nag po'i phyogs la re ltos byas pa yis, ,las ngan mtshon cha rang la 'khor ba yin, ,da ni nag po'i phyogs las bzlog par bya\", \"english\": \"When all the attempts I make To honor and offer to holy ones Seem to go awry, It's because the wheel of knives Has turned on me again: The karma of putting my hopes In the negative side of things. From now on then I'll reject All negativity.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 5697,
        "course": "C15",
        "seq": 146,
        "acip": "'KHOR LO DE'I CHE BA NI BLA NA MA MCHIS PA SOGS KYIS STON TE, 'DI MCHOG TU RMAD DU BYUNG ZHING DE LAS LHAG PA GZHAN MED PAS BLA NA MA MCHIS PA DANG , PHYIS MCHOG TU 'GYUR PA'I SKABS DANG GZHIG PA'I SKABS MED PAS SKABS MA MCHIS PA DANG , YOD MED RDZOGS PAR BSTAN PAS NGES PA'I DON DANG , RTZOD PA SMRA BA'I GZHI'I GNAS MIN PA'O, , ZHES WEN TSEG GIS BSHAD PA NI SKABS MED PA'I SNGA MA MA GTOGS PA SNGAR BDAG GIS BLA NA YOD PA SOGS KYI DON BSHAD PA LAS BZLOG PA DANG DON 'DRA'O, ,",
        "wylie": "'khor lo de'i che ba ni bla na ma mchis pa sogs kyis ston te, 'di mchog tu rmad du byung zhing de las lhag pa gzhan med pas bla na ma mchis pa dang , phyis mchog tu 'gyur pa'i skabs dang gzhig pa'i skabs med pas skabs ma mchis pa dang , yod med rdzogs par bstan pas nges pa'i don dang , rtsod pa smra ba'i gzhi'i gnas min pa'o, , zhes wen tsheg gis bshad pa ni skabs med pa'i snga ma ma gtogs pa sngar bdag gis bla na yod pa sogs kyi don bshad pa las bzlog pa dang don 'dra'o,",
        "english": "The greatness of this last turning of the wheel is indicated in the various expressions such as \"one which has nothing higher.\" Because this particular turning of the wheel is supremely awe-inspiring, and because there is no teaching which is any greater, it \"has nothing higher.\" Because there will be no later occasion upon which some more supreme teaching is imparted, and also because with this turning of the wheel there is no opening for others to raise any questions, it \"leaves no opening.\" Because it definitively relates what does exist and what does not, it is a turning of the wheel \"which can be taken literally,\" and which is not a teaching that could serve as a \"basis\" for someone to raise any \"contention.\" This is how Wentsek gives his commentary; except for the former of the two explanations of the expression \"leaves no opening\" or \"occasion,\" the basic meaning of the rest corresponds to the opposite of the meanings I have already listed for the various expressions beginning with \"does have something higher.\"",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"'KHOR LO DE'I CHE BA NI BLA NA MA MCHIS PA SOGS KYIS STON TE, 'DI MCHOG TU RMAD DU BYUNG ZHING DE LAS LHAG PA GZHAN MED PAS BLA NA MA MCHIS PA DANG , PHYIS MCHOG TU 'GYUR PA'I SKABS DANG GZHIG PA'I SKABS MED PAS SKABS MA MCHIS PA DANG , YOD MED RDZOGS PAR BSTAN PAS NGES PA'I DON DANG , RTZOD PA SMRA BA'I GZHI'I GNAS MIN PA'O, , ZHES WEN TSEG GIS BSHAD PA NI SKABS MED PA'I SNGA MA MA GTOGS PA SNGAR BDAG GIS BLA NA YOD PA SOGS KYI DON BSHAD PA LAS BZLOG PA DANG DON 'DRA'O, ,\", \"wylie\": \"'khor lo de'i che ba ni bla na ma mchis pa sogs kyis ston te, 'di mchog tu rmad du byung zhing de las lhag pa gzhan med pas bla na ma mchis pa dang , phyis mchog tu 'gyur pa'i skabs dang gzhig pa'i skabs med pas skabs ma mchis pa dang , yod med rdzogs par bstan pas nges pa'i don dang , rtsod pa smra ba'i gzhi'i gnas min pa'o, , zhes wen tsheg gis bshad pa ni skabs med pa'i snga ma ma gtogs pa sngar bdag gis bla na yod pa sogs kyi don bshad pa las bzlog pa dang don 'dra'o,\", \"english\": \"The greatness of this last turning of the wheel is indicated in the various expressions such as \\\"one which has nothing higher.\\\" Because this particular turning of the wheel is supremely awe-inspiring, and because there is no teaching which is any greater, it \\\"has nothing higher.\\\" Because there will be no later occasion upon which some more supreme teaching is imparted, and also because with this turning of the wheel there is no opening for others to raise any questions, it \\\"leaves no opening.\\\" Because it definitively relates what does exist and what does not, it is a turning of the wheel \\\"which can be taken literally,\\\" and which is not a teaching that could serve as a \\\"basis\\\" for someone to raise any \\\"contention.\\\" This is how Wentsek gives his commentary; except for the former of the two explanations of the expression \\\"leaves no opening\\\" or \\\"occasion,\\\" the basic meaning of the rest corresponds to the opposite of the meanings I have already listed for the various expressions beginning with \\\"does have something higher.\\\"\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 5839,
        "course": "C15",
        "seq": 288,
        "acip": "DE LA DGONGS NAS GANG ZAG TU LTA BA NI BLA'I STONG NYID LA LOG PAR ZIN PA DE NI DE LTA MIN NO ZHES GSUNGS TE, SNGA MA NI SHES BYA LA RMONGS PA TZAM YIN GYI SHES BYA THAMS CAD LA SKUR PA MI 'DEBS SHING GZHI DES DMYAL BAR MI SKYE LA, GZHAN CHOS 'DOD PA YANG PHUNG BAR MI BYED BSLAB PA'I GZHI LA YANG G-YEL BAR MI 'GYUR LA, PHYI MA NI DE DAG LAS BZLOG PAR 'GYUR BAS SO ZHES BYANG SA LAS GSUNGS SO, ,",
        "wylie": "de la dgongs nas gang zag tu lta ba ni bla'i stong nyid la log par zin pa de ni de lta min no zhes gsungs te, snga ma ni shes bya la rmongs pa tsam yin gyi shes bya thams cad la skur pa mi 'debs shing gzhi des dmyal bar mi skye la, gzhan chos 'dod pa yang phung bar mi byed bslab pa'i gzhi la yang g-yel bar mi 'gyur la, phyi ma ni de dag las bzlog par 'gyur bas so zhes byang sa las gsungs so,",
        "english": "It's with this fact in mind that the Levels of the Bodhisattvas has said that: It's been said that \"believing in a self-existent person is no problem compared to having a wrong idea about emptiness.\" The point here is that the former is simply a lack of awareness about knowable things; it does not represent a view in which you discount every knowable object--it is not an act that would throw you to a birth in the hell realms. The other kind of view is not one which would destroy your spiritual aspirations; nor is it one which would cause you to stray from the foundation of the rules. The latter though functions in the exact opposite way.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"DE LA DGONGS NAS GANG ZAG TU LTA BA NI BLA'I STONG NYID LA LOG PAR ZIN PA DE NI DE LTA MIN NO ZHES GSUNGS TE, SNGA MA NI SHES BYA LA RMONGS PA TZAM YIN GYI SHES BYA THAMS CAD LA SKUR PA MI 'DEBS SHING GZHI DES DMYAL BAR MI SKYE LA, GZHAN CHOS 'DOD PA YANG PHUNG BAR MI BYED BSLAB PA'I GZHI LA YANG G-YEL BAR MI 'GYUR LA, PHYI MA NI DE DAG LAS BZLOG PAR 'GYUR BAS SO ZHES BYANG SA LAS GSUNGS SO, ,\", \"wylie\": \"de la dgongs nas gang zag tu lta ba ni bla'i stong nyid la log par zin pa de ni de lta min no zhes gsungs te, snga ma ni shes bya la rmongs pa tsam yin gyi shes bya thams cad la skur pa mi 'debs shing gzhi des dmyal bar mi skye la, gzhan chos 'dod pa yang phung bar mi byed bslab pa'i gzhi la yang g-yel bar mi 'gyur la, phyi ma ni de dag las bzlog par 'gyur bas so zhes byang sa las gsungs so,\", \"english\": \"It's with this fact in mind that the Levels of the Bodhisattvas has said that: It's been said that \\\"believing in a self-existent person is no problem compared to having a wrong idea about emptiness.\\\" The point here is that the former is simply a lack of awareness about knowable things; it does not represent a view in which you discount every knowable object--it is not an act that would throw you to a birth in the hell realms. The other kind of view is not one which would destroy your spiritual aspirations; nor is it one which would cause you to stray from the foundation of the rules. The latter though functions in the exact opposite way.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6275,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 257,
        "acip": "DGAG BYA NGOS 'DZIN DNGOS BSHAD PA LA, GZUGS SOGS KYI CHOS RNAMS KUN RDZOB TU YOD TSUL LAS BZLOG PA GCIG BYUNG NA, DE NYID DON DAM LA DPYOD PA'I RTAGS KYI DGAG BYA MTHAR THUG TU 'GYUR BAS, THOG MAR GZUGS SOGS KYI CHOS RNAMS KUN RDZOB TU YOD TSUL BSHAD PA,",
        "wylie": "dgag bya ngos 'dzin dngos bshad pa la, gzugs sogs kyi chos rnams kun rdzob tu yod tshul las bzlog pa gcig byung na, de nyid don dam la dpyod pa'i rtags kyi dgag bya mthar thug tu 'gyur bas, thog mar gzugs sogs kyi chos rnams kun rdzob tu yod tshul bshad pa",
        "english": "Here now is the actual identification of the object we deny. Suppose something were to occur in some way that was opposite to the way that all the phenomena of physical form and so on exist deceptively. Anything that could occur this way would be precisely the final object we deny with reasoning that treats the ultimate. Therefore we must first explain how it is that all the phenomena of physical form and the rest exist deceptively. 26 Deceptive Reality",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"DGAG BYA NGOS 'DZIN DNGOS BSHAD PA LA, GZUGS SOGS KYI CHOS RNAMS KUN RDZOB TU YOD TSUL LAS BZLOG PA GCIG BYUNG NA, DE NYID DON DAM LA DPYOD PA'I RTAGS KYI DGAG BYA MTHAR THUG TU 'GYUR BAS, THOG MAR GZUGS SOGS KYI CHOS RNAMS KUN RDZOB TU YOD TSUL BSHAD PA,\", \"wylie\": \"dgag bya ngos 'dzin dngos bshad pa la, gzugs sogs kyi chos rnams kun rdzob tu yod tshul las bzlog pa gcig byung na, de nyid don dam la dpyod pa'i rtags kyi dgag bya mthar thug tu 'gyur bas, thog mar gzugs sogs kyi chos rnams kun rdzob tu yod tshul bshad pa\", \"english\": \"Here now is the actual identification of the object we deny. Suppose something were to occur in some way that was opposite to the way that all the phenomena of physical form and so on exist deceptively. Anything that could occur this way would be precisely the final object we deny with reasoning that treats the ultimate. Therefore we must first explain how it is that all the phenomena of physical form and the rest exist deceptively. 26 Deceptive Reality\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6383,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 365,
        "acip": ",SKAD CIG TZAM YANG BZLOG PAR MA GYUR CIG,",
        "wylie": "skad cig tsam yang bzlog par ma gyur cig",
        "english": "kechik tsamyang dokpar magyur chik. Through all the string of my future lives May Tsongkapa the Victor come to me And serve himself as my spiritual guide In the teachings of the Greater Way.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \",SKAD CIG TZAM YANG BZLOG PAR MA GYUR CIG,\", \"wylie\": \"skad cig tsam yang bzlog par ma gyur cig\", \"english\": \"kechik tsamyang dokpar magyur chik. Through all the string of my future lives May Tsongkapa the Victor come to me And serve himself as my spiritual guide In the teachings of the Greater Way.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6412,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 394,
        "acip": "PHYIN CHAD MI KHOM PA'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I THABS NI DA RES CHOS RNAM DAG CIG BSGRUB PA LAS LHAG PA BYAR MED LA, CHOS DE YANG DA LTA NYID NAS MA BSGRUBS NA, RANG NYID 'CHI BAR NGES SHING NAM 'CHI CHA MED PA DANG , 'CHI BA'I 'OG TU JI LTAR 'GYUR SNYAM NA,",
        "wylie": "phyin chad mi khom pa'i skye ba bzlog pa'i thabs ni da res chos rnam dag cig bsgrub pa las lhag pa byar med la, chos de yang da lta nyid nas ma bsgrubs na, rang nyid 'chi bar nges shing nam 'chi cha med pa dang , 'chi ba'i 'og tu ji ltar 'gyur snyam na",
        "english": "The way to avoid ever taking another birth where we have no chance for Dharma is to practice the Dharma purely now; there is no better way at all. And our practice must start this minute; you will die, you must die, it is certain you will die. But you've no idea when. And what will happen after that?",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"PHYIN CHAD MI KHOM PA'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I THABS NI DA RES CHOS RNAM DAG CIG BSGRUB PA LAS LHAG PA BYAR MED LA, CHOS DE YANG DA LTA NYID NAS MA BSGRUBS NA, RANG NYID 'CHI BAR NGES SHING NAM 'CHI CHA MED PA DANG , 'CHI BA'I 'OG TU JI LTAR 'GYUR SNYAM NA,\", \"wylie\": \"phyin chad mi khom pa'i skye ba bzlog pa'i thabs ni da res chos rnam dag cig bsgrub pa las lhag pa byar med la, chos de yang da lta nyid nas ma bsgrubs na, rang nyid 'chi bar nges shing nam 'chi cha med pa dang , 'chi ba'i 'og tu ji ltar 'gyur snyam na\", \"english\": \"The way to avoid ever taking another birth where we have no chance for Dharma is to practice the Dharma purely now; there is no better way at all. And our practice must start this minute; you will die, you must die, it is certain you will die. But you've no idea when. And what will happen after that?\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6414,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 396,
        "acip": "RE ZHIG NGAN 'GRO'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I SLAD DU DKON MCHOG GSUM LA SKYABS SU 'GRO ZHING , SNGON BYAS KYI SDIG PA LA GNYEN PO STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BSHAGS BSDAMS SHUGS DRAG DANG , DGE MI DGE'I BLANG DOR PHRA ZHING PHRA BA LA 'BAD,",
        "wylie": "re zhig ngan 'gro'i skye ba bzlog pa'i slad du dkon mchog gsum la skyabs su 'gro zhing , sngon byas kyi sdig pa la gnyen po stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bshags bsdams shugs drag dang , dge mi dge'i blang dor phra zhing phra ba la 'bad",
        "english": "We must then for the short term go for shelter to the Three Jewels, to keep ourselves from a birth in the lower realms. We must as well open up and admit the things we have done wrong before, and restrain ourselves in the future. To do so we must make strong and heartfelt use of the four forces that counteract the power of the wrong. We must too put all our effort into taking up even the smallest virtue, and giving up even the slightest harms we commit against others.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"RE ZHIG NGAN 'GRO'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I SLAD DU DKON MCHOG GSUM LA SKYABS SU 'GRO ZHING , SNGON BYAS KYI SDIG PA LA GNYEN PO STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BSHAGS BSDAMS SHUGS DRAG DANG , DGE MI DGE'I BLANG DOR PHRA ZHING PHRA BA LA 'BAD,\", \"wylie\": \"re zhig ngan 'gro'i skye ba bzlog pa'i slad du dkon mchog gsum la skyabs su 'gro zhing , sngon byas kyi sdig pa la gnyen po stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bshags bsdams shugs drag dang , dge mi dge'i blang dor phra zhing phra ba la 'bad\", \"english\": \"We must then for the short term go for shelter to the Three Jewels, to keep ourselves from a birth in the lower realms. We must as well open up and admit the things we have done wrong before, and restrain ourselves in the future. To do so we must make strong and heartfelt use of the four forces that counteract the power of the wrong. We must too put all our effort into taking up even the smallest virtue, and giving up even the slightest harms we commit against others.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6752,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 734,
        "acip": "THAR PA, ,DAM PA'I DON DU DGE RTZA BA, ,NGO TSA KHREL YOD BDAG NYID KYIS, ,DE DAG DANG LDAN MTSUNGS LDAN GYIS, ,BYA BA LA SOGS KUN SLONG BAS, ,BZLOG PA MI DGE DON DAM PAR, ,LUNG MA BSTAN PA BSTAN PA DAG,",
        "wylie": "thar pa, ,dam pa'i don du dge rtsa ba, ,ngo tsha khrel yod bdag nyid kyis, ,de dag dang ldan mtshungs ldan gyis, ,bya ba la sogs kun slong bas, ,bzlog pa mi dge don dam par, ,lung ma bstan pa bstan pa dag",
        "english": "...Freedom Is the ultimate virtue. The roots as well as Shame and a conscience are so in themselves. Those that are linked with them, by a mental link; Actions and the like, by motivation. Their opposites, non-virtue. The ultimate In the ethically neutral, those described.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"THAR PA, ,DAM PA'I DON DU DGE RTZA BA, ,NGO TSA KHREL YOD BDAG NYID KYIS, ,DE DAG DANG LDAN MTSUNGS LDAN GYIS, ,BYA BA LA SOGS KUN SLONG BAS, ,BZLOG PA MI DGE DON DAM PAR, ,LUNG MA BSTAN PA BSTAN PA DAG,\", \"wylie\": \"thar pa, ,dam pa'i don du dge rtsa ba, ,ngo tsha khrel yod bdag nyid kyis, ,de dag dang ldan mtshungs ldan gyis, ,bya ba la sogs kun slong bas, ,bzlog pa mi dge don dam par, ,lung ma bstan pa bstan pa dag\", \"english\": \"...Freedom Is the ultimate virtue. The roots as well as Shame and a conscience are so in themselves. Those that are linked with them, by a mental link; Actions and the like, by motivation. Their opposites, non-virtue. The ultimate In the ethically neutral, those described.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6757,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 739,
        "acip": "DE DAG LAS BZLOG PA MI DGE BA YIN NO, , JI LTAR NA, 'KHOR BA CHOS CAN, DON DAM PAR MI DGE BA YIN TE, SDUG BSNGAL GYIS BCINGS PAS MCHOG TU MI BDE BA'I PHYIR, NAD BZHIN,",
        "wylie": "de dag las bzlog pa mi dge ba yin no, , ji ltar na, 'khor ba chos can, don dam par mi dge ba yin te, sdug bsngal gyis bcings pas mchog tu mi bde ba'i phyir, nad bzhin",
        "english": "The opposites of each of the above are what we call \"non-virtue\"; the process is as follows. First take the cycle of life. It is the ultimate non-virtue, for it is the highest form of unhappiness--total bondage in suffering. It is like an illness.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"DE DAG LAS BZLOG PA MI DGE BA YIN NO, , JI LTAR NA, 'KHOR BA CHOS CAN, DON DAM PAR MI DGE BA YIN TE, SDUG BSNGAL GYIS BCINGS PAS MCHOG TU MI BDE BA'I PHYIR, NAD BZHIN,\", \"wylie\": \"de dag las bzlog pa mi dge ba yin no, , ji ltar na, 'khor ba chos can, don dam par mi dge ba yin te, sdug bsngal gyis bcings pas mchog tu mi bde ba'i phyir, nad bzhin\", \"english\": \"The opposites of each of the above are what we call \\\"non-virtue\\\"; the process is as follows. First take the cycle of life. It is the ultimate non-virtue, for it is the highest form of unhappiness--total bondage in suffering. It is like an illness.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6836,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 818,
        "acip": "YAN LAG BCU GNYIS PO THAMS CAD KYANG SEMS GCIG LA BRTEN PAR GSUNGS PA LA BRTEN NAS, TZAM GYI SGRAS SEMS GTZO BOR GYUR PAR BSTAN TE, BSHAD PA SNGA MA DGAG PHYOGS DANG PHYI MA SGRUB PHYOGS NAS YIN NO, , DE'I PHYIR 'GRO BA RAB TU 'JUG PA LA SEMS NYID GTZO BO'I RGYU YIN GYI, SEMS LAS GZHAN PA NI GTZO BO'I RGYU MIN PAS, MDO LAS SEMS TZAM ZHIG GTZO BOR BZHAG GI GZUGS NI MA YIN NO, , GAL TE GZUGS YOD PAR 'DOD MOD KYI STE KYANG GZUGS DE LA NI SEMS BZHIN DU 'GRO BA'I BYED PA PO NYID NI YOD PA MA YIN NO, , DES NA SEMS LAS GZHAN PA'I BYED PA PO BZLOG PA STE BKAG GI, PHYI ROL GYI GZUGS NI BKAG PA MA YIN NO, ,",
        "wylie": "yan lag bcu gnyis po thams cad kyang sems gcig la brten par gsungs pa la brten nas, tsam gyi sgras sems gtso bor gyur par bstan te, bshad pa snga ma dgag phyogs dang phyi ma sgrub phyogs nas yin no, , de'i phyir 'gro ba rab tu 'jug pa la sems nyid gtso bo'i rgyu yin gyi, sems las gzhan pa ni gtso bo'i rgyu min pas, mdo las sems tsam zhig gtso bor bzhag gi gzugs ni ma yin no, , gal te gzugs yod par 'dod mod kyi ste kyang gzugs de la ni sems bzhin du 'gro ba'i byed pa po nyid ni yod pa ma yin no, , des na sems las gzhan pa'i byed pa po bzlog pa ste bkag gi, phyi rol gyi gzugs ni bkag pa ma yin no,",
        "english": "It is stated in scripture as well that each and every one of the twelve links in the chain of interdependence depends on a single thing: the mind. These references convey yet another meaning of the word \"only\" in the expression \"mind-only.\" Here the point is that mind is the main thing. The former scriptural references are putting their point in a negative way, and the latter references are putting their point in a positive way. Thus we can see that it's the mind which is the one single principal cause that sets all life into motion. The principal cause is not something other than the mind. Therefore when the sutras speak of \"mind only,\" they are making the point that mind is the main thing, and not matter. Although we do of course admit that physical matter exists, this matter is not the one prime creator of sentient beings in the way that mind is. Therefore all these scriptural references are denying that the opposite of the mind, something other than the mind, could be the creator. They are not though saying that there are no outside physical objects at all. 358 The Asian Classics Institute Course XVI: The Great Ideas of Buddhism, Part One Reading Ten: How Karma Works, Part Two 27 On the Cause of Our Suffering The following selections are from the Overview of the Middle Way, composed by Master Kedrup Tenpa Dargye (1493-1568).",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"YAN LAG BCU GNYIS PO THAMS CAD KYANG SEMS GCIG LA BRTEN PAR GSUNGS PA LA BRTEN NAS, TZAM GYI SGRAS SEMS GTZO BOR GYUR PAR BSTAN TE, BSHAD PA SNGA MA DGAG PHYOGS DANG PHYI MA SGRUB PHYOGS NAS YIN NO, , DE'I PHYIR 'GRO BA RAB TU 'JUG PA LA SEMS NYID GTZO BO'I RGYU YIN GYI, SEMS LAS GZHAN PA NI GTZO BO'I RGYU MIN PAS, MDO LAS SEMS TZAM ZHIG GTZO BOR BZHAG GI GZUGS NI MA YIN NO, , GAL TE GZUGS YOD PAR 'DOD MOD KYI STE KYANG GZUGS DE LA NI SEMS BZHIN DU 'GRO BA'I BYED PA PO NYID NI YOD PA MA YIN NO, , DES NA SEMS LAS GZHAN PA'I BYED PA PO BZLOG PA STE BKAG GI, PHYI ROL GYI GZUGS NI BKAG PA MA YIN NO, ,\", \"wylie\": \"yan lag bcu gnyis po thams cad kyang sems gcig la brten par gsungs pa la brten nas, tsam gyi sgras sems gtso bor gyur par bstan te, bshad pa snga ma dgag phyogs dang phyi ma sgrub phyogs nas yin no, , de'i phyir 'gro ba rab tu 'jug pa la sems nyid gtso bo'i rgyu yin gyi, sems las gzhan pa ni gtso bo'i rgyu min pas, mdo las sems tsam zhig gtso bor bzhag gi gzugs ni ma yin no, , gal te gzugs yod par 'dod mod kyi ste kyang gzugs de la ni sems bzhin du 'gro ba'i byed pa po nyid ni yod pa ma yin no, , des na sems las gzhan pa'i byed pa po bzlog pa ste bkag gi, phyi rol gyi gzugs ni bkag pa ma yin no,\", \"english\": \"It is stated in scripture as well that each and every one of the twelve links in the chain of interdependence depends on a single thing: the mind. These references convey yet another meaning of the word \\\"only\\\" in the expression \\\"mind-only.\\\" Here the point is that mind is the main thing. The former scriptural references are putting their point in a negative way, and the latter references are putting their point in a positive way. Thus we can see that it's the mind which is the one single principal cause that sets all life into motion. The principal cause is not something other than the mind. Therefore when the sutras speak of \\\"mind only,\\\" they are making the point that mind is the main thing, and not matter. Although we do of course admit that physical matter exists, this matter is not the one prime creator of sentient beings in the way that mind is. Therefore all these scriptural references are denying that the opposite of the mind, something other than the mind, could be the creator. They are not though saying that there are no outside physical objects at all. 358 The Asian Classics Institute Course XVI: The Great Ideas of Buddhism, Part One Reading Ten: How Karma Works, Part Two 27 On the Cause of Our Suffering The following selections are from the Overview of the Middle Way, composed by Master Kedrup Tenpa Dargye (1493-1568).\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6938,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 920,
        "acip": ",DES NA 'PHEN BYED DGE BA LA RDZOGS BYED MI DGE BA DANG , DE LAS BZLOG PA DANG , GNYIS KA DGE BA DANG , MI DGE BA STE MU BZHI LAS, DANG PO NI 'DOD PA'I LHA MI SDUG BSNGAL CAN LTA BU DANG ,",
        "wylie": "des na 'phen byed dge ba la rdzogs byed mi dge ba dang , de las bzlog pa dang , gnyis ka dge ba dang , mi dge ba ste mu bzhi las, dang po ni 'dod pa'i lha mi sdug bsngal can lta bu dang",
        "english": "Thus we can say that all four combinations between the two are possible: projection of a life by virtue but finishing by non-virtue, the reverse, a case where both are virtue, and a case where both are non-virtue. An example of the first would be persons born as pleasure beings or humans but who possessed certain sufferings.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \",DES NA 'PHEN BYED DGE BA LA RDZOGS BYED MI DGE BA DANG , DE LAS BZLOG PA DANG , GNYIS KA DGE BA DANG , MI DGE BA STE MU BZHI LAS, DANG PO NI 'DOD PA'I LHA MI SDUG BSNGAL CAN LTA BU DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"des na 'phen byed dge ba la rdzogs byed mi dge ba dang , de las bzlog pa dang , gnyis ka dge ba dang , mi dge ba ste mu bzhi las, dang po ni 'dod pa'i lha mi sdug bsngal can lta bu dang\", \"english\": \"Thus we can say that all four combinations between the two are possible: projection of a life by virtue but finishing by non-virtue, the reverse, a case where both are virtue, and a case where both are non-virtue. An example of the first would be persons born as pleasure beings or humans but who possessed certain sufferings.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7340,
        "course": "C17",
        "seq": 302,
        "acip": "DES MA NGOMS PAR SHAR LUS 'PHAGS PO, DES MA NGOMS PAR NUB BA LANG SPYOD, DES MA NGOMS PAR BYANG SGRA MI SNYAN, DE THAMS CAD KYIS MA NGOMS PAR RGYAL CHEN BZHI RIGS KYI LHA GNAS, DES KYANG MA NGOMS PAR SUM CU RTZA GSUM DU PHYIN TE BRGYA BYIN GYI STAN PHYED LA 'DUG STE DE DANG MNYAM POR LO BRGYA DANG SO BDUN BAR DU DBANG BSGYUR, DE NAS SANGS RGYAS 'OD SRUNG GI DUS SU LHA MA YIN GYI DMAG BYUNG BA BRGYA BYIN GYIS KYANG MA BZLOG PA DE SPYI BO SKYES KYIS BZLOG PA NA BRGYA BYIN DANG MTHUN MONG DU DBANG BSKUR BAS MA TSIMS PAR RIL PO 'DOD NAS BRGYA BYIN BSAD DGOS BSAM PA'I NGAN SEMS SKYES PA NA, DES BSOD NAMS ZAD DE 'DZAM BU GLING DU LHUNG ZHING , DE NAS KYANG MI RING BAR 'JIG RTEN GZHAN DU GSHEGS PAR PHYOGS PA NA BLON PO DAG GIS LHA GSHEGS PA'I RGYU DRIS PAS, 'CHI BA'I RGYU NI NGAN SEMS KYIS BYAS PAR SMRA BA SOGS SO,",
        "wylie": "des ma ngoms par shar lus 'phags po, des ma ngoms par nub ba lang spyod, des ma ngoms par byang sgra mi snyan, de thams cad kyis ma ngoms par rgyal chen bzhi rigs kyi lha gnas, des kyang ma ngoms par sum cu rtsa gsum du phyin te brgya byin gyi stan phyed la 'dug ste de dang mnyam por lo brgya dang so bdun bar du dbang bsgyur, de nas sangs rgyas 'od srung gi dus su lha ma yin gyi dmag byung ba brgya byin gyis kyang ma bzlog pa de spyi bo skyes kyis bzlog pa na brgya byin dang mthun mong du dbang bskur bas ma tshims par ril po 'dod nas brgya byin bsad dgos bsam pa'i ngan sems skyes pa na, des bsod nams zad de 'dzam bu gling du lhung zhing , de nas kyang mi ring bar 'jig rten gzhan du gshegs par phyogs pa na blon po dag gis lha gshegs pa'i rgyu dris pas, 'chi ba'i rgyu ni ngan sems kyis byas par smra ba sogs so",
        "english": "Dissatisfied with only this much, he then went to the eastern continent of Greatbody, and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he then went to the western continent of Cattle-Users, and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he hen went to the northern continent of Unpleasant Sound and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he went to the Heaven of the Thirty-Three, and appropriated half the throne of Shakra, the king of the full pleasure beings. He reigned there with this being, for the length of 137 years. Then, during the time of the Buddha named Kashyapa, a war with the lesser pleasure beings broke out. Even Shakra found himself unable to repulse them, but Headborn was successful. Then an evil thought began to form in his mind: dissatisfied with the idea of continuing to share his power with Shakra, he decided that he would have to murder the being. This single thought erased all the good energy that was keeping Headborn in this realm, and he fell back to Dzambu Continent. Not long after that he was on his deathbed, ready to pass on to the next world. His ministers crowded around him, and asked the mighty being what it was that could force him to leave them so. And then it was that he gave his famous reply, describing how his bad thoughts had brought him to death, and so on.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C17\", \"acip\": \"DES MA NGOMS PAR SHAR LUS 'PHAGS PO, DES MA NGOMS PAR NUB BA LANG SPYOD, DES MA NGOMS PAR BYANG SGRA MI SNYAN, DE THAMS CAD KYIS MA NGOMS PAR RGYAL CHEN BZHI RIGS KYI LHA GNAS, DES KYANG MA NGOMS PAR SUM CU RTZA GSUM DU PHYIN TE BRGYA BYIN GYI STAN PHYED LA 'DUG STE DE DANG MNYAM POR LO BRGYA DANG SO BDUN BAR DU DBANG BSGYUR, DE NAS SANGS RGYAS 'OD SRUNG GI DUS SU LHA MA YIN GYI DMAG BYUNG BA BRGYA BYIN GYIS KYANG MA BZLOG PA DE SPYI BO SKYES KYIS BZLOG PA NA BRGYA BYIN DANG MTHUN MONG DU DBANG BSKUR BAS MA TSIMS PAR RIL PO 'DOD NAS BRGYA BYIN BSAD DGOS BSAM PA'I NGAN SEMS SKYES PA NA, DES BSOD NAMS ZAD DE 'DZAM BU GLING DU LHUNG ZHING , DE NAS KYANG MI RING BAR 'JIG RTEN GZHAN DU GSHEGS PAR PHYOGS PA NA BLON PO DAG GIS LHA GSHEGS PA'I RGYU DRIS PAS, 'CHI BA'I RGYU NI NGAN SEMS KYIS BYAS PAR SMRA BA SOGS SO,\", \"wylie\": \"des ma ngoms par shar lus 'phags po, des ma ngoms par nub ba lang spyod, des ma ngoms par byang sgra mi snyan, de thams cad kyis ma ngoms par rgyal chen bzhi rigs kyi lha gnas, des kyang ma ngoms par sum cu rtsa gsum du phyin te brgya byin gyi stan phyed la 'dug ste de dang mnyam por lo brgya dang so bdun bar du dbang bsgyur, de nas sangs rgyas 'od srung gi dus su lha ma yin gyi dmag byung ba brgya byin gyis kyang ma bzlog pa de spyi bo skyes kyis bzlog pa na brgya byin dang mthun mong du dbang bskur bas ma tshims par ril po 'dod nas brgya byin bsad dgos bsam pa'i ngan sems skyes pa na, des bsod nams zad de 'dzam bu gling du lhung zhing , de nas kyang mi ring bar 'jig rten gzhan du gshegs par phyogs pa na blon po dag gis lha gshegs pa'i rgyu dris pas, 'chi ba'i rgyu ni ngan sems kyis byas par smra ba sogs so\", \"english\": \"Dissatisfied with only this much, he then went to the eastern continent of Greatbody, and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he then went to the western continent of Cattle-Users, and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he hen went to the northern continent of Unpleasant Sound and took it over as well. Dissatisfied with only this much, he went to the Heaven of the Thirty-Three, and appropriated half the throne of Shakra, the king of the full pleasure beings. He reigned there with this being, for the length of 137 years. Then, during the time of the Buddha named Kashyapa, a war with the lesser pleasure beings broke out. Even Shakra found himself unable to repulse them, but Headborn was successful. Then an evil thought began to form in his mind: dissatisfied with the idea of continuing to share his power with Shakra, he decided that he would have to murder the being. This single thought erased all the good energy that was keeping Headborn in this realm, and he fell back to Dzambu Continent. Not long after that he was on his deathbed, ready to pass on to the next world. His ministers crowded around him, and asked the mighty being what it was that could force him to leave them so. And then it was that he gave his famous reply, describing how his bad thoughts had brought him to death, and so on.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7466,
        "course": "C17",
        "seq": 428,
        "acip": ",'CHI BDAG LA BROS PAS MI THAR ZHING SNGAGS LA SOGS PAS BZLOG TU MED PA NI, RGYAL PO LA GDAMS PA LAS, DPER NA, PHYOGS BZHI NAS RI CHEN PO SRA BA BRTAN PA SNYING PO DANG LDAN PA MA ZHIG PA MA GAS PA 'PHAN MED PA SHIN TU MKHREGS PA GCIG TU STUG PO BZHI GNAM LA REG CING SA BRLOG STE, RTZVA DANG SHING DANG SHING GEL PA DANG YAL GA DANG LO MA THAMS CAD DANG SEMS CAN SROG CHAGS 'BYUNG PO THAMS CAD PHYE MAR 'THAG CING 'ONG BA LA NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO,",
        "wylie": "'chi bdag la bros pas mi thar zhing sngags la sogs pas bzlog tu med pa ni, rgyal po la gdams pa las, dper na, phyogs bzhi nas ri chen po sra ba brtan pa snying po dang ldan pa ma zhig pa ma gas pa 'phan med pa shin tu mkhregs pa gcig tu stug po bzhi gnam la reg cing sa brlog ste, rtsva dang shing dang shing gel pa dang yal ga dang lo ma thams cad dang sems can srog chags 'byung po thams cad phye mar 'thag cing 'ong ba la ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no",
        "english": "Beyond this is the fact that no one can free themselves from the Lord of Death by outrunning him, nor turn him back with magic words or the like. As the scripture known as Advice to the King states, Suppose for example that you were surrounded on all four sides by four great mountains, solid, firm, hard to the core, indestructible, unbreakable, indomitable, adamantine and massive, stretching to the highest limits of the sky. And then suppose suddenly that they caved in on you. Every trace of vegetation--from the roots, to the stalks, to the branches, to the twigs, and out to the leaves, would be crushed to powder. And every living creature there, every sentient being, every spirit too, would be smashed to dust. And as the mountains fell in it would happen in a minute, and no one would be able to outrun them, or force them back, or buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C17\", \"acip\": \",'CHI BDAG LA BROS PAS MI THAR ZHING SNGAGS LA SOGS PAS BZLOG TU MED PA NI, RGYAL PO LA GDAMS PA LAS, DPER NA, PHYOGS BZHI NAS RI CHEN PO SRA BA BRTAN PA SNYING PO DANG LDAN PA MA ZHIG PA MA GAS PA 'PHAN MED PA SHIN TU MKHREGS PA GCIG TU STUG PO BZHI GNAM LA REG CING SA BRLOG STE, RTZVA DANG SHING DANG SHING GEL PA DANG YAL GA DANG LO MA THAMS CAD DANG SEMS CAN SROG CHAGS 'BYUNG PO THAMS CAD PHYE MAR 'THAG CING 'ONG BA LA NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO,\", \"wylie\": \"'chi bdag la bros pas mi thar zhing sngags la sogs pas bzlog tu med pa ni, rgyal po la gdams pa las, dper na, phyogs bzhi nas ri chen po sra ba brtan pa snying po dang ldan pa ma zhig pa ma gas pa 'phan med pa shin tu mkhregs pa gcig tu stug po bzhi gnam la reg cing sa brlog ste, rtsva dang shing dang shing gel pa dang yal ga dang lo ma thams cad dang sems can srog chags 'byung po thams cad phye mar 'thag cing 'ong ba la ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no\", \"english\": \"Beyond this is the fact that no one can free themselves from the Lord of Death by outrunning him, nor turn him back with magic words or the like. As the scripture known as Advice to the King states, Suppose for example that you were surrounded on all four sides by four great mountains, solid, firm, hard to the core, indestructible, unbreakable, indomitable, adamantine and massive, stretching to the highest limits of the sky. And then suppose suddenly that they caved in on you. Every trace of vegetation--from the roots, to the stalks, to the branches, to the twigs, and out to the leaves, would be crushed to powder. And every living creature there, every sentient being, every spirit too, would be smashed to dust. And as the mountains fell in it would happen in a minute, and no one would be able to outrun them, or force them back, or buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7467,
        "course": "C17",
        "seq": 429,
        "acip": ",RGYAL PO CHEN PO, DE BZHIN DU 'JIGS PA CHEN PO BZHI 'DI DAG 'ONG STE DE DAG LAS MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN GYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO,",
        "wylie": "rgyal po chen po, de bzhin du 'jigs pa chen po bzhi 'di dag 'ong ste de dag las mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman gyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no",
        "english": "Here, great King, our life is the same. When the four great terrors make their appearance, no one can outrun them, nor force them back, nor buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C17\", \"acip\": \",RGYAL PO CHEN PO, DE BZHIN DU 'JIGS PA CHEN PO BZHI 'DI DAG 'ONG STE DE DAG LAS MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN GYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO,\", \"wylie\": \"rgyal po chen po, de bzhin du 'jigs pa chen po bzhi 'di dag 'ong ste de dag las mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman gyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no\", \"english\": \"Here, great King, our life is the same. When the four great terrors make their appearance, no one can outrun them, nor force them back, nor buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7468,
        "course": "C17",
        "seq": 430,
        "acip": ",BZHI GANG ZHE NA RGA BA DANG NA BA DANG 'CHI BA DANG RGUD PA'O, , RGYAL PO CHEN PO, RGA BA NI DAR LA BAB PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , NA BA NI NAD MED PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , RGUD PA NI PHUN SUM TSOGS PA THAMS CAD 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , 'CHI BA NI SROG 'JOMS SHING 'ONG STE, DE DAG LAS NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS ZHI BAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO, , ZHES SO,",
        "wylie": "bzhi gang zhe na rga ba dang na ba dang 'chi ba dang rgud pa'o, , rgyal po chen po, rga ba ni dar la bab pa 'joms shing 'ong , na ba ni nad med pa 'joms shing 'ong , rgud pa ni phun sum tshogs pa thams cad 'joms shing 'ong , 'chi ba ni srog 'joms shing 'ong ste, de dag las ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis zhi bar sla ba ni ma yin no, , zhes so",
        "english": "And what are these four terrors? They are aging, and sickness, and death, and trouble. Great King, I say to you: aging is the fact that our vitality is inevitably obliterated; sickness is the fact that our health is inevitably obliterated; trouble is the fact that any good thing that ever comes to us is inevitably obliterated; and death is the fact that our very life is inevitably obliterated. All four of these come to us, and we cannot outrun them, nor force them back, nor buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C17\", \"acip\": \",BZHI GANG ZHE NA RGA BA DANG NA BA DANG 'CHI BA DANG RGUD PA'O, , RGYAL PO CHEN PO, RGA BA NI DAR LA BAB PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , NA BA NI NAD MED PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , RGUD PA NI PHUN SUM TSOGS PA THAMS CAD 'JOMS SHING 'ONG , 'CHI BA NI SROG 'JOMS SHING 'ONG STE, DE DAG LAS NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS ZHI BAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO, , ZHES SO,\", \"wylie\": \"bzhi gang zhe na rga ba dang na ba dang 'chi ba dang rgud pa'o, , rgyal po chen po, rga ba ni dar la bab pa 'joms shing 'ong , na ba ni nad med pa 'joms shing 'ong , rgud pa ni phun sum tshogs pa thams cad 'joms shing 'ong , 'chi ba ni srog 'joms shing 'ong ste, de dag las ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis zhi bar sla ba ni ma yin no, , zhes so\", \"english\": \"And what are these four terrors? They are aging, and sickness, and death, and trouble. Great King, I say to you: aging is the fact that our vitality is inevitably obliterated; sickness is the fact that our health is inevitably obliterated; trouble is the fact that any good thing that ever comes to us is inevitably obliterated; and death is the fact that our very life is inevitably obliterated. All four of these come to us, and we cannot outrun them, nor force them back, nor buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7630,
        "course": "C17",
        "seq": 592,
        "acip": ",DE YANG LTUNG BA PHYIR BCOS NI SDOM PA GONG 'OG SO SO NAS GSUNGS PA LTAR BYA LA SDIG PA PHYIR BCOS PA NI STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BYA STE, CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA'I MDO LAS, BYAMS PA BYANG CHUB SEMS DPA' SEMS DPA' CHEN PO CHOS BZHI DANG LDAN NA SDIG PA BYAS SHING BSAGS PA YANG ZIL GYIS GNON PAR 'GYUR RO, , BZHI GANG ZHE NA, 'DI LTA STE, RNAM PAR SUN 'BYIN PA KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG , GNYEN PO KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG , NYES PA LAS BZLOG PA'I STOBS DANG , RTEN GYI STOBS SO ZHES GSUNGS TE BYAS SHING BSAGS PA'I LAS NI MYONG NGES YIN PAS DE ZIL GYIS GNON NUS NA MA NGES PA LTA CI SMOS,",
        "wylie": "de yang ltung ba phyir bcos ni sdom pa gong 'og so so nas gsungs pa ltar bya la sdig pa phyir bcos pa ni stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bya ste, chos bzhi bstan pa'i mdo las, byams pa byang chub sems dpa' sems dpa' chen po chos bzhi dang ldan na sdig pa byas shing bsags pa yang zil gyis gnon par 'gyur ro, , bzhi gang zhe na, 'di lta ste, rnam par sun 'byin pa kun tu spyod pa dang , gnyen po kun tu spyod pa dang , nyes pa las bzlog pa'i stobs dang , rten gyi stobs so zhes gsungs te byas shing bsags pa'i las ni myong nges yin pas de zil gyis gnon nus na ma nges pa lta ci smos",
        "english": "Instances in which you have committed some transgression of your vows, at whichever level of vows you may have, should be repaired according to the methods prescribed for those particular vows. For repairing the damage caused by other kinds of bad deeds you should utilize the four powers. As the Sutra on the Four Practices states, If, oh Loving One, some great bodhisattva has succeeded in the four practices, then even those bad deeds which have been both committed and collected can be overcome. What are the four practices? They are as follows: the activity of destruction; the activity of applying the antidote; the force of restraining from the particular bad deed; and the force of the foundation. The expression \"committed and collected\" refers to actions whose karmic results are \"certain to be experienced.\" If even these types of karmic results can be overcome by the four practices, then there is no need to mention those which are not certain to be experienced.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C17\", \"acip\": \",DE YANG LTUNG BA PHYIR BCOS NI SDOM PA GONG 'OG SO SO NAS GSUNGS PA LTAR BYA LA SDIG PA PHYIR BCOS PA NI STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BYA STE, CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA'I MDO LAS, BYAMS PA BYANG CHUB SEMS DPA' SEMS DPA' CHEN PO CHOS BZHI DANG LDAN NA SDIG PA BYAS SHING BSAGS PA YANG ZIL GYIS GNON PAR 'GYUR RO, , BZHI GANG ZHE NA, 'DI LTA STE, RNAM PAR SUN 'BYIN PA KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG , GNYEN PO KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG , NYES PA LAS BZLOG PA'I STOBS DANG , RTEN GYI STOBS SO ZHES GSUNGS TE BYAS SHING BSAGS PA'I LAS NI MYONG NGES YIN PAS DE ZIL GYIS GNON NUS NA MA NGES PA LTA CI SMOS,\", \"wylie\": \"de yang ltung ba phyir bcos ni sdom pa gong 'og so so nas gsungs pa ltar bya la sdig pa phyir bcos pa ni stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bya ste, chos bzhi bstan pa'i mdo las, byams pa byang chub sems dpa' sems dpa' chen po chos bzhi dang ldan na sdig pa byas shing bsags pa yang zil gyis gnon par 'gyur ro, , bzhi gang zhe na, 'di lta ste, rnam par sun 'byin pa kun tu spyod pa dang , gnyen po kun tu spyod pa dang , nyes pa las bzlog pa'i stobs dang , rten gyi stobs so zhes gsungs te byas shing bsags pa'i las ni myong nges yin pas de zil gyis gnon nus na ma nges pa lta ci smos\", \"english\": \"Instances in which you have committed some transgression of your vows, at whichever level of vows you may have, should be repaired according to the methods prescribed for those particular vows. For repairing the damage caused by other kinds of bad deeds you should utilize the four powers. As the Sutra on the Four Practices states, If, oh Loving One, some great bodhisattva has succeeded in the four practices, then even those bad deeds which have been both committed and collected can be overcome. What are the four practices? They are as follows: the activity of destruction; the activity of applying the antidote; the force of restraining from the particular bad deed; and the force of the foundation. The expression \\\"committed and collected\\\" refers to actions whose karmic results are \\\"certain to be experienced.\\\" If even these types of karmic results can be overcome by the four practices, then there is no need to mention those which are not certain to be experienced.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7733,
        "course": "C17",
        "seq": 695,
        "acip": ",GSUM PA NI, DPE DE BZHIN DU PHYI ROL GYI GNOD BYED KYI DNGOS PO YANG MTHA' DAG BDAG GIS PHYIR BZLOG PAR BYED PAS MI LANG GIS, BDAG GI SEMS 'DI YUL DE DAG LA DMIGS NAS KHONG KHRO BSKYED PA LAS PHYIR BZLOG PAR BYA'I DE GOMS PA LAS BZOD PA RDZOGS PAR 'GYUR GYI, KHRO BA'I YUL GZHAN RNAMS BDAG GIS CI ZHIG BZLOG STE BZLOG MI NUS SHING DGOS PA MED PA'I PHYIR RO,",
        "wylie": "gsum pa ni, dpe de bzhin du phyi rol gyi gnod byed kyi dngos po yang mtha' dag bdag gis phyir bzlog par byed pas mi lang gis, bdag gi sems 'di yul de dag la dmigs nas khong khro bskyed pa las phyir bzlog par bya'i de goms pa las bzod pa rdzogs par 'gyur gyi, khro ba'i yul gzhan rnams bdag gis ci zhig bzlog ste bzlog mi nus shing dgos pa med pa'i phyir ro",
        "english": "Here is the third part. My present case is the same as this metaphor. It would be an impossibility for me to stop every single outer object that might bring me any kind of pain. Rather I should stop this tendency of my own mind to focus on these same objects and then feel anger. It is through the constant practice of this action that I could eventually bring patience to its culmination. Why though should I try to stop all the other objects, everything that could ever make me angry? I never could, and there is no need even to try. 14 What it Means to Perfect Joyful Effort",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C17\", \"acip\": \",GSUM PA NI, DPE DE BZHIN DU PHYI ROL GYI GNOD BYED KYI DNGOS PO YANG MTHA' DAG BDAG GIS PHYIR BZLOG PAR BYED PAS MI LANG GIS, BDAG GI SEMS 'DI YUL DE DAG LA DMIGS NAS KHONG KHRO BSKYED PA LAS PHYIR BZLOG PAR BYA'I DE GOMS PA LAS BZOD PA RDZOGS PAR 'GYUR GYI, KHRO BA'I YUL GZHAN RNAMS BDAG GIS CI ZHIG BZLOG STE BZLOG MI NUS SHING DGOS PA MED PA'I PHYIR RO,\", \"wylie\": \"gsum pa ni, dpe de bzhin du phyi rol gyi gnod byed kyi dngos po yang mtha' dag bdag gis phyir bzlog par byed pas mi lang gis, bdag gi sems 'di yul de dag la dmigs nas khong khro bskyed pa las phyir bzlog par bya'i de goms pa las bzod pa rdzogs par 'gyur gyi, khro ba'i yul gzhan rnams bdag gis ci zhig bzlog ste bzlog mi nus shing dgos pa med pa'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"Here is the third part. My present case is the same as this metaphor. It would be an impossibility for me to stop every single outer object that might bring me any kind of pain. Rather I should stop this tendency of my own mind to focus on these same objects and then feel anger. It is through the constant practice of this action that I could eventually bring patience to its culmination. Why though should I try to stop all the other objects, everything that could ever make me angry? I never could, and there is no need even to try. 14 What it Means to Perfect Joyful Effort\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7993,
        "course": "C18",
        "seq": 198,
        "acip": ",CI PHYIR KUN GYI SDUG BSNGAL NI, ,BZLOG PAR BYA ZHES BRTZAD DU MED, ,GAL TE BZLOG NA'ANG THAMS CAD BZLOG,DE MIN BDAG KYANG SEMS CAN BZHIN,",
        "wylie": "ci phyir kun gyi sdug bsngal ni, ,bzlog par bya zhes brtsad du med, ,gal te bzlog na'ang thams cad bzlog,de min bdag kyang sems can bzhin",
        "english": "You can't continue your argument Of asking why the pains of all Are something you must stop. If you're going to stop it, then You must stop all of it; if not, Then mine's like other beings'.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C18\", \"acip\": \",CI PHYIR KUN GYI SDUG BSNGAL NI, ,BZLOG PAR BYA ZHES BRTZAD DU MED, ,GAL TE BZLOG NA'ANG THAMS CAD BZLOG,DE MIN BDAG KYANG SEMS CAN BZHIN,\", \"wylie\": \"ci phyir kun gyi sdug bsngal ni, ,bzlog par bya zhes brtsad du med, ,gal te bzlog na'ang thams cad bzlog,de min bdag kyang sems can bzhin\", \"english\": \"You can't continue your argument Of asking why the pains of all Are something you must stop. If you're going to stop it, then You must stop all of it; if not, Then mine's like other beings'.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 8563,
        "course": "C18",
        "seq": 768,
        "acip": "SHOG,SPYOD LAM KUN TU RANG RGYUD LA, ,RTOG CING NYON MONGS SKYES MA THAG,BDAG GZHAN MA RUNGS BYED PAS NA, ,BTZAN THABS GDONG NAS BZLOG PAR",
        "wylie": "shog,spyod lam kun tu rang rgyud la, ,rtog cing nyon mongs skyes ma thag,bdag gzhan ma rungs byed pas na, ,btsan thabs gdong nas bzlog par",
        "english": "(3) As I go through the day may I watch my mind, To see if a negative thought has come; If it does may I stop it right there, with force, Since it hurts myself and others.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C18\", \"acip\": \"SHOG,SPYOD LAM KUN TU RANG RGYUD LA, ,RTOG CING NYON MONGS SKYES MA THAG,BDAG GZHAN MA RUNGS BYED PAS NA, ,BTZAN THABS GDONG NAS BZLOG PAR\", \"wylie\": \"shog,spyod lam kun tu rang rgyud la, ,rtog cing nyon mongs skyes ma thag,bdag gzhan ma rungs byed pas na, ,btsan thabs gdong nas bzlog par\", \"english\": \"(3) As I go through the day may I watch my mind, To see if a negative thought has come; If it does may I stop it right there, with force, Since it hurts myself and others.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 8577,
        "course": "C18",
        "seq": 782,
        "acip": ",'KHOR BA'I SKYON TSANG SOMS DANG , ,ZHEN PA RTING NAS BZLOG PA'I GNAD YIN,",
        "wylie": "'khor ba'i skyon tshang soms dang , ,zhen pa rting nas bzlog pa'i gnad yin",
        "english": "Think of the viper's nest of the problems of this life; This is the key for stopping Attachment at the bottom of your heart.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C18\", \"acip\": \",'KHOR BA'I SKYON TSANG SOMS DANG , ,ZHEN PA RTING NAS BZLOG PA'I GNAD YIN,\", \"wylie\": \"'khor ba'i skyon tshang soms dang , ,zhen pa rting nas bzlog pa'i gnad yin\", \"english\": \"Think of the viper's nest of the problems of this life; This is the key for stopping Attachment at the bottom of your heart.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 8580,
        "course": "C18",
        "seq": 785,
        "acip": ",SNOD BCUD YI DAM DU SBYONGS DANG , ,THA MAL GYI ZHEN PA BZLOG PA'I GNAD YIN,",
        "wylie": "snod bcud yi dam du sbyongs dang , ,tha mal gyi zhen pa bzlog pa'i gnad yin",
        "english": "See the world and people as Angels; This is the key for stopping The idea that life is ordinary.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C18\", \"acip\": \",SNOD BCUD YI DAM DU SBYONGS DANG , ,THA MAL GYI ZHEN PA BZLOG PA'I GNAD YIN,\", \"wylie\": \"snod bcud yi dam du sbyongs dang , ,tha mal gyi zhen pa bzlog pa'i gnad yin\", \"english\": \"See the world and people as Angels; This is the key for stopping The idea that life is ordinary.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 8622,
        "course": "C18",
        "seq": 827,
        "acip": ",DKA' BA GSUM NYON MONGS PA SKYES PA NA, DRAN PA, BZLOG PA, RGYUN CHOD PA GSUM, LA BSLAB PAR BYA, , RGYU YI GTZO BO RNAM GSUM BLA MA MJAL, CHOS NYAMS SU LEN, CHOS KYI MTHUN RKYEN 'DZOM PA STE, BLANG , , NYAMS PA MED PA RNAM GSUM MOS GUS, SPRO BA, BSLAB BYA, BSGOM, LUS NGAG YID GSUM DGE BA DANG , 'BRAL MED GSUM DANG LDAN PAR BYA,",
        "wylie": "dka' ba gsum nyon mongs pa skyes pa na, dran pa, bzlog pa, rgyun chod pa gsum, la bslab par bya, , rgyu yi gtso bo rnam gsum bla ma mjal, chos nyams su len, chos kyi mthun rkyen 'dzom pa ste, blang , , nyams pa med pa rnam gsum mos gus, spro ba, bslab bya, bsgom, lus ngag yid gsum dge ba dang , 'bral med gsum dang ldan par bya",
        "english": "Train yourself in the three kinds of hardship. Take to yourself the three main causes. Live in three ways that never get weaker. Keep the three that should never be lost. Note by the Tibetan commentator: The \"three hardships\" are learning to detect the start of a mental affliction; to stop it; and to stop the habit. The \"three main causes\" are finding a Lama, practicing the Dharma, and having all the support you need to practice the Dharma come together. The \"three ways\" are feeling devotion [for your Lama], joy [for practicing the lojong or instructions for developing a good heart], and keeping your various vows and commitments [through strong awareness and recollection]. The \"three that should never be lost\" are virtuous actions in your actions, your words, and your thoughts.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C18\", \"acip\": \",DKA' BA GSUM NYON MONGS PA SKYES PA NA, DRAN PA, BZLOG PA, RGYUN CHOD PA GSUM, LA BSLAB PAR BYA, , RGYU YI GTZO BO RNAM GSUM BLA MA MJAL, CHOS NYAMS SU LEN, CHOS KYI MTHUN RKYEN 'DZOM PA STE, BLANG , , NYAMS PA MED PA RNAM GSUM MOS GUS, SPRO BA, BSLAB BYA, BSGOM, LUS NGAG YID GSUM DGE BA DANG , 'BRAL MED GSUM DANG LDAN PAR BYA,\", \"wylie\": \"dka' ba gsum nyon mongs pa skyes pa na, dran pa, bzlog pa, rgyun chod pa gsum, la bslab par bya, , rgyu yi gtso bo rnam gsum bla ma mjal, chos nyams su len, chos kyi mthun rkyen 'dzom pa ste, blang , , nyams pa med pa rnam gsum mos gus, spro ba, bslab bya, bsgom, lus ngag yid gsum dge ba dang , 'bral med gsum dang ldan par bya\", \"english\": \"Train yourself in the three kinds of hardship. Take to yourself the three main causes. Live in three ways that never get weaker. Keep the three that should never be lost. Note by the Tibetan commentator: The \\\"three hardships\\\" are learning to detect the start of a mental affliction; to stop it; and to stop the habit. The \\\"three main causes\\\" are finding a Lama, practicing the Dharma, and having all the support you need to practice the Dharma come together. The \\\"three ways\\\" are feeling devotion [for your Lama], joy [for practicing the lojong or instructions for developing a good heart], and keeping your various vows and commitments [through strong awareness and recollection]. The \\\"three that should never be lost\\\" are virtuous actions in your actions, your words, and your thoughts.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 33724,
        "course": "EM",
        "seq": 1016,
        "acip": "DE DAG NI SO PAS DGRA BYUNG BA MTHONG BA NA DE BZLOG PA LA BRTZON PA BZHIN NO,,",
        "wylie": "de dag ni so pas dgra byung ba mthong ba na de bzlog pa la brtson pa bzhin no",
        "english": "This whole process is similar to the way in which we rush to repel an enemy once our sentries have seen that the foe is at the gates.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"EM\", \"acip\": \"DE DAG NI SO PAS DGRA BYUNG BA MTHONG BA NA DE BZLOG PA LA BRTZON PA BZHIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de dag ni so pas dgra byung ba mthong ba na de bzlog pa la brtson pa bzhin no\", \"english\": \"This whole process is similar to the way in which we rush to repel an enemy once our sentries have seen that the foe is at the gates.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 23532,
        "course": "GIE",
        "seq": 20,
        "acip": "GANG GIS BSHAD PA DE MI RIGS PA YIN SNYAM NAS LEGS PAR DPYAD PA NA, DE NAS, RANG GI STON PA'I BSHAD PA NI LEGS PAR BSHAD PA YIN LA, CIG SHOS KYI NI DE LAS BZLOG PA'O SNYAM DU NGES NAS RANG GI STON PA DANG DE'I BSTAN PA'I RJES SU 'JUG DGOS PA YIN NO,,",
        "wylie": "gang gis bshad pa de mi rigs pa yin snyam nas legs par dpyad pa na, de nas, rang gi ston pa'i bshad pa ni legs par bshad pa yin la, cig shos kyi ni de las bzlog pa'o snyam du nges nas rang gi ston pa dang de'i bstan pa'i rjes su 'jug dgos pa yin no",
        "english": "By weighing presentations carefully to see if they seem reasonable or not, these people become confident that their own teacher's explanations are good ones, and that others' explanations are the opposite.  This is how we should come to follow our own teacher, and what that teacher teaches.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GIE\", \"acip\": \"GANG GIS BSHAD PA DE MI RIGS PA YIN SNYAM NAS LEGS PAR DPYAD PA NA, DE NAS, RANG GI STON PA'I BSHAD PA NI LEGS PAR BSHAD PA YIN LA, CIG SHOS KYI NI DE LAS BZLOG PA'O SNYAM DU NGES NAS RANG GI STON PA DANG DE'I BSTAN PA'I RJES SU 'JUG DGOS PA YIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"gang gis bshad pa de mi rigs pa yin snyam nas legs par dpyad pa na, de nas, rang gi ston pa'i bshad pa ni legs par bshad pa yin la, cig shos kyi ni de las bzlog pa'o snyam du nges nas rang gi ston pa dang de'i bstan pa'i rjes su 'jug dgos pa yin no\", \"english\": \"By weighing presentations carefully to see if they seem reasonable or not, these people become confident that their own teacher's explanations are good ones, and that others' explanations are the opposite.  This is how we should come to follow our own teacher, and what that teacher teaches.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 24122,
        "course": "GIE",
        "seq": 610,
        "acip": "MYANG 'DAS LA LHAG BCAS SOGS GSUM LAS, LHAG BCAS MYANG 'DAS NI SDUG BSNGAL GYI PHUNG PO DANG BCAS PA'I MYANG 'DAS, LHAG MED NI DE LAS BZLOG PAR 'DOD CING, SANGS RGYAS KYI SA'I MYANG 'DAS RNAMS MI GNAS PA'I MYANG 'DAS SO,,",
        "wylie": "myang 'das la lhag bcas sogs gsum las, lhag bcas myang 'das ni sdug bsngal gyi phung po dang bcas pa'i myang 'das, lhag med ni de las bzlog par 'dod cing, sangs rgyas kyi sa'i myang 'das rnams mi gnas pa'i myang 'das so",
        "english": "The position of this branch of the Middle Way on the three types of nirvana—nirvana where there is “still something left over” and so on—is as follows.  They say that nirvana with something still left over is when we achieve nirvana, but still possess the heaps, or components of ourselves, that constitute suffering.  Nirvana where nothing is left over then would be the opposite of that.  Nirvana where “we no longer stay,” finally, would be nirvana at the level of an Enlightened Being.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GIE\", \"acip\": \"MYANG 'DAS LA LHAG BCAS SOGS GSUM LAS, LHAG BCAS MYANG 'DAS NI SDUG BSNGAL GYI PHUNG PO DANG BCAS PA'I MYANG 'DAS, LHAG MED NI DE LAS BZLOG PAR 'DOD CING, SANGS RGYAS KYI SA'I MYANG 'DAS RNAMS MI GNAS PA'I MYANG 'DAS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"myang 'das la lhag bcas sogs gsum las, lhag bcas myang 'das ni sdug bsngal gyi phung po dang bcas pa'i myang 'das, lhag med ni de las bzlog par 'dod cing, sangs rgyas kyi sa'i myang 'das rnams mi gnas pa'i myang 'das so\", \"english\": \"The position of this branch of the Middle Way on the three types of nirvana—nirvana where there is “still something left over” and so on—is as follows.  They say that nirvana with something still left over is when we achieve nirvana, but still possess the heaps, or components of ourselves, that constitute suffering.  Nirvana where nothing is left over then would be the opposite of that.  Nirvana where “we no longer stay,” finally, would be nirvana at the level of an Enlightened Being.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 24347,
        "course": "GIE",
        "seq": 835,
        "acip": "[B38] RNAM 'GYUR GYI TSOGS THAMS CAD KYANG SKYE BA'I RIM PA LAS BZLOG PA'I SGO NAS GTZO BO DE NYID DU RAB TU THIM PA'I PHYIR, MI GSAL BA'I NGO BOR GYUR PA DE'I TSE SKYES BU NYAG GCIG GNAS TE, DE'I PHYIR GROL BA ZHES BYA'O,,",
        "wylie": "[b38] rnam 'gyur gyi tshogs thams cad kyang skye ba'i rim pa las bzlog pa'i sgo nas gtso bo de nyid du rab tu thim pa'i phyir, mi gsal ba'i ngo bor gyur pa de'i tshe skyes bu nyag gcig gnas te, de'i phyir grol ba zhes bya'o",
        "english": "At this point the entire group of expressions—the creations—dissolves completely back into the primal nature, in reverse order to the process by which they were created.  The nature then is reduced to its own featureless essential form, and the soul simply rests in total isolation.  This is how this system describes liberation.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GIE\", \"acip\": \"[B38] RNAM 'GYUR GYI TSOGS THAMS CAD KYANG SKYE BA'I RIM PA LAS BZLOG PA'I SGO NAS GTZO BO DE NYID DU RAB TU THIM PA'I PHYIR, MI GSAL BA'I NGO BOR GYUR PA DE'I TSE SKYES BU NYAG GCIG GNAS TE, DE'I PHYIR GROL BA ZHES BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"[b38] rnam 'gyur gyi tshogs thams cad kyang skye ba'i rim pa las bzlog pa'i sgo nas gtso bo de nyid du rab tu thim pa'i phyir, mi gsal ba'i ngo bor gyur pa de'i tshe skyes bu nyag gcig gnas te, de'i phyir grol ba zhes bya'o\", \"english\": \"At this point the entire group of expressions—the creations—dissolves completely back into the primal nature, in reverse order to the process by which they were created.  The nature then is reduced to its own featureless essential form, and the soul simply rests in total isolation.  This is how this system describes liberation.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 24379,
        "course": "GIE",
        "seq": 867,
        "acip": "[B70] RNAM PAR 'GYUR BA'I TSOGS THAMS CAD KYANG SKYE BA'I RIM PA LAS BZLOG PA'I SGO NAS DE NYID DU RAB TU ZHI BA'I PHYIR MI GSAL BA'I NGO BOR GYUR PA DE'I TSE SKYES BU NYAG GCIG GNAS TE DE'I PHYIR GROL BA ZHES BYA'O,,",
        "wylie": "[b70] rnam par 'gyur ba'i tshogs thams cad kyang skye ba'i rim pa las bzlog pa'i sgo nas de nyid du rab tu zhi ba'i phyir mi gsal ba'i ngo bor gyur pa de'i tshe skyes bu nyag gcig gnas te de'i phyir grol ba zhes bya'o",
        "english": "At this point the entire group of expressions—the creations—dissolves completely back into the primal nature, in the reverse order of the process by which they were created.  The nature then returns to complete rest and is reduced to its own featureless essential form; and the soul just rests in total isolation.  This is how this system describes liberation.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GIE\", \"acip\": \"[B70] RNAM PAR 'GYUR BA'I TSOGS THAMS CAD KYANG SKYE BA'I RIM PA LAS BZLOG PA'I SGO NAS DE NYID DU RAB TU ZHI BA'I PHYIR MI GSAL BA'I NGO BOR GYUR PA DE'I TSE SKYES BU NYAG GCIG GNAS TE DE'I PHYIR GROL BA ZHES BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"[b70] rnam par 'gyur ba'i tshogs thams cad kyang skye ba'i rim pa las bzlog pa'i sgo nas de nyid du rab tu zhi ba'i phyir mi gsal ba'i ngo bor gyur pa de'i tshe skyes bu nyag gcig gnas te de'i phyir grol ba zhes bya'o\", \"english\": \"At this point the entire group of expressions—the creations—dissolves completely back into the primal nature, in the reverse order of the process by which they were created.  The nature then returns to complete rest and is reduced to its own featureless essential form; and the soul just rests in total isolation.  This is how this system describes liberation.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 24454,
        "course": "GIE",
        "seq": 942,
        "acip": "[C55] BYI BI BA'I {%SHI BI PA] RGYAL POR GYUR PA'I TSE SLONG BA PO'I TSIG PHYIR MI BZLOG PAR BYIN PA LTA BU SOGS KYIS PHYAG RING ZHING MDZES PA DANG,",
        "wylie": "[c55] byi bi ba'i {%shi bi pa] rgyal por gyur pa'i tshe slong ba po'i tshig phyir mi bzlog par byin pa lta bu sogs kyis phyag ring zhing mdzes pa dang",
        "english": "(S12) When our Teacher was the king of Shivipa, he would give absolutely whatever someone asked of him; through this and other such deeds, his hands became long and lovely.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GIE\", \"acip\": \"[C55] BYI BI BA'I {%SHI BI PA] RGYAL POR GYUR PA'I TSE SLONG BA PO'I TSIG PHYIR MI BZLOG PAR BYIN PA LTA BU SOGS KYIS PHYAG RING ZHING MDZES PA DANG,\", \"wylie\": \"[c55] byi bi ba'i {%shi bi pa] rgyal por gyur pa'i tshe slong ba po'i tshig phyir mi bzlog par byin pa lta bu sogs kyis phyag ring zhing mdzes pa dang\", \"english\": \"(S12) When our Teacher was the king of Shivipa, he would give absolutely whatever someone asked of him; through this and other such deeds, his hands became long and lovely.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27840,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 521,
        "acip": "DE DAG GIS NI SUN 'BYIN PA'I THAL BA DANG, DES BZLOG PA 'PHANGS PA GNYIS LA KHYAB BYED MA NGES PA'I SUN 'BYIN LTAR SNANG DU BSTAN PA NI, RIGS PA DANG 'GAL BA'O, ,LUNG DANG 'GAL BA NI SNGAR MANG DU BSHAD ZIN TO,,",
        "wylie": "de dag gis ni sun 'byin pa'i thal ba dang, des bzlog pa 'phangs pa gnyis la khyab byed ma nges pa'i sun 'byin ltar snang du bstan pa ni, rigs pa dang 'gal ba'o, ,lung dang 'gal ba ni sngar mang du bshad zin to",
        "english": "Saying that the pair of (1) the statement of necessity here which overthrows the other's position and (2) the opposite which this implies are a false refutation due to the necessity failing to hold in every instance contradicts logic. And we have already demonstrated, with many citations, that it contradicts scriptural authority.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE DAG GIS NI SUN 'BYIN PA'I THAL BA DANG, DES BZLOG PA 'PHANGS PA GNYIS LA KHYAB BYED MA NGES PA'I SUN 'BYIN LTAR SNANG DU BSTAN PA NI, RIGS PA DANG 'GAL BA'O, ,LUNG DANG 'GAL BA NI SNGAR MANG DU BSHAD ZIN TO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de dag gis ni sun 'byin pa'i thal ba dang, des bzlog pa 'phangs pa gnyis la khyab byed ma nges pa'i sun 'byin ltar snang du bstan pa ni, rigs pa dang 'gal ba'o, ,lung dang 'gal ba ni sngar mang du bshad zin to\", \"english\": \"Saying that the pair of (1) the statement of necessity here which overthrows the other's position and (2) the opposite which this implies are a false refutation due to the necessity failing to hold in every instance contradicts logic. And we have already demonstrated, with many citations, that it contradicts scriptural authority.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28071,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 752,
        "acip": "GANG ZHIG RANG GIS SNGON NYES SPYOD BYAS PA'I 'BRAS BU SDUG BSNGAL LA LONGS SPYOD BZHIN DU, DE LA GTI MUG PAS GZHAN GYIS BDAG LA GNOD PA BYAS SO SNYAM DU RTOG PA DE LA NI, GNOD BYED LA KHRO BA SKYE LA, GNOD LAN BYAS PAS PHYIS DE'I GNOD PA'I SDUG BSNGAL MI 'BYUNG BAR 'DOD PA DE YANG BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I PHYIR BSHAD PA NI,",
        "wylie": "gang zhig rang gis sngon nyes spyod byas pa'i 'bras bu sdug bsngal la longs spyod bzhin du, de la gti mug pas gzhan gyis bdag la gnod pa byas so snyam du rtog pa de la ni, gnod byed la khro ba skye la, gnod lan byas pas phyis de'i gnod pa'i sdug bsngal mi 'byung bar 'dod pa de yang bzlog par bya ba'i phyir bshad pa ni",
        "english": "Consider now a person who is experiencing pain which is a result of a negative deed that they've done to someone in the past. Out of ignorance, they might get the wrong idea that “This other person has hurt me”; and then towards them—towards the person who has hurt them—they might feel anger. And then they may try to hurt the person back, hoping that this will somehow prevent the pain of the same kind of injury, in the future. The lines are seeking to prevent this kind of thinking.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GANG ZHIG RANG GIS SNGON NYES SPYOD BYAS PA'I 'BRAS BU SDUG BSNGAL LA LONGS SPYOD BZHIN DU, DE LA GTI MUG PAS GZHAN GYIS BDAG LA GNOD PA BYAS SO SNYAM DU RTOG PA DE LA NI, GNOD BYED LA KHRO BA SKYE LA, GNOD LAN BYAS PAS PHYIS DE'I GNOD PA'I SDUG BSNGAL MI 'BYUNG BAR 'DOD PA DE YANG BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I PHYIR BSHAD PA NI,\", \"wylie\": \"gang zhig rang gis sngon nyes spyod byas pa'i 'bras bu sdug bsngal la longs spyod bzhin du, de la gti mug pas gzhan gyis bdag la gnod pa byas so snyam du rtog pa de la ni, gnod byed la khro ba skye la, gnod lan byas pas phyis de'i gnod pa'i sdug bsngal mi 'byung bar 'dod pa de yang bzlog par bya ba'i phyir bshad pa ni\", \"english\": \"Consider now a person who is experiencing pain which is a result of a negative deed that they've done to someone in the past. Out of ignorance, they might get the wrong idea that “This other person has hurt me”; and then towards them—towards the person who has hurt them—they might feel anger. And then they may try to hurt the person back, hoping that this will somehow prevent the pain of the same kind of injury, in the future. The lines are seeking to prevent this kind of thinking.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28279,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 960,
        "acip": "DON DAM PAR SGRO 'DOGS PA'I MTHAR MI LTUNG BA LA, DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN MED PA KHO NA LA LAS 'BRAS MTHONG BA DGOS PAR GSUNGS SHING, DE GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA NI RTAG CHAD KYI MTHAR LTUNG BAR BSHAD LA,",
        "wylie": "don dam par sgro 'dogs pa'i mthar mi ltung ba la, dngos po rang bzhin med pa kho na la las 'bras mthong ba dgos par gsungs shing, de gnyis las bzlog pa ni rtag chad kyi mthar ltung bar bshad la",
        "english": "And to avoid falling into the extreme view of overrating ultimate reality, we must understand that the workings of deeds & their consequences can only be observed in the absence of things having any nature of their own. Master Chandrakirti explains that the opposite of these two is to fall into the extreme positions of thinking that nothing changes, or that nothing exists at all.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DON DAM PAR SGRO 'DOGS PA'I MTHAR MI LTUNG BA LA, DNGOS PO RANG BZHIN MED PA KHO NA LA LAS 'BRAS MTHONG BA DGOS PAR GSUNGS SHING, DE GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA NI RTAG CHAD KYI MTHAR LTUNG BAR BSHAD LA,\", \"wylie\": \"don dam par sgro 'dogs pa'i mthar mi ltung ba la, dngos po rang bzhin med pa kho na la las 'bras mthong ba dgos par gsungs shing, de gnyis las bzlog pa ni rtag chad kyi mthar ltung bar bshad la\", \"english\": \"And to avoid falling into the extreme view of overrating ultimate reality, we must understand that the workings of deeds & their consequences can only be observed in the absence of things having any nature of their own. Master Chandrakirti explains that the opposite of these two is to fall into the extreme positions of thinking that nothing changes, or that nothing exists at all.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28558,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1239,
        "acip": "DE LTAR THAL BA LAS THA MI DAD PAR DMIGS PA YANG MA YIN PA'I PHYIR, DE GNYIS GYI NGO BO KUN NAS GCIG PA YANG MA YIN NO ZHES BZLOG PA 'PHEN PA MDZAD DE, BZLOG PA 'PHEN PA DANG BZLOG PA RANG RGYUD 'PHEN PA LA KHYAD PAR SHIN TU CHE'O,,",
        "wylie": "de ltar thal ba las tha mi dad par dmigs pa yang ma yin pa'i phyir, de gnyis gyi ngo bo kun nas gcig pa yang ma yin no zhes bzlog pa 'phen pa mdzad de, bzlog pa 'phen pa dang bzlog pa rang rgyud 'phen pa la khyad par shin tu che'o",
        "english": "And if this absurd consequence held true, then we could never observe the seed & the sprout displaying anything but qualities that weren't different. The statement of absurdity thus implies the denial, which states: “And so neither can we say that these two are such that to be one is to be, in every shape & manner, the other.” There is a massive difference then between such a statement implying a denial, and a denial implying a statement of principle.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE LTAR THAL BA LAS THA MI DAD PAR DMIGS PA YANG MA YIN PA'I PHYIR, DE GNYIS GYI NGO BO KUN NAS GCIG PA YANG MA YIN NO ZHES BZLOG PA 'PHEN PA MDZAD DE, BZLOG PA 'PHEN PA DANG BZLOG PA RANG RGYUD 'PHEN PA LA KHYAD PAR SHIN TU CHE'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"de ltar thal ba las tha mi dad par dmigs pa yang ma yin pa'i phyir, de gnyis gyi ngo bo kun nas gcig pa yang ma yin no zhes bzlog pa 'phen pa mdzad de, bzlog pa 'phen pa dang bzlog pa rang rgyud 'phen pa la khyad par shin tu che'o\", \"english\": \"And if this absurd consequence held true, then we could never observe the seed & the sprout displaying anything but qualities that weren't different. The statement of absurdity thus implies the denial, which states: “And so neither can we say that these two are such that to be one is to be, in every shape & manner, the other.” There is a massive difference then between such a statement implying a denial, and a denial implying a statement of principle.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28623,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1304,
        "acip": "THAL BA GNYIS KA LA BZLOG PA 'PHEN PA 'GREL PAR BSHAD DO,,",
        "wylie": "thal ba gnyis ka la bzlog pa 'phen pa 'grel par bshad do",
        "english": "The autocommentary describes both of these absurd statements as the type which implies the truth of the very opposite of what the opponent is asserting. The source of the absurdity",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"THAL BA GNYIS KA LA BZLOG PA 'PHEN PA 'GREL PAR BSHAD DO,,\", \"wylie\": \"thal ba gnyis ka la bzlog pa 'phen pa 'grel par bshad do\", \"english\": \"The autocommentary describes both of these absurd statements as the type which implies the truth of the very opposite of what the opponent is asserting. The source of the absurdity\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28624,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1305,
        "acip": "GNYIS PA LA GNYIS, GZHAN SKYE LA HA CANG THAL BA 'JUG PA'I RGYU MTSAN, THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA KHAS LEN PA YI 'GAL BA'O,,",
        "wylie": "gnyis pa la gnyis, gzhan skye la ha cang thal ba 'jug pa'i rgyu mtshan, thal ba las bzlog pa khas len pa yi 'gal ba'o",
        "english": "Having completed our demonstration of absurdity, we move on to the second topic here: a logical examination of the relevant points. This itself comes in two steps: the reason why the idea that things could grow from something else leads to the absurdity; and then the contradiction that arises from accepting the opposite of the absurdity.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GNYIS PA LA GNYIS, GZHAN SKYE LA HA CANG THAL BA 'JUG PA'I RGYU MTSAN, THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA KHAS LEN PA YI 'GAL BA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"gnyis pa la gnyis, gzhan skye la ha cang thal ba 'jug pa'i rgyu mtshan, thal ba las bzlog pa khas len pa yi 'gal ba'o\", \"english\": \"Having completed our demonstration of absurdity, we move on to the second topic here: a logical examination of the relevant points. This itself comes in two steps: the reason why the idea that things could grow from something else leads to the absurdity; and then the contradiction that arises from accepting the opposite of the absurdity.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28642,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1323,
        "acip": "GNYIS PA NI, GAL TE SNGAR BSHAD PA'I THAL BA GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA'I DON KHAS LEN NA, TSIG GSAL LAS, THAL BAR 'GYUR BA BZLOG PA'I DON DANG YANG PHA ROL PO NYID 'BREL BA YIN GYI, KHO BO CAG NI MA YIN TE, RANG LA DAM BCA' BA MED PA'I PHYIR RO, ,ZHES DANG,",
        "wylie": "gnyis pa ni, gal te sngar bshad pa'i thal ba gnyis las bzlog pa'i don khas len na, tshig gsal las, thal bar 'gyur ba bzlog pa'i don dang yang pha rol po nyid 'brel ba yin gyi, kho bo cag ni ma yin te, rang la dam bca' ba med pa'i phyir ro, ,zhes dang",
        "english": "This brings us to the second step from above: the contradiction which arises from accepting the opposite of the absurdity. One may begin with the following question, in three parts: Well, assume that what we are supposed to accept is the point which is the opposite of the two absurdities which have just been presented. How then, first of all, can A Clarification of the Verses then make the statement that “And if we examine the point which is the opposite of the absurdity, we would have to say that it relates to the position of the other party; and not to our own. And that's because we take no position on the matter at all.”",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GNYIS PA NI, GAL TE SNGAR BSHAD PA'I THAL BA GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA'I DON KHAS LEN NA, TSIG GSAL LAS, THAL BAR 'GYUR BA BZLOG PA'I DON DANG YANG PHA ROL PO NYID 'BREL BA YIN GYI, KHO BO CAG NI MA YIN TE, RANG LA DAM BCA' BA MED PA'I PHYIR RO, ,ZHES DANG,\", \"wylie\": \"gnyis pa ni, gal te sngar bshad pa'i thal ba gnyis las bzlog pa'i don khas len na, tshig gsal las, thal bar 'gyur ba bzlog pa'i don dang yang pha rol po nyid 'brel ba yin gyi, kho bo cag ni ma yin te, rang la dam bca' ba med pa'i phyir ro, ,zhes dang\", \"english\": \"This brings us to the second step from above: the contradiction which arises from accepting the opposite of the absurdity. One may begin with the following question, in three parts: Well, assume that what we are supposed to accept is the point which is the opposite of the two absurdities which have just been presented. How then, first of all, can A Clarification of the Verses then make the statement that “And if we examine the point which is the opposite of the absurdity, we would have to say that it relates to the position of the other party; and not to our own. And that's because we take no position on the matter at all.”\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28643,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1324,
        "acip": "RANG BZHIN MED PAR SMRA BAS RANG BZHIN DANG BCAS PAR SMRA BA LA THAL BA BSGRUBS PA NA, THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA'I DON CAN DU THAL BAR GA LA 'GYUR TE, ZHES DANG,",
        "wylie": "rang bzhin med par smra bas rang bzhin dang bcas par smra ba la thal ba bsgrubs pa na, thal ba las bzlog pa'i don can du thal bar ga la 'gyur te, zhes dang",
        "english": "And how can the work further then state, “Why on earth would it be the case that—when those who deny that things have any nature of their own demonstrate an absurd consequence of the beliefs of those who say that things do have such a nature—they would then necessarily have to be thinkers who support the point which is the opposite of this absurdity?”",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RANG BZHIN MED PAR SMRA BAS RANG BZHIN DANG BCAS PAR SMRA BA LA THAL BA BSGRUBS PA NA, THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA'I DON CAN DU THAL BAR GA LA 'GYUR TE, ZHES DANG,\", \"wylie\": \"rang bzhin med par smra bas rang bzhin dang bcas par smra ba la thal ba bsgrubs pa na, thal ba las bzlog pa'i don can du thal bar ga la 'gyur te, zhes dang\", \"english\": \"And how can the work further then state, “Why on earth would it be the case that—when those who deny that things have any nature of their own demonstrate an absurd consequence of the beliefs of those who say that things do have such a nature—they would then necessarily have to be thinkers who support the point which is the opposite of this absurdity?”\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28644,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1325,
        "acip": "DE'I PHYIR THAL BA SGRUB PA NI PHA ROL PO'I DAM BCA' BA 'GOG PA TZAM GYI 'BRAS BU CAN YIN PA'I PHYIR, THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA'I DON DU 'GYUR BA YOD PA MA YIN NO, ZHES GSUNGS PA RNAMS JI LTAR DRANG SNYAM NA,",
        "wylie": "de'i phyir thal ba sgrub pa ni pha rol po'i dam bca' ba 'gog pa tsam gyi 'bras bu can yin pa'i phyir, thal ba las bzlog pa'i don du 'gyur ba yod pa ma yin no, zhes gsungs pa rnams ji ltar drang snyam na",
        "english": "How finally can the same text say, “As such the demonstration of absurdity here is meant only to have the effect of denying the position of the other party; it is not the case that there is some point which is the opposite of the absurdity and which is meant to be included into this effect.”",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE'I PHYIR THAL BA SGRUB PA NI PHA ROL PO'I DAM BCA' BA 'GOG PA TZAM GYI 'BRAS BU CAN YIN PA'I PHYIR, THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA'I DON DU 'GYUR BA YOD PA MA YIN NO, ZHES GSUNGS PA RNAMS JI LTAR DRANG SNYAM NA,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i phyir thal ba sgrub pa ni pha rol po'i dam bca' ba 'gog pa tsam gyi 'bras bu can yin pa'i phyir, thal ba las bzlog pa'i don du 'gyur ba yod pa ma yin no, zhes gsungs pa rnams ji ltar drang snyam na\", \"english\": \"How finally can the same text say, “As such the demonstration of absurdity here is meant only to have the effect of denying the position of the other party; it is not the case that there is some point which is the opposite of the absurdity and which is meant to be included into this effect.”\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28646,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1327,
        "acip": "DE'I THAL CHOS NI SKYE BA DON MED DANG THUG MED DU THAL BA TZAM MIN GYI, SLAR YANG SKYE BA DON MED DANG THUG MED YIN LA, THAL CHOS DE BZLOG PA'I DON SLAR YANG SKYE BA DON BCAS DANG THUG BCAS NI GRANGS CAN KHO NA 'DOD KYI, RANG LA DE'I DAM BCA' BA MED PA'I RGYU MTSAN GYIS, DE KHAS BLANGS PA'I GRUB MTHA' DANG 'GAL BA MED CES PA'I DON TE LUNG DANG PO'I DON NO,,",
        "wylie": "de'i thal chos ni skye ba don med dang thug med du thal ba tsam min gyi, slar yang skye ba don med dang thug med yin la, thal chos de bzlog pa'i don slar yang skye ba don bcas dang thug bcas ni grangs can kho na 'dod kyi, rang la de'i dam bca' ba med pa'i rgyu mtshan gyis, de khas blangs pa'i grub mtha' dang 'gal ba med ces pa'i don te lung dang po'i don no",
        "english": "The point of the absurdity is not solely to say that the growing of things would then have to be pointless, and endless; but rather to say that their repeated growth would be pointless and endless. The idea that would be the opposite of these two absurdities would be that the repeated growth of things did have a point, and did come to an end—and that would be accepted only by the Numerists. What the first citation you've given then is actually saying is that—because we ourselves take no position on this matter—there is no danger of our contradicting the other position that our school does take.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE'I THAL CHOS NI SKYE BA DON MED DANG THUG MED DU THAL BA TZAM MIN GYI, SLAR YANG SKYE BA DON MED DANG THUG MED YIN LA, THAL CHOS DE BZLOG PA'I DON SLAR YANG SKYE BA DON BCAS DANG THUG BCAS NI GRANGS CAN KHO NA 'DOD KYI, RANG LA DE'I DAM BCA' BA MED PA'I RGYU MTSAN GYIS, DE KHAS BLANGS PA'I GRUB MTHA' DANG 'GAL BA MED CES PA'I DON TE LUNG DANG PO'I DON NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i thal chos ni skye ba don med dang thug med du thal ba tsam min gyi, slar yang skye ba don med dang thug med yin la, thal chos de bzlog pa'i don slar yang skye ba don bcas dang thug bcas ni grangs can kho na 'dod kyi, rang la de'i dam bca' ba med pa'i rgyu mtshan gyis, de khas blangs pa'i grub mtha' dang 'gal ba med ces pa'i don te lung dang po'i don no\", \"english\": \"The point of the absurdity is not solely to say that the growing of things would then have to be pointless, and endless; but rather to say that their repeated growth would be pointless and endless. The idea that would be the opposite of these two absurdities would be that the repeated growth of things did have a point, and did come to an end—and that would be accepted only by the Numerists. What the first citation you've given then is actually saying is that—because we ourselves take no position on this matter—there is no danger of our contradicting the other position that our school does take.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28649,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1330,
        "acip": "LUNG GNYIS PA'I DON NI RANG BZHIN MED PAR SMRA BA DBU MA PAS, RANG BZHIN DANG BCAS PAR SMRA BA GRANGS CAN LA SNGAR BSHAD PA'I THAL BA GNYIS 'PHANGS PA NA, RANG LA BZLOG DON 'PHEN PA KHAS LEN 'DOD MED LA, DE MED KYANG RANG DBANG MED PAR BYED PAR YANG MIN PAS, THAL BA DE GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA KHAS MI LEN ZHES PA'I DON NO,,",
        "wylie": "lung gnyis pa'i don ni rang bzhin med par smra ba dbu ma pas, rang bzhin dang bcas par smra ba grangs can la sngar bshad pa'i thal ba gnyis 'phangs pa na, rang la bzlog don 'phen pa khas len 'dod med la, de med kyang rang dbang med par byed par yang min pas, thal ba de gnyis las bzlog pa khas mi len zhes pa'i don no",
        "english": "The point of the second citation above is to express how those who say that nothing has any nature of its own—the followers of the middle way—might address the Numerists, drawing the two absurd consequences just described; but even as they do, it is not the case that they themselves would then accept the point which is the opposite of these absurdities. And even assuming they would not want to, there is further no necessity that they would have to do so, despite themselves. As such, what's being said is that they would not in fact accept the points opposite to the absurdities listed.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"LUNG GNYIS PA'I DON NI RANG BZHIN MED PAR SMRA BA DBU MA PAS, RANG BZHIN DANG BCAS PAR SMRA BA GRANGS CAN LA SNGAR BSHAD PA'I THAL BA GNYIS 'PHANGS PA NA, RANG LA BZLOG DON 'PHEN PA KHAS LEN 'DOD MED LA, DE MED KYANG RANG DBANG MED PAR BYED PAR YANG MIN PAS, THAL BA DE GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA KHAS MI LEN ZHES PA'I DON NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"lung gnyis pa'i don ni rang bzhin med par smra ba dbu ma pas, rang bzhin dang bcas par smra ba grangs can la sngar bshad pa'i thal ba gnyis 'phangs pa na, rang la bzlog don 'phen pa khas len 'dod med la, de med kyang rang dbang med par byed par yang min pas, thal ba de gnyis las bzlog pa khas mi len zhes pa'i don no\", \"english\": \"The point of the second citation above is to express how those who say that nothing has any nature of its own—the followers of the middle way—might address the Numerists, drawing the two absurd consequences just described; but even as they do, it is not the case that they themselves would then accept the point which is the opposite of these absurdities. And even assuming they would not want to, there is further no necessity that they would have to do so, despite themselves. As such, what's being said is that they would not in fact accept the points opposite to the absurdities listed.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28650,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1331,
        "acip": "LUNG GSUM PA'I DON NI THAL BA GNYIS KYI THAL CHOS BZLOG PA'I DON SLAR YANG SKYE BA DON BCAS DANG THUG BCAS KYI RTAGS KYIS BDAG SKYE MED PA MI SGRUB KYANG DGOS PA MED PA MIN TE, GRANGS CAN MI 'DOD PA'I SKYE BA DON MED DANG THUG MED BSGRUBS PAS, GRANGS CAN GYIS DAM BCAS PA'I BDAG LAS SKYE BA RNAM PAR BCAD PA TZAM ZHIG SGRUB PA'I DGOS PA CAN YIN PA'I PHYIR RO ZHES PA'I DON NO,,",
        "wylie": "lung gsum pa'i don ni thal ba gnyis kyi thal chos bzlog pa'i don slar yang skye ba don bcas dang thug bcas kyi rtags kyis bdag skye med pa mi sgrub kyang dgos pa med pa min te, grangs can mi 'dod pa'i skye ba don med dang thug med bsgrubs pas, grangs can gyis dam bcas pa'i bdag las skye ba rnam par bcad pa tsam zhig sgrub pa'i dgos pa can yin pa'i phyir ro zhes pa'i don no",
        "english": "What the third citation is saying is the following. The opposites of the quality being expressed in the two absurdities are (1) that there would be some point to things growing once again after they had already grown once; and (2) that such repeated growing would not be endless. Presented as reasons, they cannot, admittedly, establish that the growth of things does not occur through any kind of self-nature. But neither do they serve no purpose; for they do have the benefit of establishing that something which the Numerists would not want to accept—that the growth of something was pointless, and that it would be repeated endlessly—would have to be the case. And as such, they do disallow the growth through some self-nature which the Numerists do assert.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"LUNG GSUM PA'I DON NI THAL BA GNYIS KYI THAL CHOS BZLOG PA'I DON SLAR YANG SKYE BA DON BCAS DANG THUG BCAS KYI RTAGS KYIS BDAG SKYE MED PA MI SGRUB KYANG DGOS PA MED PA MIN TE, GRANGS CAN MI 'DOD PA'I SKYE BA DON MED DANG THUG MED BSGRUBS PAS, GRANGS CAN GYIS DAM BCAS PA'I BDAG LAS SKYE BA RNAM PAR BCAD PA TZAM ZHIG SGRUB PA'I DGOS PA CAN YIN PA'I PHYIR RO ZHES PA'I DON NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"lung gsum pa'i don ni thal ba gnyis kyi thal chos bzlog pa'i don slar yang skye ba don bcas dang thug bcas kyi rtags kyis bdag skye med pa mi sgrub kyang dgos pa med pa min te, grangs can mi 'dod pa'i skye ba don med dang thug med bsgrubs pas, grangs can gyis dam bcas pa'i bdag las skye ba rnam par bcad pa tsam zhig sgrub pa'i dgos pa can yin pa'i phyir ro zhes pa'i don no\", \"english\": \"What the third citation is saying is the following. The opposites of the quality being expressed in the two absurdities are (1) that there would be some point to things growing once again after they had already grown once; and (2) that such repeated growing would not be endless. Presented as reasons, they cannot, admittedly, establish that the growth of things does not occur through any kind of self-nature. But neither do they serve no purpose; for they do have the benefit of establishing that something which the Numerists would not want to accept—that the growth of something was pointless, and that it would be repeated endlessly—would have to be the case. And as such, they do disallow the growth through some self-nature which the Numerists do assert.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28651,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1332,
        "acip": "HA CANG THAL BA GNYIS LA NI THAL BA'I BZLOG DON RANG NYID KYIS KHAS LEN PAS, THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA'I DON LA RANG GIS KHAS LEN PA DANG MI LEN PA GNYIS SHES PAR BYA'O,,",
        "wylie": "ha cang thal ba gnyis la ni thal ba'i bzlog don rang nyid kyis khas len pas, thal ba las bzlog pa'i don la rang gis khas len pa dang mi len pa gnyis shes par bya'o",
        "english": "Our readers should understand then that we might first accept that there is a point which is the opposite of that which is made by these two absurdities; but that, by the same token, there are two possibilities here: one where we would ourselves accept this point that was the opposite of the absurdity, and another where we would not. A response to absurdity",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"HA CANG THAL BA GNYIS LA NI THAL BA'I BZLOG DON RANG NYID KYIS KHAS LEN PAS, THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA'I DON LA RANG GIS KHAS LEN PA DANG MI LEN PA GNYIS SHES PAR BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"ha cang thal ba gnyis la ni thal ba'i bzlog don rang nyid kyis khas len pas, thal ba las bzlog pa'i don la rang gis khas len pa dang mi len pa gnyis shes par bya'o\", \"english\": \"Our readers should understand then that we might first accept that there is a point which is the opposite of that which is made by these two absurdities; but that, by the same token, there are two possibilities here: one where we would ourselves accept this point that was the opposite of the absurdity, and another where we would not. A response to absurdity\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28710,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1391,
        "acip": "'GOG PA'I TSE LTAR SNANG 'BA' ZHIG SMRAS NAS, DE NYID TSUR LA BZLOG PA'I TSE BSNYON 'DINGS PA LA RE NA, KHO BO CAG SMYON PA DANG LHAN CIG TU MI RTZOD DO, ZHES TSIG GSAL LAS GSUNGS PA LTAR BYA'O,,",
        "wylie": "'gog pa'i tshe ltar snang 'ba' zhig smras nas, de nyid tshur la bzlog pa'i tshe bsnyon 'dings pa la re na, kho bo cag smyon pa dang lhan cig tu mi rtsod do, zhes tshig gsal las gsungs pa ltar bya'o",
        "english": "If rather you put your hopes in people who are deranged—who attempt to disprove the ideas of others but who, at the same time, employ only arguments which can be turned back upon their own positions—then all we can do is repeat the words of the Clarification of the Verses: “For our part, we refuse to debate with those who have lost their sanity.” Simultaneous others could never grow",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'GOG PA'I TSE LTAR SNANG 'BA' ZHIG SMRAS NAS, DE NYID TSUR LA BZLOG PA'I TSE BSNYON 'DINGS PA LA RE NA, KHO BO CAG SMYON PA DANG LHAN CIG TU MI RTZOD DO, ZHES TSIG GSAL LAS GSUNGS PA LTAR BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"'gog pa'i tshe ltar snang 'ba' zhig smras nas, de nyid tshur la bzlog pa'i tshe bsnyon 'dings pa la re na, kho bo cag smyon pa dang lhan cig tu mi rtsod do, zhes tshig gsal las gsungs pa ltar bya'o\", \"english\": \"If rather you put your hopes in people who are deranged—who attempt to disprove the ideas of others but who, at the same time, employ only arguments which can be turned back upon their own positions—then all we can do is repeat the words of the Clarification of the Verses: “For our part, we refuse to debate with those who have lost their sanity.” Simultaneous others could never grow\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28740,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1421,
        "acip": "'BRAL BAR BYED PA'I THABS RANG BZHIN MED PA'I TSUL YANG DANG YANG DU THOS PA DANG BRAL BA, 'JIG RTEN GYI GNOD PA'I RDOL THABS TE BLA BRDOL LA BRTEN PA 'DI NI, 'JIG RTEN GYI BYUNG TSUL MANG PO CIG MA BSHAD PAR 'JIG RTEN GYI GNOD PA'I BLA BRDOL LAS BZLOG PAR MI NUS PAS,",
        "wylie": "'bral bar byed pa'i thabs rang bzhin med pa'i tshul yang dang yang du thos pa dang bral ba, 'jig rten gyi gnod pa'i rdol thabs te bla brdol la brten pa 'di ni, 'jig rten gyi byung tshul mang po cig ma bshad par 'jig rten gyi gnod pa'i bla brdol las bzlog par mi nus pas",
        "english": "The way to leave the friend behind is to hear teaching, again & again, on how things have no nature of their own—but these friends have had no such teaching. And now they babble about what contradicts what the world sees; they are delivering themselves to meaningless chatter. In so doing, they will never be able to battle the blather that truly hurts the world—they are missing the many details of what really brought this world of pain into being.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'BRAL BAR BYED PA'I THABS RANG BZHIN MED PA'I TSUL YANG DANG YANG DU THOS PA DANG BRAL BA, 'JIG RTEN GYI GNOD PA'I RDOL THABS TE BLA BRDOL LA BRTEN PA 'DI NI, 'JIG RTEN GYI BYUNG TSUL MANG PO CIG MA BSHAD PAR 'JIG RTEN GYI GNOD PA'I BLA BRDOL LAS BZLOG PAR MI NUS PAS,\", \"wylie\": \"'bral bar byed pa'i thabs rang bzhin med pa'i tshul yang dang yang du thos pa dang bral ba, 'jig rten gyi gnod pa'i rdol thabs te bla brdol la brten pa 'di ni, 'jig rten gyi byung tshul mang po cig ma bshad par 'jig rten gyi gnod pa'i bla brdol las bzlog par mi nus pas\", \"english\": \"The way to leave the friend behind is to hear teaching, again & again, on how things have no nature of their own—but these friends have had no such teaching. And now they babble about what contradicts what the world sees; they are delivering themselves to meaningless chatter. In so doing, they will never be able to battle the blather that truly hurts the world—they are missing the many details of what really brought this world of pain into being.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28814,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1495,
        "acip": "DE LTA BU'I GNOD PA MED PA'I SHES PA DRUG GIS GZUNG PA'I KUN RDZOB PA'I DON DANG, DE LAS BZLOG PA'I DON LA YANG DAG DANG LOG PAR 'JOG PA NI 'JIG RTEN PA'I SHES PA KHO NA LA LTOS NAS YIN TE, DE DAG JI LTAR SNANG BA LTAR GYI DON DU YOD PA LA 'JIG RTEN PA'I SHES PAS GNOD PA MED PA DANG YOD PA'I PHYIR RO,,",
        "wylie": "de lta bu'i gnod pa med pa'i shes pa drug gis gzung pa'i kun rdzob pa'i don dang, de las bzlog pa'i don la yang dag dang log par 'jog pa ni 'jig rten pa'i shes pa kho na la ltos nas yin te, de dag ji ltar snang ba ltar gyi don du yod pa la 'jig rten pa'i shes pas gnod pa med pa dang yod pa'i phyir ro",
        "english": "When we speak of the deceptive objects which are perceived by six forms of consciousness which are not adversely affected; and when we posit the opposite types of objects, dividing them into correct and mistaken; we do so relative only to states of mind of the everyday world. And that's because we are deciding whether or not these objects exist in the way they appear depending upon whether states of mind belonging to the everyday world can draw them into question or not.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE LTA BU'I GNOD PA MED PA'I SHES PA DRUG GIS GZUNG PA'I KUN RDZOB PA'I DON DANG, DE LAS BZLOG PA'I DON LA YANG DAG DANG LOG PAR 'JOG PA NI 'JIG RTEN PA'I SHES PA KHO NA LA LTOS NAS YIN TE, DE DAG JI LTAR SNANG BA LTAR GYI DON DU YOD PA LA 'JIG RTEN PA'I SHES PAS GNOD PA MED PA DANG YOD PA'I PHYIR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de lta bu'i gnod pa med pa'i shes pa drug gis gzung pa'i kun rdzob pa'i don dang, de las bzlog pa'i don la yang dag dang log par 'jog pa ni 'jig rten pa'i shes pa kho na la ltos nas yin te, de dag ji ltar snang ba ltar gyi don du yod pa la 'jig rten pa'i shes pas gnod pa med pa dang yod pa'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"When we speak of the deceptive objects which are perceived by six forms of consciousness which are not adversely affected; and when we posit the opposite types of objects, dividing them into correct and mistaken; we do so relative only to states of mind of the everyday world. And that's because we are deciding whether or not these objects exist in the way they appear depending upon whether states of mind belonging to the everyday world can draw them into question or not.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28915,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1596,
        "acip": "JI LTAR ZHE NA, RE ZHIG GAL TE DNGOS PO DE YID DANG MTHUN PA YIN NA NI, DE'I TSE DE LA RJES SU CHAGS PA BZLOG PAR DKA'O, ,CI STE MI MTHUN NA NI DE'I TSE DE LA KHONG KHRO NGA {%BA} DANG TSIG PA ZA BA BZLOG PAR DKA'O, ZHES GSUNGS SHING,",
        "wylie": "ji ltar zhe na, re zhig gal te dngos po de yid dang mthun pa yin na ni, de'i tshe de la rjes su chags pa bzlog par dka'o, ,ci ste mi mthun na ni de'i tshe de la khong khro nga {%ba} dang tshig pa za ba bzlog par dka'o, zhes gsungs shing",
        "english": "And how is that? If at the moment these things fit the mind, then it will be difficult to stop desire for them. If they do not, then it will be difficult to stop repulsion, and anger, for them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"JI LTAR ZHE NA, RE ZHIG GAL TE DNGOS PO DE YID DANG MTHUN PA YIN NA NI, DE'I TSE DE LA RJES SU CHAGS PA BZLOG PAR DKA'O, ,CI STE MI MTHUN NA NI DE'I TSE DE LA KHONG KHRO NGA {%BA} DANG TSIG PA ZA BA BZLOG PAR DKA'O, ZHES GSUNGS SHING,\", \"wylie\": \"ji ltar zhe na, re zhig gal te dngos po de yid dang mthun pa yin na ni, de'i tshe de la rjes su chags pa bzlog par dka'o, ,ci ste mi mthun na ni de'i tshe de la khong khro nga {%ba} dang tshig pa za ba bzlog par dka'o, zhes gsungs shing\", \"english\": \"And how is that? If at the moment these things fit the mind, then it will be difficult to stop desire for them. If they do not, then it will be difficult to stop repulsion, and anger, for them.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 29614,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 2295,
        "acip": "GCIG LA CIG SHOS MED PA'I STONG PA 'DI DPER BRJOD DE DANG 'DRA BAR 'GYUR TSUL NI BCOM LDAN 'DAS KYI RANG BZHIN GYIS DBEN PA'I STONG NYID GSUNGS PA NI SEMS CAN RNAMS THOG MA MED PA NAS GZUGS SOGS KYI DNGOS PO LA BDEN PAR GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I MNGON ZHEN BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU YIN PA LA,",
        "wylie": "gcig la cig shos med pa'i stong pa 'di dper brjod de dang 'dra bar 'gyur tshul ni bcom ldan 'das kyi rang bzhin gyis dben pa'i stong nyid gsungs pa ni sems can rnams thog ma med pa nas gzugs sogs kyi dngos po la bden par grub par 'dzin pa'i mngon zhen bzlog pa'i phyir du yin pa la",
        "english": "Now let's consider this approach, where—as in the example just cited—we speak of emptiness as one thing lacking another. When the conquering Buddhas make statements like this, about an emptiness where things are devoid of any nature, the reason they do so is to stop the belief of living beings, for time with no beginning, where they grasp to the idea that entities such as form and the like could exist as real.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GCIG LA CIG SHOS MED PA'I STONG PA 'DI DPER BRJOD DE DANG 'DRA BAR 'GYUR TSUL NI BCOM LDAN 'DAS KYI RANG BZHIN GYIS DBEN PA'I STONG NYID GSUNGS PA NI SEMS CAN RNAMS THOG MA MED PA NAS GZUGS SOGS KYI DNGOS PO LA BDEN PAR GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I MNGON ZHEN BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU YIN PA LA,\", \"wylie\": \"gcig la cig shos med pa'i stong pa 'di dper brjod de dang 'dra bar 'gyur tshul ni bcom ldan 'das kyi rang bzhin gyis dben pa'i stong nyid gsungs pa ni sems can rnams thog ma med pa nas gzugs sogs kyi dngos po la bden par grub par 'dzin pa'i mngon zhen bzlog pa'i phyir du yin pa la\", \"english\": \"Now let's consider this approach, where—as in the example just cited—we speak of emptiness as one thing lacking another. When the conquering Buddhas make statements like this, about an emptiness where things are devoid of any nature, the reason they do so is to stop the belief of living beings, for time with no beginning, where they grasp to the idea that entities such as form and the like could exist as real.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 29763,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 2444,
        "acip": "PHYOGS SNGA MA'I THAL BA DANG BZLOG PA GNYIS KA LA'ANG KHYAB PA MED PA'I LAN 'DEBS PAS, RANG RIG MED KYANG DRAN PA SKYE TSUL JI LTAR YIN ZHE NA,",
        "wylie": "phyogs snga ma'i thal ba dang bzlog pa gnyis ka la'ang khyab pa med pa'i lan 'debs pas, rang rig med kyang dran pa skye tshul ji ltar yin zhe na",
        "english": "And so one may question us here, saying— You have answered “that is not necessarily the case” to both the statement of necessity of the other party here, and to the position it implies. How then, according to you, is it that a recollection can arise even in the absence of apperception?",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"PHYOGS SNGA MA'I THAL BA DANG BZLOG PA GNYIS KA LA'ANG KHYAB PA MED PA'I LAN 'DEBS PAS, RANG RIG MED KYANG DRAN PA SKYE TSUL JI LTAR YIN ZHE NA,\", \"wylie\": \"phyogs snga ma'i thal ba dang bzlog pa gnyis ka la'ang khyab pa med pa'i lan 'debs pas, rang rig med kyang dran pa skye tshul ji ltar yin zhe na\", \"english\": \"And so one may question us here, saying— You have answered “that is not necessarily the case” to both the statement of necessity of the other party here, and to the position it implies. How then, according to you, is it that a recollection can arise even in the absence of apperception?\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 29835,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 2516,
        "acip": "'DI 'DRA BA'I RNAM GZHAG PHRA MO NI RANG GIS GZHAN LA, 'DI YI YOD PA GANG GIS SHES PAR 'GYUR, ZHES SOGS KYI GNOD PA BRJOD PA TSUR LA BZLOG PA NA,",
        "wylie": "'di 'dra ba'i rnam gzhag phra mo ni rang gis gzhan la, 'di yi yod pa gang gis shes par 'gyur, zhes sogs kyi gnod pa brjod pa tshur la bzlog pa na",
        "english": "And so suppose that our opponent turns the tables and addresses us with the same fault in our argument that we originally addressed to them, considering this subtle presentation with the question, “And what is it that allows you to understand the existence of this thing?”",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'DI 'DRA BA'I RNAM GZHAG PHRA MO NI RANG GIS GZHAN LA, 'DI YI YOD PA GANG GIS SHES PAR 'GYUR, ZHES SOGS KYI GNOD PA BRJOD PA TSUR LA BZLOG PA NA,\", \"wylie\": \"'di 'dra ba'i rnam gzhag phra mo ni rang gis gzhan la, 'di yi yod pa gang gis shes par 'gyur, zhes sogs kyi gnod pa brjod pa tshur la bzlog pa na\", \"english\": \"And so suppose that our opponent turns the tables and addresses us with the same fault in our argument that we originally addressed to them, considering this subtle presentation with the question, “And what is it that allows you to understand the existence of this thing?”\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 29924,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 2605,
        "acip": "KHO BO CAG NI KUN RDZOB 'KHRUL PA'I SNANG BA RANG RGYUD LA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR SHIN TU TSEGS CHE BAS LAM SGOM PA LA GNAS PAR 'GYUR GYI, GAL TE KHYOD LA 'JIG RTEN GYIS MI GNOD NA, KHYOD KYIS 'JIG RTEN NYID LA LTOS NAS KUN RDZOB 'DI NI DGAG PAR GYIS SHIG ,DE KHYOD KYI RIGS PAS 'GOG NUS NA NGAS KYANG KHYOD LA STONGS GDAB PA STE GROGS BYA'O,,",
        "wylie": "kho bo cag ni kun rdzob 'khrul pa'i snang ba rang rgyud la bzlog pa'i phyir shin tu tshegs che bas lam sgom pa la gnas par 'gyur gyi, gal te khyod la 'jig rten gyis mi gnod na, khyod kyis 'jig rten nyid la ltos nas kun rdzob 'di ni dgag par gyis shig ,de khyod kyi rigs pas 'gog nus na ngas kyang khyod la stongs gdab pa ste grogs bya'o",
        "english": "We for our part have kept up our practice of the path with incredibly great effort, in order to put a stop to the mistaken appearance of deceptive reality within our own minds. If what the world says is not something that could call your ideas into question, then you can go ahead and deny this deceptive reality relative to the world itself. If you find that this is something that you can deny with your particular reasoning, then I will gladly “join hands with you”—meaning, I will accompany you.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"KHO BO CAG NI KUN RDZOB 'KHRUL PA'I SNANG BA RANG RGYUD LA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR SHIN TU TSEGS CHE BAS LAM SGOM PA LA GNAS PAR 'GYUR GYI, GAL TE KHYOD LA 'JIG RTEN GYIS MI GNOD NA, KHYOD KYIS 'JIG RTEN NYID LA LTOS NAS KUN RDZOB 'DI NI DGAG PAR GYIS SHIG ,DE KHYOD KYI RIGS PAS 'GOG NUS NA NGAS KYANG KHYOD LA STONGS GDAB PA STE GROGS BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"kho bo cag ni kun rdzob 'khrul pa'i snang ba rang rgyud la bzlog pa'i phyir shin tu tshegs che bas lam sgom pa la gnas par 'gyur gyi, gal te khyod la 'jig rten gyis mi gnod na, khyod kyis 'jig rten nyid la ltos nas kun rdzob 'di ni dgag par gyis shig ,de khyod kyi rigs pas 'gog nus na ngas kyang khyod la stongs gdab pa ste grogs bya'o\", \"english\": \"We for our part have kept up our practice of the path with incredibly great effort, in order to put a stop to the mistaken appearance of deceptive reality within our own minds. If what the world says is not something that could call your ideas into question, then you can go ahead and deny this deceptive reality relative to the world itself. If you find that this is something that you can deny with your particular reasoning, then I will gladly “join hands with you”—meaning, I will accompany you.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30011,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 2692,
        "acip": "DES NA SEMS LAS GZHAN PA'I BYED PA PO BZLOG PA STE BKAG GI ,PHYI ROL GYI GZUGS NI BKAG PA MA YIN NO,,",
        "wylie": "des na sems las gzhan pa'i byed pa po bzlog pa ste bkag gi ,phyi rol gyi gzugs ni bkag pa ma yin no",
        "english": "As such, we reject—which is to say, we deny—any creator other than the mind itself. But we are not thereby denying outer form itself.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DES NA SEMS LAS GZHAN PA'I BYED PA PO BZLOG PA STE BKAG GI ,PHYI ROL GYI GZUGS NI BKAG PA MA YIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"des na sems las gzhan pa'i byed pa po bzlog pa ste bkag gi ,phyi rol gyi gzugs ni bkag pa ma yin no\", \"english\": \"As such, we reject—which is to say, we deny—any creator other than the mind itself. But we are not thereby denying outer form itself.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30052,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 2733,
        "acip": ",MDO SDE GANG LAS PHYI ROL SNANG YOD MIN, ,SEMS NI SNA TSOGS SNANG NGO ZHES GSUNGS PA, ,GZUGS LA SHIN TU CHAGS GANG DE DAG LA, ,GZUGS BZLOG PA STE DE YANG DRANG DON NYID,",
        "wylie": "mdo sde gang las phyi rol snang yod min, ,sems ni sna tshogs snang ngo zhes gsungs pa, ,gzugs la shin tu chags gang de dag la, ,gzugs bzlog pa ste de yang drang don nyid",
        "english": "There is that sutra Which states that The appearance Of the outside Is nothing that exists. What this is saying Is that the mind Appears in a Variety of ways. There are those With an extreme Attraction to forms; And so they put A stop to forms For these individuals— And those were only intended As something metaphorical.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",MDO SDE GANG LAS PHYI ROL SNANG YOD MIN, ,SEMS NI SNA TSOGS SNANG NGO ZHES GSUNGS PA, ,GZUGS LA SHIN TU CHAGS GANG DE DAG LA, ,GZUGS BZLOG PA STE DE YANG DRANG DON NYID,\", \"wylie\": \"mdo sde gang las phyi rol snang yod min, ,sems ni sna tshogs snang ngo zhes gsungs pa, ,gzugs la shin tu chags gang de dag la, ,gzugs bzlog pa ste de yang drang don nyid\", \"english\": \"There is that sutra Which states that The appearance Of the outside Is nothing that exists. What this is saying Is that the mind Appears in a Variety of ways. There are those With an extreme Attraction to forms; And so they put A stop to forms For these individuals— And those were only intended As something metaphorical.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30056,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 2737,
        "acip": "DE DAG LA BCOM LDAN 'DAS KYIS GZUGS KYI RKYEN CAN GYI NYON MONGS PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR, CHAGS PA CAN DAG LA PHYI ROL GYI YUL LA CHAGS PA 'JOMS PA'I KENG RUS LTAR,",
        "wylie": "de dag la bcom ldan 'das kyis gzugs kyi rkyen can gyi nyon mongs pa bzlog pa'i phyir, chags pa can dag la phyi rol gyi yul la chags pa 'joms pa'i keng rus ltar",
        "english": "The Conquerors then undertake to help these individuals put a stop to the negative emotions they feel—those emotions that find their causes in form. And so then they teach them something like the visualization of skeletons, which smashes the attraction to outer objects that these people with craving feel so strongly.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE DAG LA BCOM LDAN 'DAS KYIS GZUGS KYI RKYEN CAN GYI NYON MONGS PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR, CHAGS PA CAN DAG LA PHYI ROL GYI YUL LA CHAGS PA 'JOMS PA'I KENG RUS LTAR,\", \"wylie\": \"de dag la bcom ldan 'das kyis gzugs kyi rkyen can gyi nyon mongs pa bzlog pa'i phyir, chags pa can dag la phyi rol gyi yul la chags pa 'joms pa'i keng rus ltar\", \"english\": \"The Conquerors then undertake to help these individuals put a stop to the negative emotions they feel—those emotions that find their causes in form. And so then they teach them something like the visualization of skeletons, which smashes the attraction to outer objects that these people with craving feel so strongly.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30157,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 2838,
        "acip": "DE LA GANG ZAG DANG BDAG GCES PA'I SROG DANG, DBANG PO LA SOGS PA SROG MA YIN PA DANG, MNGON MTHO DANG NGES LEGS 'GRUB PA'I CHOS DANG, DE LAS BZLOG PA'I CHOS MIN PA DANG, NYON MONGS PA'I ZAG PA DANG 'CHAL KHRIMS 'GOG PA'I SDOM PA DANG, LA SOGS PAS BDE BA DANG, SDUG BSNGAL BA DANG, SHES PA LAS SKYES SHING SHES PA'I RGYUR GYUR PA'I 'DU BYA BA'I SHUGS TE, TSIG GI DON DGU'I RNAM GZHAG BYAS NAS",
        "wylie": "de la gang zag dang bdag gces pa'i srog dang, dbang po la sogs pa srog ma yin pa dang, mngon mtho dang nges legs 'grub pa'i chos dang, de las bzlog pa'i chos min pa dang, nyon mongs pa'i zag pa dang 'chal khrims 'gog pa'i sdom pa dang, la sogs pas bde ba dang, sdug bsngal ba dang, shes pa las skyes shing shes pa'i rgyur gyur pa'i 'du bya ba'i shugs te, tshig gi don dgu'i rnam gzhag byas nas",
        "english": "They set forth nine different principles, which involve the person and the life which is cherished by the self; those things which are not the life, such as the powers and so forth; the teachings which help us achieve the higher rebirths and definite goodness; the opposite of these teachings, negative teachings; the impurities of the negativities; the commitments which help prevent immoral behavior; and “so on”—which would include pleasure, pain, the power that brings things together born from mind and supplying the causes for mind.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE LA GANG ZAG DANG BDAG GCES PA'I SROG DANG, DBANG PO LA SOGS PA SROG MA YIN PA DANG, MNGON MTHO DANG NGES LEGS 'GRUB PA'I CHOS DANG, DE LAS BZLOG PA'I CHOS MIN PA DANG, NYON MONGS PA'I ZAG PA DANG 'CHAL KHRIMS 'GOG PA'I SDOM PA DANG, LA SOGS PAS BDE BA DANG, SDUG BSNGAL BA DANG, SHES PA LAS SKYES SHING SHES PA'I RGYUR GYUR PA'I 'DU BYA BA'I SHUGS TE, TSIG GI DON DGU'I RNAM GZHAG BYAS NAS\", \"wylie\": \"de la gang zag dang bdag gces pa'i srog dang, dbang po la sogs pa srog ma yin pa dang, mngon mtho dang nges legs 'grub pa'i chos dang, de las bzlog pa'i chos min pa dang, nyon mongs pa'i zag pa dang 'chal khrims 'gog pa'i sdom pa dang, la sogs pas bde ba dang, sdug bsngal ba dang, shes pa las skyes shing shes pa'i rgyur gyur pa'i 'du bya ba'i shugs te, tshig gi don dgu'i rnam gzhag byas nas\", \"english\": \"They set forth nine different principles, which involve the person and the life which is cherished by the self; those things which are not the life, such as the powers and so forth; the teachings which help us achieve the higher rebirths and definite goodness; the opposite of these teachings, negative teachings; the impurities of the negativities; the commitments which help prevent immoral behavior; and “so on”—which would include pleasure, pain, the power that brings things together born from mind and supplying the causes for mind.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30420,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3101,
        "acip": "RNAM 'GYUR GYI TSOGS THAMS CAD KYANG SKYE BA'I RIM PA LAS BZLOG PA'I SGO NAS GTZO BO DE NYID DU RAB TU THIM PA'I PHYIR, MI GSAL BA'I NGO BOR GYUR PA DE'I TSE SKYES BU NYAG GCIG GNAS TE, DE'I PHYIR GROL BA ZHES BYA'O,,",
        "wylie": "rnam 'gyur gyi tshogs thams cad kyang skye ba'i rim pa las bzlog pa'i sgo nas gtso bo de nyid du rab tu thim pa'i phyir, mi gsal ba'i ngo bor gyur pa de'i tshe skyes bu nyag gcig gnas te, de'i phyir grol ba zhes bya'o",
        "english": "At this point the entire group of expressions—the creations—dissolves completely back into the primal nature, in reverse order to the process by which they were created. The nature then is reduced to its own featureless essential form, and the soul simply rests in total isolation. This is how this system describes liberation.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RNAM 'GYUR GYI TSOGS THAMS CAD KYANG SKYE BA'I RIM PA LAS BZLOG PA'I SGO NAS GTZO BO DE NYID DU RAB TU THIM PA'I PHYIR, MI GSAL BA'I NGO BOR GYUR PA DE'I TSE SKYES BU NYAG GCIG GNAS TE, DE'I PHYIR GROL BA ZHES BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"rnam 'gyur gyi tshogs thams cad kyang skye ba'i rim pa las bzlog pa'i sgo nas gtso bo de nyid du rab tu thim pa'i phyir, mi gsal ba'i ngo bor gyur pa de'i tshe skyes bu nyag gcig gnas te, de'i phyir grol ba zhes bya'o\", \"english\": \"At this point the entire group of expressions—the creations—dissolves completely back into the primal nature, in reverse order to the process by which they were created. The nature then is reduced to its own featureless essential form, and the soul simply rests in total isolation. This is how this system describes liberation.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30440,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3121,
        "acip": "SDUG BSNGAL NI DE LAS BZLOG PA'O,,",
        "wylie": "sdug bsngal ni de las bzlog pa'o",
        "english": "(3) Suffering is the opposite of that.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"SDUG BSNGAL NI DE LAS BZLOG PA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"sdug bsngal ni de las bzlog pa'o\", \"english\": \"(3) Suffering is the opposite of that.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30445,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3126,
        "acip": "DE LAS BZLOG PA NI CHOS MA YIN PA'O,,",
        "wylie": "de las bzlog pa ni chos ma yin pa'o",
        "english": "(8) The reverse of that is what we refer to when we speak of the opposite of the teachings.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE LAS BZLOG PA NI CHOS MA YIN PA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"de las bzlog pa ni chos ma yin pa'o\", \"english\": \"(8) The reverse of that is what we refer to when we speak of the opposite of the teachings.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30830,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3511,
        "acip": "PHAR LA 'PHANGS PA'I SKYON TSUR BZLOG PA'I TSE LAN MA THEBS PA NA KHAS BLANG MED CES SMRA BAR MI BYA'O,,",
        "wylie": "phar la 'phangs pa'i skyon tshur bzlog pa'i tshe lan ma thebs pa na khas blang med ces smra bar mi bya'o",
        "english": "It is not though the case that when problems with their position that we have pointed out are implied in it are cast back upon us, you should simply sit there without offering any reply, declaring that “We are beyond taking any position.” Another interpretation of names",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"PHAR LA 'PHANGS PA'I SKYON TSUR BZLOG PA'I TSE LAN MA THEBS PA NA KHAS BLANG MED CES SMRA BAR MI BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"phar la 'phangs pa'i skyon tshur bzlog pa'i tshe lan ma thebs pa na khas blang med ces smra bar mi bya'o\", \"english\": \"It is not though the case that when problems with their position that we have pointed out are implied in it are cast back upon us, you should simply sit there without offering any reply, declaring that “We are beyond taking any position.” Another interpretation of names\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30838,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3519,
        "acip": "ZHES DE LTA BUR RNAM PAR GNAS PA'I 'JIG RTEN GYI KUN RDZOB PHYIN CI LOG TU SMRA BA DE'I LTAR NA, BSHAD MA THAG PA'I YAN LAG CAN MED PA'I GTAN TSIGS DE NYID KYIS, YAN LAG LA SOGS PA TZAM YANG MED PAR 'GYUR BAS 'JIG RTEN NA GRAGS PA'I KUN RDZOB SHING RTA LA SOGS PA MA BRLAG PA STE 'JIG PAR MA BYED CIG CES 'DI NI BZLOG PAR BYA BA KHO NA'O,,",
        "wylie": "zhes de lta bur rnam par gnas pa'i 'jig rten gyi kun rdzob phyin ci log tu smra ba de'i ltar na, bshad ma thag pa'i yan lag can med pa'i gtan tshigs de nyid kyis, yan lag la sogs pa tsam yang med par 'gyur bas 'jig rten na grags pa'i kun rdzob shing rta la sogs pa ma brlag pa ste 'jig par ma byed cig ces 'di ni bzlog par bya ba kho na'o",
        "english": "Thus it is that that they describe, mistakenly, a deceptive nature of the world which exists in this way. According to the logical statement just described—where it is said that no whole of the composite parts exists—even just those parts and so on could no longer exist. And then the expression about the deceptive in the common parlance of the world—where someone says “don't ruin that wagon” or the like, asking that we refrain from destroying it—would only be something that was refused.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"ZHES DE LTA BUR RNAM PAR GNAS PA'I 'JIG RTEN GYI KUN RDZOB PHYIN CI LOG TU SMRA BA DE'I LTAR NA, BSHAD MA THAG PA'I YAN LAG CAN MED PA'I GTAN TSIGS DE NYID KYIS, YAN LAG LA SOGS PA TZAM YANG MED PAR 'GYUR BAS 'JIG RTEN NA GRAGS PA'I KUN RDZOB SHING RTA LA SOGS PA MA BRLAG PA STE 'JIG PAR MA BYED CIG CES 'DI NI BZLOG PAR BYA BA KHO NA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"zhes de lta bur rnam par gnas pa'i 'jig rten gyi kun rdzob phyin ci log tu smra ba de'i ltar na, bshad ma thag pa'i yan lag can med pa'i gtan tshigs de nyid kyis, yan lag la sogs pa tsam yang med par 'gyur bas 'jig rten na grags pa'i kun rdzob shing rta la sogs pa ma brlag pa ste 'jig par ma byed cig ces 'di ni bzlog par bya ba kho na'o\", \"english\": \"Thus it is that that they describe, mistakenly, a deceptive nature of the world which exists in this way. According to the logical statement just described—where it is said that no whole of the composite parts exists—even just those parts and so on could no longer exist. And then the expression about the deceptive in the common parlance of the world—where someone says “don't ruin that wagon” or the like, asking that we refrain from destroying it—would only be something that was refused.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 30875,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3556,
        "acip": "BDAG BRTEN NAS GDAGS PA LA 'JOG PA NA, BRTAN PA DANG MI BRTAN PA LA SOGS PA'I MTHAR 'DZIN GYI RTOG PA'I RTEN MIN PAS MI RTAG PA LA SOGS PAR NGO BO NYID KYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I RTOG PA BZLOG PAR SLA BAR 'GYUR RO ZHES BSHAD PA,",
        "wylie": "bdag brten nas gdags pa la 'jog pa na, brtan pa dang mi brtan pa la sogs pa'i mthar 'dzin gyi rtog pa'i rten min pas mi rtag pa la sogs par ngo bo nyid kyis grub par 'dzin pa'i rtog pa bzlog par sla bar 'gyur ro zhes bshad pa",
        "english": "Our next section here is presented in order to make the following point. Suppose we can establish that the self is something which is labeled in dependence. In this case, it would no longer serve as a foundation for the kind of thinking where we hold to the extremes of believing that things are either solid, or not solid. It then becomes relatively easy to stop the idea where we hold that things like impermanence could exist through some essence of their own.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"BDAG BRTEN NAS GDAGS PA LA 'JOG PA NA, BRTAN PA DANG MI BRTAN PA LA SOGS PA'I MTHAR 'DZIN GYI RTOG PA'I RTEN MIN PAS MI RTAG PA LA SOGS PAR NGO BO NYID KYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I RTOG PA BZLOG PAR SLA BAR 'GYUR RO ZHES BSHAD PA,\", \"wylie\": \"bdag brten nas gdags pa la 'jog pa na, brtan pa dang mi brtan pa la sogs pa'i mthar 'dzin gyi rtog pa'i rten min pas mi rtag pa la sogs par ngo bo nyid kyis grub par 'dzin pa'i rtog pa bzlog par sla bar 'gyur ro zhes bshad pa\", \"english\": \"Our next section here is presented in order to make the following point. Suppose we can establish that the self is something which is labeled in dependence. In this case, it would no longer serve as a foundation for the kind of thinking where we hold to the extremes of believing that things are either solid, or not solid. It then becomes relatively easy to stop the idea where we hold that things like impermanence could exist through some essence of their own.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31021,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3702,
        "acip": "DE LTA NA KHO BOS DNGOS POR SMRA BA'I RTZOD PA THAMS CAD BZLOG PA YIN LA, CHOS MTHUN GYI LAN YANG SUS KYANG BRJOD PAR MI NUS SO,,",
        "wylie": "de lta na kho bos dngos por smra ba'i rtsod pa thams cad bzlog pa yin la, chos mthun gyi lan yang sus kyang brjod par mi nus so",
        "english": "And so it is that we are able to put a stop to all the arguments proposed by the Functionalists; the fact is that no one could ever offer a truly relevant response.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE LTA NA KHO BOS DNGOS POR SMRA BA'I RTZOD PA THAMS CAD BZLOG PA YIN LA, CHOS MTHUN GYI LAN YANG SUS KYANG BRJOD PAR MI NUS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de lta na kho bos dngos por smra ba'i rtsod pa thams cad bzlog pa yin la, chos mthun gyi lan yang sus kyang brjod par mi nus so\", \"english\": \"And so it is that we are able to put a stop to all the arguments proposed by the Functionalists; the fact is that no one could ever offer a truly relevant response.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31035,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3716,
        "acip": "DE'I PHYIR SUN CI PHYIN DU RGOL BA GA LA YOD, GANG GIS NA RANG GI PHYOGS GZHAG TU MED CING GZHAN GYI PHYOGS BZLOG PA DON DAM PAR 'DOD PA'I MTSAN NYID BYAS PA 'DI, SUN CI PHYIN DU RGOL BA PA'I YANG DAG PA'I MTSAN NYID DU 'GYUR BA,",
        "wylie": "de'i phyir sun ci phyin du rgol ba ga la yod, gang gis na rang gi phyogs gzhag tu med cing gzhan gyi phyogs bzlog pa don dam par 'dod pa'i mtshan nyid byas pa 'di, sun ci phyin du rgol ba pa'i yang dag pa'i mtshan nyid du 'gyur ba",
        "english": "How then could there ever be an opponent that we overcame? Suppose that there were no position of our own that we could ever put forward, but that at the same time we posited some definitive statement that could put a stop to the other side's position. This would in itself require a definitive opponent—one that existed really, in order for us to overcome.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE'I PHYIR SUN CI PHYIN DU RGOL BA GA LA YOD, GANG GIS NA RANG GI PHYOGS GZHAG TU MED CING GZHAN GYI PHYOGS BZLOG PA DON DAM PAR 'DOD PA'I MTSAN NYID BYAS PA 'DI, SUN CI PHYIN DU RGOL BA PA'I YANG DAG PA'I MTSAN NYID DU 'GYUR BA,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i phyir sun ci phyin du rgol ba ga la yod, gang gis na rang gi phyogs gzhag tu med cing gzhan gyi phyogs bzlog pa don dam par 'dod pa'i mtshan nyid byas pa 'di, sun ci phyin du rgol ba pa'i yang dag pa'i mtshan nyid du 'gyur ba\", \"english\": \"How then could there ever be an opponent that we overcame? Suppose that there were no position of our own that we could ever put forward, but that at the same time we posited some definitive statement that could put a stop to the other side's position. This would in itself require a definitive opponent—one that existed really, in order for us to overcome.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31036,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3717,
        "acip": "DBU MA PA SUS RANG GI PHYOGS THA SNYAD DU MA BZHAG CING, GZHAN GYI PHYOGS BZLOG PA DON DAM DU 'DOD PA GNYIS KA MED PAS, DBU MA PA LA SUN CI PHYIN DU RGOL BA YANG YOD PA MIN PAS, RNAM PA THAMS CAD DU MI RIGS SO,,",
        "wylie": "dbu ma pa sus rang gi phyogs tha snyad du ma bzhag cing, gzhan gyi phyogs bzlog pa don dam du 'dod pa gnyis ka med pas, dbu ma pa la sun ci phyin du rgol ba yang yod pa min pas, rnam pa thams cad du mi rigs so",
        "english": "But neither of these things exists: there is no adherent of the middle way that would not want to put forth their own position only in a nominal manner; or who would want to disprove the other side's position in some ultimate manner. As such, there does not even exist a debater who could undertake to overthrow those of the middle way; and for this reason the entire exercise would be futile.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DBU MA PA SUS RANG GI PHYOGS THA SNYAD DU MA BZHAG CING, GZHAN GYI PHYOGS BZLOG PA DON DAM DU 'DOD PA GNYIS KA MED PAS, DBU MA PA LA SUN CI PHYIN DU RGOL BA YANG YOD PA MIN PAS, RNAM PA THAMS CAD DU MI RIGS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"dbu ma pa sus rang gi phyogs tha snyad du ma bzhag cing, gzhan gyi phyogs bzlog pa don dam du 'dod pa gnyis ka med pas, dbu ma pa la sun ci phyin du rgol ba yang yod pa min pas, rnam pa thams cad du mi rigs so\", \"english\": \"But neither of these things exists: there is no adherent of the middle way that would not want to put forth their own position only in a nominal manner; or who would want to disprove the other side's position in some ultimate manner. As such, there does not even exist a debater who could undertake to overthrow those of the middle way; and for this reason the entire exercise would be futile.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31129,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3810,
        "acip": "STONG PA NYID BDEN PAS STONG PAR GSUNGS PA NI STONG NYID DAM CHOS NYID LA DNGOS PO STE BDEN PAR 'DZIN PA'I BLO CAN GYI BDEN PAR 'DZIN PA BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I PHYIR RGYAL BA'I YUM LAS GSUNGS PA YIN NO,,",
        "wylie": "stong pa nyid bden pas stong par gsungs pa ni stong nyid dam chos nyid la dngos po ste bden par 'dzin pa'i blo can gyi bden par 'dzin pa bzlog par bya ba'i phyir rgyal ba'i yum las gsungs pa yin no",
        "english": "These statements that emptiness itself is empty of any true existence were made in the Mother Sutras themselves, in order to block the tendency to hold things as being real, in the thoughts of those whose minds were already grasping to the idea that things could be real: who were holding that emptiness or ultimate reality itself was a real thing.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"STONG PA NYID BDEN PAS STONG PAR GSUNGS PA NI STONG NYID DAM CHOS NYID LA DNGOS PO STE BDEN PAR 'DZIN PA'I BLO CAN GYI BDEN PAR 'DZIN PA BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I PHYIR RGYAL BA'I YUM LAS GSUNGS PA YIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"stong pa nyid bden pas stong par gsungs pa ni stong nyid dam chos nyid la dngos po ste bden par 'dzin pa'i blo can gyi bden par 'dzin pa bzlog par bya ba'i phyir rgyal ba'i yum las gsungs pa yin no\", \"english\": \"These statements that emptiness itself is empty of any true existence were made in the Mother Sutras themselves, in order to block the tendency to hold things as being real, in the thoughts of those whose minds were already grasping to the idea that things could be real: who were holding that emptiness or ultimate reality itself was a real thing.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31138,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3819,
        "acip": "SHAR LA SOGS PA 'DI DAG BCU CHAR PHYOGS RNAMS KYI NGO BO NYID KYIS GRUB PAS STONG PA NYID NI GANG YIN PA DE NI CHEN PO STONG PA NYID YIN TE, PHYOGS RNAMS CHEN POR RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU GSUNGS TE, PHYOGS LA PHYIN CI LOG TU 'DZIN PA NI MU STEGS BYE BRAG PAS PHYOGS RNAMS RTAG PA'I RDZAS NYID DU 'DZIN PA LTA BU'O,,",
        "wylie": "shar la sogs pa 'di dag bcu char phyogs rnams kyi ngo bo nyid kyis grub pas stong pa nyid ni gang yin pa de ni chen po stong pa nyid yin te, phyogs rnams chen por rang bzhin gyis grub par 'dzin pa bzlog pa'i phyir du gsungs te, phyogs la phyin ci log tu 'dzin pa ni mu stegs bye brag pas phyogs rnams rtag pa'i rdzas nyid du 'dzin pa lta bu'o",
        "english": "The fact that all ten directions of east and the rest are empty of existing through any essential nature of their own is “great emptiness.” And it was taught in order to stop people from holding that the directions are vast in a way that exists through some nature of its own. An example of taking the directions in a mistaken way would be where the River school called the Detailers holds that a direction represents some perfectly unchanging substance. 6) Ultimate emptiness",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"SHAR LA SOGS PA 'DI DAG BCU CHAR PHYOGS RNAMS KYI NGO BO NYID KYIS GRUB PAS STONG PA NYID NI GANG YIN PA DE NI CHEN PO STONG PA NYID YIN TE, PHYOGS RNAMS CHEN POR RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU GSUNGS TE, PHYOGS LA PHYIN CI LOG TU 'DZIN PA NI MU STEGS BYE BRAG PAS PHYOGS RNAMS RTAG PA'I RDZAS NYID DU 'DZIN PA LTA BU'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"shar la sogs pa 'di dag bcu char phyogs rnams kyi ngo bo nyid kyis grub pas stong pa nyid ni gang yin pa de ni chen po stong pa nyid yin te, phyogs rnams chen por rang bzhin gyis grub par 'dzin pa bzlog pa'i phyir du gsungs te, phyogs la phyin ci log tu 'dzin pa ni mu stegs bye brag pas phyogs rnams rtag pa'i rdzas nyid du 'dzin pa lta bu'o\", \"english\": \"The fact that all ten directions of east and the rest are empty of existing through any essential nature of their own is “great emptiness.” And it was taught in order to stop people from holding that the directions are vast in a way that exists through some nature of its own. An example of taking the directions in a mistaken way would be where the River school called the Detailers holds that a direction represents some perfectly unchanging substance. 6) Ultimate emptiness\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31140,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3821,
        "acip": ",MYANG 'DAS DNGOS PO'I BLO CAN GYI, ,'DZIN PA BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I PHYIR, ,DON DAM MKHYEN PAS DON DAM PA, ,STONG PA NYID NI BSTAN PAR MDZAD,",
        "wylie": "myang 'das dngos po'i blo can gyi, ,'dzin pa bzlog par bya ba'i phyir, ,don dam mkhyen pas don dam pa, ,stong pa nyid ni bstan par mdzad",
        "english": "The goal here Is to help put a stop To the tendency That people have In their mind To hold this nirvana As actual. This is why The one who knows The ultimate Undertook to teach This ultimate emptiness.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",MYANG 'DAS DNGOS PO'I BLO CAN GYI, ,'DZIN PA BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I PHYIR, ,DON DAM MKHYEN PAS DON DAM PA, ,STONG PA NYID NI BSTAN PAR MDZAD,\", \"wylie\": \"myang 'das dngos po'i blo can gyi, ,'dzin pa bzlog par bya ba'i phyir, ,don dam mkhyen pas don dam pa, ,stong pa nyid ni bstan par mdzad\", \"english\": \"The goal here Is to help put a stop To the tendency That people have In their mind To hold this nirvana As actual. This is why The one who knows The ultimate Undertook to teach This ultimate emptiness.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31143,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3824,
        "acip": "DE YANG MYA NGAN LAS 'DAS PA CHOS SKU DE LA DNGOS PO STE BDEN PAR 'DZIN PA'I BLO CAN GYI BDEN PAR 'DZIN PA BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I PHYIR DON DAM MKHYEN PA SANGS RGYAS KYIS DON DAM PA STONG PA NYID NI BSTAN PAR MDZAD DO,,",
        "wylie": "de yang mya ngan las 'das pa chos sku de la dngos po ste bden par 'dzin pa'i blo can gyi bden par 'dzin pa bzlog par bya ba'i phyir don dam mkhyen pa sangs rgyas kyis don dam pa stong pa nyid ni bstan par mdzad do",
        "english": "And our goal here is to help put a stop to the tendency that people have in their mind to hold this as being real: where they hold that this nirvana, this body of all things, is “actual”—meaning here “real.” And this is why the Buddha, the one who knows the ultimate, undertook to teach this ultimate emptiness. 7) The emptiness of produced things",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE YANG MYA NGAN LAS 'DAS PA CHOS SKU DE LA DNGOS PO STE BDEN PAR 'DZIN PA'I BLO CAN GYI BDEN PAR 'DZIN PA BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I PHYIR DON DAM MKHYEN PA SANGS RGYAS KYIS DON DAM PA STONG PA NYID NI BSTAN PAR MDZAD DO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de yang mya ngan las 'das pa chos sku de la dngos po ste bden par 'dzin pa'i blo can gyi bden par 'dzin pa bzlog par bya ba'i phyir don dam mkhyen pa sangs rgyas kyis don dam pa stong pa nyid ni bstan par mdzad do\", \"english\": \"And our goal here is to help put a stop to the tendency that people have in their mind to hold this as being real: where they hold that this nirvana, this body of all things, is “actual”—meaning here “real.” And this is why the Buddha, the one who knows the ultimate, undertook to teach this ultimate emptiness. 7) The emptiness of produced things\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31156,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 3837,
        "acip": "'DI NI TING 'DZIN RGYAL PO LAS, YOD MED DANG GTZANG MI GTZANG GI MTHA' GNYIS SPANGS NAS, DBUS LA'ANG GNAS PAR MI BYED DO, ZHES GSUNGS PA LTAR MTHA' SPANGS PA'I DBUS BDEN NAM SNYAM PA'I BDEN 'DZIN BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU GSUNGS PA STE,",
        "wylie": "'di ni ting 'dzin rgyal po las, yod med dang gtsang mi gtsang gi mtha' gnyis spangs nas, dbus la'ang gnas par mi byed do, zhes gsungs pa ltar mtha' spangs pa'i dbus bden nam snyam pa'i bden 'dzin bzlog pa'i phyir du gsungs pa ste",
        "english": "All this recalls for example the King of Concentration, where it says that we should give up pairs of extremes such as existing or not existing; or else being clean or not being clean; but that neither should we then stay somewhere in the middle between these extremes. This was spoken to help us stop our habit of wondering whether or not the view in the middle might exist in truth, once we have eliminated the two extremes in each case.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'DI NI TING 'DZIN RGYAL PO LAS, YOD MED DANG GTZANG MI GTZANG GI MTHA' GNYIS SPANGS NAS, DBUS LA'ANG GNAS PAR MI BYED DO, ZHES GSUNGS PA LTAR MTHA' SPANGS PA'I DBUS BDEN NAM SNYAM PA'I BDEN 'DZIN BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU GSUNGS PA STE,\", \"wylie\": \"'di ni ting 'dzin rgyal po las, yod med dang gtsang mi gtsang gi mtha' gnyis spangs nas, dbus la'ang gnas par mi byed do, zhes gsungs pa ltar mtha' spangs pa'i dbus bden nam snyam pa'i bden 'dzin bzlog pa'i phyir du gsungs pa ste\", \"english\": \"All this recalls for example the King of Concentration, where it says that we should give up pairs of extremes such as existing or not existing; or else being clean or not being clean; but that neither should we then stay somewhere in the middle between these extremes. This was spoken to help us stop our habit of wondering whether or not the view in the middle might exist in truth, once we have eliminated the two extremes in each case.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31633,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 4314,
        "acip": "DE LTAR BSHAD PA LAS BZLOG PA NI GNAS MIN PA ZHES BYA STE, DPER NA DGE BA LAS RNAM SMIN YID DU MI 'ONG BA 'BYUNG BA DANG, MTHONG LAM THOB PAS LAS DBANG GIS SRID PA BRGYAD PA 'GRUB PA NI GNAS MA YIN ZHING GO SKABS MED PA LTA BU'O,,",
        "wylie": "de ltar bshad pa las bzlog pa ni gnas min pa zhes bya ste, dper na dge ba las rnam smin yid du mi 'ong ba 'byung ba dang, mthong lam thob pas las dbang gis srid pa brgyad pa 'grub pa ni gnas ma yin zhing go skabs med pa lta bu'o",
        "english": "Anything that could happen in the opposite way from what we have described here then would be called what is “not actual.” For example, it would not be actual (there would be no chance of it) if a virtuous action led to an unpleasant ripening; or if an individual had attained the path of seeing and then—due to the power of karma—were to experience an eighth rebirth afterwards.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE LTAR BSHAD PA LAS BZLOG PA NI GNAS MIN PA ZHES BYA STE, DPER NA DGE BA LAS RNAM SMIN YID DU MI 'ONG BA 'BYUNG BA DANG, MTHONG LAM THOB PAS LAS DBANG GIS SRID PA BRGYAD PA 'GRUB PA NI GNAS MA YIN ZHING GO SKABS MED PA LTA BU'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"de ltar bshad pa las bzlog pa ni gnas min pa zhes bya ste, dper na dge ba las rnam smin yid du mi 'ong ba 'byung ba dang, mthong lam thob pas las dbang gis srid pa brgyad pa 'grub pa ni gnas ma yin zhing go skabs med pa lta bu'o\", \"english\": \"Anything that could happen in the opposite way from what we have described here then would be called what is “not actual.” For example, it would not be actual (there would be no chance of it) if a virtuous action led to an unpleasant ripening; or if an individual had attained the path of seeing and then—due to the power of karma—were to experience an eighth rebirth afterwards.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31637,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 4318,
        "acip": "DE GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA STE LAS 'DRES MA DANG, ZAG BCAS KYI LAS NGAN PA ZAD PAR BYED PA'I ZAG MED KYI DNGOS PO'I LAS RNAMS DANG, LAM DE'I RNAM SMIN SHIN TU MI 'DRA BA SNA TSOGS PA LA'ANG,",
        "wylie": "de gnyis las bzlog pa ste las 'dres ma dang, zag bcas kyi las ngan pa zad par byed pa'i zag med kyi dngos po'i las rnams dang, lam de'i rnam smin shin tu mi 'dra ba sna tshogs pa la'ang",
        "english": "And then there are cases of the opposite of these two; meaning that the karmas are mixed. And then there are the karmas that represent entities which are immaculate—which in fact function to finish off negative karmas. And then there are the great variety of different ripenings that are produced by this path.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA STE LAS 'DRES MA DANG, ZAG BCAS KYI LAS NGAN PA ZAD PAR BYED PA'I ZAG MED KYI DNGOS PO'I LAS RNAMS DANG, LAM DE'I RNAM SMIN SHIN TU MI 'DRA BA SNA TSOGS PA LA'ANG,\", \"wylie\": \"de gnyis las bzlog pa ste las 'dres ma dang, zag bcas kyi las ngan pa zad par byed pa'i zag med kyi dngos po'i las rnams dang, lam de'i rnam smin shin tu mi 'dra ba sna tshogs pa la'ang\", \"english\": \"And then there are cases of the opposite of these two; meaning that the karmas are mixed. And then there are the karmas that represent entities which are immaculate—which in fact function to finish off negative karmas. And then there are the great variety of different ripenings that are produced by this path.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31763,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 4444,
        "acip": ",GANG GI PHYIR NA MI MKHAS DNGOS DANG DNGOS MED PAR ZHEN PA YI BLO CAN GYIS, ,SKYE DANG 'JIG GNAS SKABS DANG SDUG DANG MI SDUG BRAL PHRAD KYIS BSKYED SDUG BSNGAL DANG, ,SDIG CAN 'GRO BA 'THOB PA DE PHYIR 'JIG RTEN THUGS BRTZE'I YUL DU RAB DONG BAS, ,BCOM LDAN THUGS RJES KHYOD THUGS ZHI LAS BZLOG PAS KHYOD LA MYA NGAN 'DAS MI MNGA',",
        "wylie": "gang gi phyir na mi mkhas dngos dang dngos med par zhen pa yi blo can gyis, ,skye dang 'jig gnas skabs dang sdug dang mi sdug bral phrad kyis bskyed sdug bsngal dang, ,sdig can 'gro ba 'thob pa de phyir 'jig rten thugs brtse'i yul du rab dong bas, ,bcom ldan thugs rjes khyod thugs zhi las bzlog pas khyod la mya ngan 'das mi mnga'",
        "english": "What is the Reason for this? It is all because Those who are Not well versed Still possess A state of mind Where they have Strong craving. And because of this, They undergo Periods when they Are born & destroyed. They are torn From what feels Attractive, and Meet with The undesirable; They achieve The future fate of Those who still have Terrible deeds. The world Has crowded to Worthy objects Of compassion. As such, you— The Conqueror— Have, out of This compassion, Turned your heart Away from peace, Without allowing Yourself to go Into nirvana.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",GANG GI PHYIR NA MI MKHAS DNGOS DANG DNGOS MED PAR ZHEN PA YI BLO CAN GYIS, ,SKYE DANG 'JIG GNAS SKABS DANG SDUG DANG MI SDUG BRAL PHRAD KYIS BSKYED SDUG BSNGAL DANG, ,SDIG CAN 'GRO BA 'THOB PA DE PHYIR 'JIG RTEN THUGS BRTZE'I YUL DU RAB DONG BAS, ,BCOM LDAN THUGS RJES KHYOD THUGS ZHI LAS BZLOG PAS KHYOD LA MYA NGAN 'DAS MI MNGA',\", \"wylie\": \"gang gi phyir na mi mkhas dngos dang dngos med par zhen pa yi blo can gyis, ,skye dang 'jig gnas skabs dang sdug dang mi sdug bral phrad kyis bskyed sdug bsngal dang, ,sdig can 'gro ba 'thob pa de phyir 'jig rten thugs brtse'i yul du rab dong bas, ,bcom ldan thugs rjes khyod thugs zhi las bzlog pas khyod la mya ngan 'das mi mnga'\", \"english\": \"What is the Reason for this? It is all because Those who are Not well versed Still possess A state of mind Where they have Strong craving. And because of this, They undergo Periods when they Are born & destroyed. They are torn From what feels Attractive, and Meet with The undesirable; They achieve The future fate of Those who still have Terrible deeds. The world Has crowded to Worthy objects Of compassion. As such, you— The Conqueror— Have, out of This compassion, Turned your heart Away from peace, Without allowing Yourself to go Into nirvana.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31768,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 4449,
        "acip": "'JIG RTEN GYI SKYE BO DE RNAMS THUGS BRTZE BA CHEN PO'I YUL DU RAB TU DONG BA STE SONG BAS NA, THUGS RJE CHEN POS BCOM LDAN 'DAS KHYOD KYI THUGS ZHI BA PHYOGS GCIG PA LA GZHOL BA LAS BZLOG PAS KHYOD LA ZHI BA PHYOGS GCIG PA'I MYA NGAN LAS 'DAS PA MI MNGA' BAR 'JIG RTEN DU BZHUGS SO,,",
        "wylie": "'jig rten gyi skye bo de rnams thugs brtse ba chen po'i yul du rab tu dong ba ste song bas na, thugs rje chen pos bcom ldan 'das khyod kyi thugs zhi ba phyogs gcig pa la gzhol ba las bzlog pas khyod la zhi ba phyogs gcig pa'i mya ngan las 'das pa mi mnga' bar 'jig rten du bzhugs so",
        "english": "The beings of this world have “crowded to”—have become—worthy objects of great compassion. As such, “you”—the Conqueror—have, out of this great compassion, turned away from allowing your heart to be lost to but a partial state of peace. That is, you remain here in the world, but without allowing yourself to go into a kind of nirvana which represents one side of the condition of peace. How the commentary was composed",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'JIG RTEN GYI SKYE BO DE RNAMS THUGS BRTZE BA CHEN PO'I YUL DU RAB TU DONG BA STE SONG BAS NA, THUGS RJE CHEN POS BCOM LDAN 'DAS KHYOD KYI THUGS ZHI BA PHYOGS GCIG PA LA GZHOL BA LAS BZLOG PAS KHYOD LA ZHI BA PHYOGS GCIG PA'I MYA NGAN LAS 'DAS PA MI MNGA' BAR 'JIG RTEN DU BZHUGS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"'jig rten gyi skye bo de rnams thugs brtse ba chen po'i yul du rab tu dong ba ste song bas na, thugs rje chen pos bcom ldan 'das khyod kyi thugs zhi ba phyogs gcig pa la gzhol ba las bzlog pas khyod la zhi ba phyogs gcig pa'i mya ngan las 'das pa mi mnga' bar 'jig rten du bzhugs so\", \"english\": \"The beings of this world have “crowded to”—have become—worthy objects of great compassion. As such, “you”—the Conqueror—have, out of this great compassion, turned away from allowing your heart to be lost to but a partial state of peace. That is, you remain here in the world, but without allowing yourself to go into a kind of nirvana which represents one side of the condition of peace. How the commentary was composed\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31812,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 4493,
        "acip": ",MI PHROGS PA'I MKHYEN RAB DANG THUGS RJE MNGA' BA, ,RI MOR BRIS PA'I BA DRUS LAS 'O MA BZHOS PAS BDEN PAR ZHEN PA BZLOG PAR MDZAD PAS SBYAR BA RDZOGS SO,,",
        "wylie": "mi phrogs pa'i mkhyen rab dang thugs rje mnga' ba, ,ri mor bris pa'i ba drus las 'o ma bzhos pas bden par zhen pa bzlog par mdzad pas sbyar ba rdzogs so",
        "english": "He held a high level of knowledge that was never stolen away; and he possessed compassion as well. He was known for sketching a cow that was about to give birth, and then milking real milk from it. With this, he was able to stop others from believing that things are real. This then is the one who completed this composition.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",MI PHROGS PA'I MKHYEN RAB DANG THUGS RJE MNGA' BA, ,RI MOR BRIS PA'I BA DRUS LAS 'O MA BZHOS PAS BDEN PAR ZHEN PA BZLOG PAR MDZAD PAS SBYAR BA RDZOGS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"mi phrogs pa'i mkhyen rab dang thugs rje mnga' ba, ,ri mor bris pa'i ba drus las 'o ma bzhos pas bden par zhen pa bzlog par mdzad pas sbyar ba rdzogs so\", \"english\": \"He held a high level of knowledge that was never stolen away; and he possessed compassion as well. He was known for sketching a cow that was about to give birth, and then milking real milk from it. With this, he was able to stop others from believing that things are real. This then is the one who completed this composition.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31981,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 4662,
        "acip": "b11) THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA KHAS LEN PA YI 'GAL BA, [101a]",
        "wylie": "b11) thal ba las bzlog pa khas len pa yi 'gal ba, [101a]",
        "english": "The contradiction that arises from accepting the opposite of the absurdity",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"b11) THAL BA LAS BZLOG PA KHAS LEN PA YI 'GAL BA, [101a]\", \"wylie\": \"b11) thal ba las bzlog pa khas len pa yi 'gal ba, [101a]\", \"english\": \"The contradiction that arises from accepting the opposite of the absurdity\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 23251,
        "course": "MB2",
        "seq": 502,
        "acip": "BDAG CAG NI DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA DANG, SEMS CAN THAMS CAD LA BYAMS PA DANG, BYAS PA GZO BA LAGS TE, DUS MA YIN PAR 'GUM PA THAMS CAD BZLOG PAR BGYI'O,,",
        "wylie": "bdag cag ni de bzhin gshegs pa dang, sems can thams cad la byams pa dang, byas pa gzo ba lags te, dus ma yin par 'gum pa thams cad bzlog par bgyi'o",
        "english": "We will keep thoughts of love in our hearts for these Ones who have Gone That Way; and for each and every living being.  We will repay the kindness shown to us, and turn back all death that comes before its time.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"MB2\", \"acip\": \"BDAG CAG NI DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA DANG, SEMS CAN THAMS CAD LA BYAMS PA DANG, BYAS PA GZO BA LAGS TE, DUS MA YIN PAR 'GUM PA THAMS CAD BZLOG PAR BGYI'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"bdag cag ni de bzhin gshegs pa dang, sems can thams cad la byams pa dang, byas pa gzo ba lags te, dus ma yin par 'gum pa thams cad bzlog par bgyi'o\", \"english\": \"We will keep thoughts of love in our hearts for these Ones who have Gone That Way; and for each and every living being.  We will repay the kindness shown to us, and turn back all death that comes before its time.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 36095,
        "course": "OSE",
        "seq": 67,
        "acip": ",DE LAS BZLOG PAR MTHONG BA NA, ,STONG LA BYA BA MI RUNG ZHING, ,BYA DANG BCAS LA STONG MED PAS, ,NYAM NGA'I G-YANG DU LHUNG BAR BZHED,",
        "wylie": "de las bzlog par mthong ba na, ,stong la bya ba mi rung zhing, ,bya dang bcas la stong med pas, ,nyam nga'i g-yang du lhung bar bzhed",
        "english": "If you see things As being the opposite Of that, Then the workings of things Could never apply Where things were empty. What we say is that- Since then nothing Involving action Could be empty- One would fall Into the pitiful abyss.",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \",DE LAS BZLOG PAR MTHONG BA NA, ,STONG LA BYA BA MI RUNG ZHING, ,BYA DANG BCAS LA STONG MED PAS, ,NYAM NGA'I G-YANG DU LHUNG BAR BZHED,\", \"wylie\": \"de las bzlog par mthong ba na, ,stong la bya ba mi rung zhing, ,bya dang bcas la stong med pas, ,nyam nga'i g-yang du lhung bar bzhed\", \"english\": \"If you see things As being the opposite Of that, Then the workings of things Could never apply Where things were empty. What we say is that- Since then nothing Involving action Could be empty- One would fall Into the pitiful abyss.\", \"course\": \"OSE\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 36333,
        "course": "OSE",
        "seq": 305,
        "acip": ",DE LAS BZLOG PAR MTHONG BA NA, ,STONG LA BYA BA MI RUNG ZHING, ,BYA DANG BCAS LA STONG MED PAS, ,NYAM NGA'I G-YANG DU LHUNG BAR BZHED,",
        "wylie": "de las bzlog par mthong ba na, ,stong la bya ba mi rung zhing, ,bya dang bcas la stong med pas, ,nyam nga'i g-yang du lhung bar bzhed",
        "english": "If you see things As being the opposite Of that, Then the workings of things Could never apply Where things were empty. What we say is that- Since then nothing Involving action Could be empty- One would fall Into the pitiful abyss.",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \",DE LAS BZLOG PAR MTHONG BA NA, ,STONG LA BYA BA MI RUNG ZHING, ,BYA DANG BCAS LA STONG MED PAS, ,NYAM NGA'I G-YANG DU LHUNG BAR BZHED,\", \"wylie\": \"de las bzlog par mthong ba na, ,stong la bya ba mi rung zhing, ,bya dang bcas la stong med pas, ,nyam nga'i g-yang du lhung bar bzhed\", \"english\": \"If you see things As being the opposite Of that, Then the workings of things Could never apply Where things were empty. What we say is that- Since then nothing Involving action Could be empty- One would fall Into the pitiful abyss.\", \"course\": \"OSE\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34253,
        "course": "P1",
        "seq": 8,
        "acip": "RE ZHIG NGAN 'GRO'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I SLAD DU DKON MCHOG GSUM LA SKYABS SU 'GRO, ZHING SNGON BYAS KYI SDIG PA LA GNYEN PO STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BSHAGS BSDAMS SHUGS DRAG DANG DGE MI DGE'I BLANG DOR PHRA ZHING PHRA BA LA 'BAD",
        "wylie": "re zhig ngan 'gro'i skye ba bzlog pa'i slad du dkon mchog gsum la skyabs su 'gro, zhing sngon byas kyi sdig pa la gnyen po stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bshags bsdams shugs drag dang dge mi dge'i blang dor phra zhing phra ba la 'bad",
        "english": "We must then for the short term go for shelter to the Three Jewels, to keep ourselves from a birth in the lower realms. We must as well open up and admit the things we have done wrong before, and restrain ourselves in the future. To do so we must make strong and heartfelt use of the four forces that counteract the power of the wrong. We must too put all our effort into taking up even the smallest virtue, and giving up even the slightest harms we commit",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P1\", \"acip\": \"RE ZHIG NGAN 'GRO'I SKYE BA BZLOG PA'I SLAD DU DKON MCHOG GSUM LA SKYABS SU 'GRO, ZHING SNGON BYAS KYI SDIG PA LA GNYEN PO STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BSHAGS BSDAMS SHUGS DRAG DANG DGE MI DGE'I BLANG DOR PHRA ZHING PHRA BA LA 'BAD\", \"wylie\": \"re zhig ngan 'gro'i skye ba bzlog pa'i slad du dkon mchog gsum la skyabs su 'gro, zhing sngon byas kyi sdig pa la gnyen po stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bshags bsdams shugs drag dang dge mi dge'i blang dor phra zhing phra ba la 'bad\", \"english\": \"We must then for the short term go for shelter to the Three Jewels, to keep ourselves from a birth in the lower realms. We must as well open up and admit the things we have done wrong before, and restrain ourselves in the future. To do so we must make strong and heartfelt use of the four forces that counteract the power of the wrong. We must too put all our effort into taking up even the smallest virtue, and giving up even the slightest harms we commit\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 35026,
        "course": "P10",
        "seq": 17,
        "acip": "TSE RABS KUN TU RGYAL BA TZONG KHA PAS, THEG MCHOG BSHES GNYEN DNGOS SU MDZAD PA'I MTHUS, RGYAL BAS BSNGAGS PA'I LAM BZANG DE NYID LAS, SKAD CIG TZAM YANG BZLOG PAR MA GYUR CIG",
        "wylie": "tshe rabs kun tu rgyal ba tsong kha pas, theg mchog bshes gnyen dngos su mdzad pa'i mthus, rgyal bas bsngags pa'i lam bzang de nyid las, skad cig tsam yang bzlog par ma gyur cig",
        "english": "Through all the string of my future lives May Tsongkapa the Victor come to me And serve himself as my spiritual guide In the teachings of the Greater Way. ACI Daily Practice X A Thousand Angels of the Heaven of Bliss",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P10\", \"acip\": \"TSE RABS KUN TU RGYAL BA TZONG KHA PAS, THEG MCHOG BSHES GNYEN DNGOS SU MDZAD PA'I MTHUS, RGYAL BAS BSNGAGS PA'I LAM BZANG DE NYID LAS, SKAD CIG TZAM YANG BZLOG PAR MA GYUR CIG\", \"wylie\": \"tshe rabs kun tu rgyal ba tsong kha pas, theg mchog bshes gnyen dngos su mdzad pa'i mthus, rgyal bas bsngags pa'i lam bzang de nyid las, skad cig tsam yang bzlog par ma gyur cig\", \"english\": \"Through all the string of my future lives May Tsongkapa the Victor come to me And serve himself as my spiritual guide In the teachings of the Greater Way. ACI Daily Practice X A Thousand Angels of the Heaven of Bliss\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34613,
        "course": "P5",
        "seq": 36,
        "acip": "RTZWA DANG SHING DANG SHING GEL PA DANG YAL GA DANG LO, MA THAMS CAD DANG SEMS CAN SROG CHAGS 'BYUNG PO THAMS CAD PHYE MAR 'THAG CING 'ONG BA LA NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO",
        "wylie": "rtswa dang shing dang shing gel pa dang yal ga dang lo, ma thams cad dang sems can srog chags 'byung po thams cad phye mar 'thag cing 'ong ba la ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no",
        "english": "Every trace of vegetation— from the roots, to the stalks, to the branches, to the twigs, and out to the leaves, would be crushed to powder. And every livingcreature there, would be smashed to dust. And as the mountains fell in it would happen in a minute, and no one would be able to outrun them, or force them back, or buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P5\", \"acip\": \"RTZWA DANG SHING DANG SHING GEL PA DANG YAL GA DANG LO, MA THAMS CAD DANG SEMS CAN SROG CHAGS 'BYUNG PO THAMS CAD PHYE MAR 'THAG CING 'ONG BA LA NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO\", \"wylie\": \"rtswa dang shing dang shing gel pa dang yal ga dang lo, ma thams cad dang sems can srog chags 'byung po thams cad phye mar 'thag cing 'ong ba la ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no\", \"english\": \"Every trace of vegetation— from the roots, to the stalks, to the branches, to the twigs, and out to the leaves, would be crushed to powder. And every livingcreature there, would be smashed to dust. And as the mountains fell in it would happen in a minute, and no one would be able to outrun them, or force them back, or buy them off. Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34614,
        "course": "P5",
        "seq": 37,
        "acip": "RGYAL PO CHEN PO DE BZHIN DU 'JIGS PA CHEN PO, BZHI 'DI DAG 'ONG STE DE DAG LAS MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN GYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO",
        "wylie": "rgyal po chen po de bzhin du 'jigs pa chen po, bzhi 'di dag 'ong ste de dag las mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman gyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no",
        "english": "Here, great King, our life is the same. When the four great terrors no one can outrun them, Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P5\", \"acip\": \"RGYAL PO CHEN PO DE BZHIN DU 'JIGS PA CHEN PO, BZHI 'DI DAG 'ONG STE DE DAG LAS MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN GYIS BZLOG PAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO\", \"wylie\": \"rgyal po chen po de bzhin du 'jigs pa chen po, bzhi 'di dag 'ong ste de dag las mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman gyis bzlog par sla ba ni ma yin no\", \"english\": \"Here, great King, our life is the same. When the four great terrors no one can outrun them, Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34615,
        "course": "P5",
        "seq": 38,
        "acip": "BZHI GANG ZHE NA RGA BA DANG NA BA DANG 'CHI BA DANG RGUD, PA'O RGYAL PO CHEN PO RGA BA NI DAR LA BAB PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG NA BA NI NAD MED PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG RGUD PA NI PHUN SUM TSOGS PA THAMS CAD 'JOMS SHING 'ONG 'CHI BA NI SROG 'JOMS SHING 'ONG STE DE DAG LAS NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS ZHI BAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO ZHES SO",
        "wylie": "bzhi gang zhe na rga ba dang na ba dang 'chi ba dang rgud, pa'o rgyal po chen po rga ba ni dar la bab pa 'joms shing 'ong na ba ni nad med pa 'joms shing 'ong rgud pa ni phun sum tshogs pa thams cad 'joms shing 'ong 'chi ba ni srog 'joms shing 'ong ste de dag las ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis zhi bar sla ba ni ma yin no zhes so",
        "english": "And what are these four terrors? They are aging, and sickness, and death, and trouble. Great King, I say to you: aging is the fact that our vitality is inevitably obliterated; sickness is the fact that our health is inevitably obliterated; trouble is the fact that any good thing that ever comes to us is inevitably obliterated; and death is the fact that our very life is inevitably obliterated. All four of these come to us, and we cannot outrun them, Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P5\", \"acip\": \"BZHI GANG ZHE NA RGA BA DANG NA BA DANG 'CHI BA DANG RGUD, PA'O RGYAL PO CHEN PO RGA BA NI DAR LA BAB PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG NA BA NI NAD MED PA 'JOMS SHING 'ONG RGUD PA NI PHUN SUM TSOGS PA THAMS CAD 'JOMS SHING 'ONG 'CHI BA NI SROG 'JOMS SHING 'ONG STE DE DAG LAS NI MGYOGS PAS 'BROS PA'AM STOBS KYIS BZLOG PA'AM NOR GYIS BZLOG PA'AM RDZAS DANG SNGAGS DANG SMAN RNAMS KYIS ZHI BAR SLA BA NI MA YIN NO ZHES SO\", \"wylie\": \"bzhi gang zhe na rga ba dang na ba dang 'chi ba dang rgud, pa'o rgyal po chen po rga ba ni dar la bab pa 'joms shing 'ong na ba ni nad med pa 'joms shing 'ong rgud pa ni phun sum tshogs pa thams cad 'joms shing 'ong 'chi ba ni srog 'joms shing 'ong ste de dag las ni mgyogs pas 'bros pa'am stobs kyis bzlog pa'am nor gyis bzlog pa'am rdzas dang sngags dang sman rnams kyis zhi bar sla ba ni ma yin no zhes so\", \"english\": \"And what are these four terrors? They are aging, and sickness, and death, and trouble. Great King, I say to you: aging is the fact that our vitality is inevitably obliterated; sickness is the fact that our health is inevitably obliterated; trouble is the fact that any good thing that ever comes to us is inevitably obliterated; and death is the fact that our very life is inevitably obliterated. All four of these come to us, and we cannot outrun them, Nor but with the greatest difficulty could you use any kind of magic substance, or magic words, or any sort of elixir to stop them.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34714,
        "course": "P6",
        "seq": 11,
        "acip": "DE YANG LTUNG BA PHYIR BCOS NI SDOM PA GONG 'OG SO SO NAS GSUNGS PA LTAR BYA LA SDIG PA, PHYIR BCOS PA NI STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BYA STE CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA'I MDO LAS BYAMS PA BYANG CHUB SEMS DPA' SEMS DPA' CHEN PO CHOS BZHI DANG LDAN NA SDIG PA BYAS SHING BSAGS PA YANG ZIL GYIS GNON PAR 'GYUR RO BZHI GANG ZHE NA 'DI LTA STE RNAM PAR SUN 'BYIN PA KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG GNYEN PO KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG NYES PA LAS BZLOG PA'I STOBS DANG RTEN GYI STOBS SO ZHES GSUNGS TE BYAS SHING BSAGS PA'I LAS NI MYONG NGES YIN PAS DE ZIL GYIS GNON NUS NA MA NGES PA LTA CI SMOS",
        "wylie": "de yang ltung ba phyir bcos ni sdom pa gong 'og so so nas gsungs pa ltar bya la sdig pa, phyir bcos pa ni stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bya ste chos bzhi bstan pa'i mdo las byams pa byang chub sems dpa' sems dpa' chen po chos bzhi dang ldan na sdig pa byas shing bsags pa yang zil gyis gnon par 'gyur ro bzhi gang zhe na 'di lta ste rnam par sun 'byin pa kun tu spyod pa dang gnyen po kun tu spyod pa dang nyes pa las bzlog pa'i stobs dang rten gyi stobs so zhes gsungs te byas shing bsags pa'i las ni myong nges yin pas de zil gyis gnon nus na ma nges pa lta ci smos",
        "english": "Instances in which you have committed some transgression of your vows, at whichever level of vows you may have, should be repaired according to the methods prescribed for those particular vows. For repairing the damage caused by other kinds of bad deeds you should utilize the four powers. As the Sutra on the Four Practices states, O Loving One, if a great bodhisattva keeps four practices, then he or she can never be overwhelmed by bad deeds that they have both committed and collected. And what are these four practices? They are the activity of destruction, and the activity of an antidote, and the power of restoring, and the power of the The expression \"committed and collected\" refers to actions whose karmic results are \"certain to be experienced.\" If even these types of karmic results can be overcome by the four practices, then there is no need to mention those which are not certain to be experienced. An Original Source for the Principle that Old Karma Can be Removed The general principle that old karma can be removed from our mind streams is clearly stated in a number of important works by Lord Buddha himself. One of the most famous of these is a part from the famous Diamond-Cutter Sutra, which appears in the following selection in bold lettering. The commentary that follows the sutra is the only known native Tibetan explanation of the work, and was composed by Chone Drakpa Shedrup (1675-1748) of the incomparable Sera Mey Tibetan Monastery. Chone Lama skillfully weaves the root text into his explanation, and these words have also been printed in bold. He moreover quotes several other important original sources to demonstrate that old karma can be removed.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P6\", \"acip\": \"DE YANG LTUNG BA PHYIR BCOS NI SDOM PA GONG 'OG SO SO NAS GSUNGS PA LTAR BYA LA SDIG PA, PHYIR BCOS PA NI STOBS BZHI'I SGO NAS BYA STE CHOS BZHI BSTAN PA'I MDO LAS BYAMS PA BYANG CHUB SEMS DPA' SEMS DPA' CHEN PO CHOS BZHI DANG LDAN NA SDIG PA BYAS SHING BSAGS PA YANG ZIL GYIS GNON PAR 'GYUR RO BZHI GANG ZHE NA 'DI LTA STE RNAM PAR SUN 'BYIN PA KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG GNYEN PO KUN TU SPYOD PA DANG NYES PA LAS BZLOG PA'I STOBS DANG RTEN GYI STOBS SO ZHES GSUNGS TE BYAS SHING BSAGS PA'I LAS NI MYONG NGES YIN PAS DE ZIL GYIS GNON NUS NA MA NGES PA LTA CI SMOS\", \"wylie\": \"de yang ltung ba phyir bcos ni sdom pa gong 'og so so nas gsungs pa ltar bya la sdig pa, phyir bcos pa ni stobs bzhi'i sgo nas bya ste chos bzhi bstan pa'i mdo las byams pa byang chub sems dpa' sems dpa' chen po chos bzhi dang ldan na sdig pa byas shing bsags pa yang zil gyis gnon par 'gyur ro bzhi gang zhe na 'di lta ste rnam par sun 'byin pa kun tu spyod pa dang gnyen po kun tu spyod pa dang nyes pa las bzlog pa'i stobs dang rten gyi stobs so zhes gsungs te byas shing bsags pa'i las ni myong nges yin pas de zil gyis gnon nus na ma nges pa lta ci smos\", \"english\": \"Instances in which you have committed some transgression of your vows, at whichever level of vows you may have, should be repaired according to the methods prescribed for those particular vows. For repairing the damage caused by other kinds of bad deeds you should utilize the four powers. As the Sutra on the Four Practices states, O Loving One, if a great bodhisattva keeps four practices, then he or she can never be overwhelmed by bad deeds that they have both committed and collected. And what are these four practices? They are the activity of destruction, and the activity of an antidote, and the power of restoring, and the power of the The expression \\\"committed and collected\\\" refers to actions whose karmic results are \\\"certain to be experienced.\\\" If even these types of karmic results can be overcome by the four practices, then there is no need to mention those which are not certain to be experienced. An Original Source for the Principle that Old Karma Can be Removed The general principle that old karma can be removed from our mind streams is clearly stated in a number of important works by Lord Buddha himself. One of the most famous of these is a part from the famous Diamond-Cutter Sutra, which appears in the following selection in bold lettering. The commentary that follows the sutra is the only known native Tibetan explanation of the work, and was composed by Chone Drakpa Shedrup (1675-1748) of the incomparable Sera Mey Tibetan Monastery. Chone Lama skillfully weaves the root text into his explanation, and these words have also been printed in bold. He moreover quotes several other important original sources to demonstrate that old karma can be removed.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34741,
        "course": "P6",
        "seq": 38,
        "acip": "PA NI MDZA' BO'I BU GLING GZHAN DU 'GRO BAR 'DOD PA MAS BRTZE BAS RKANG PA DAG LA, 'JUS NAS BZLOG PA NAM LA KHRO BA DRAG POS MGO BO LA RKANG PA BRGYAB NAS GZINGS LA ZHON PA NAS JI SRID GZINGS ZHIG NAS RGYA MTSO'I 'GROM NA NYI TSE BA'I DMYAL BA YOD PAR 'DIS MA LA MA GUS PA BYAS PA'I LAS ZAD PAS SDOD CIG 'KHOR LO 'DI KHYOD KYI SPYI BOR BSKOR BAR 'GYUR RO ZHES PA'I SGRA BAR SNANG LAS THOS NAS GANG SU'ANG RUNG BA 'GRO BA 'DI NA MA LA MI GUS PAR BYAS PA RNAMS DANG BYED PA RNAMS DANG BYED PAR 'GYUR PA DE RNAMS KYI LAS DE BDAG KHO NA LA RNAM PAR SMIN PAR GYUR CIGS YANG RNAM PA 'DI LTA BU'I SDUG BSNGAL GYI SKAL BA CAN DU MA GYUR CIG CES SHUGS DRAG POS SMON PA LA GNAS PA",
        "wylie": "pa ni mdza' bo'i bu gling gzhan du 'gro bar 'dod pa mas brtse bas rkang pa dag la, 'jus nas bzlog pa nam la khro ba drag pos mgo bo la rkang pa brgyab nas gzings la zhon pa nas ji srid gzings zhig nas rgya mtsho'i 'grom na nyi tshe ba'i dmyal ba yod par 'dis ma la ma gus pa byas pa'i las zad pas sdod cig 'khor lo 'di khyod kyi spyi bor bskor bar 'gyur ro zhes pa'i sgra bar snang las thos nas gang su'ang rung ba 'gro ba 'di na ma la mi gus par byas pa rnams dang byed pa rnams dang byed par 'gyur pa de rnams kyi las de bdag kho na la rnam par smin par gyur cigs yang rnam pa 'di lta bu'i sdug bsngal gyi skal ba can du ma gyur cig ces shugs drag pos smon pa la gnas pa",
        "english": "A person who relies on the Wish in the form of a prayer can rip out, from its roots, the obstacle created by the deed they've committed. There are a number of references we could use to demonstrate this fact. The young man named \"Child of the Friend\" wanted to travel across the sea to another land; his mother, for sheer love of the boy, would kneel and grasp him by his two legs, and was thus able to stop him. There came a point though where he was seized by violent anger at her, and he hit her on the head with his leg. Then he went to sea, but his ship went down in a storm. There was a near-hell at the edge of the ocean, and here the karma seed from disrespecting his mother was awakened. He heard a voice from the sky that said \"Kneel here, for the crown of knives is about to spin down upon your head.\" And he stood, making to himself a fervent prayer: May the karma of all those who have ever disrespected their mother in the past; or who are disrespecting their mother at this moment; or who may ever in the future disrespect their mother all ripen upon me alone, at this very moment. May no one else ever suffer the pain that I am about to undergo.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P6\", \"acip\": \"PA NI MDZA' BO'I BU GLING GZHAN DU 'GRO BAR 'DOD PA MAS BRTZE BAS RKANG PA DAG LA, 'JUS NAS BZLOG PA NAM LA KHRO BA DRAG POS MGO BO LA RKANG PA BRGYAB NAS GZINGS LA ZHON PA NAS JI SRID GZINGS ZHIG NAS RGYA MTSO'I 'GROM NA NYI TSE BA'I DMYAL BA YOD PAR 'DIS MA LA MA GUS PA BYAS PA'I LAS ZAD PAS SDOD CIG 'KHOR LO 'DI KHYOD KYI SPYI BOR BSKOR BAR 'GYUR RO ZHES PA'I SGRA BAR SNANG LAS THOS NAS GANG SU'ANG RUNG BA 'GRO BA 'DI NA MA LA MI GUS PAR BYAS PA RNAMS DANG BYED PA RNAMS DANG BYED PAR 'GYUR PA DE RNAMS KYI LAS DE BDAG KHO NA LA RNAM PAR SMIN PAR GYUR CIGS YANG RNAM PA 'DI LTA BU'I SDUG BSNGAL GYI SKAL BA CAN DU MA GYUR CIG CES SHUGS DRAG POS SMON PA LA GNAS PA\", \"wylie\": \"pa ni mdza' bo'i bu gling gzhan du 'gro bar 'dod pa mas brtse bas rkang pa dag la, 'jus nas bzlog pa nam la khro ba drag pos mgo bo la rkang pa brgyab nas gzings la zhon pa nas ji srid gzings zhig nas rgya mtsho'i 'grom na nyi tshe ba'i dmyal ba yod par 'dis ma la ma gus pa byas pa'i las zad pas sdod cig 'khor lo 'di khyod kyi spyi bor bskor bar 'gyur ro zhes pa'i sgra bar snang las thos nas gang su'ang rung ba 'gro ba 'di na ma la mi gus par byas pa rnams dang byed pa rnams dang byed par 'gyur pa de rnams kyi las de bdag kho na la rnam par smin par gyur cigs yang rnam pa 'di lta bu'i sdug bsngal gyi skal ba can du ma gyur cig ces shugs drag pos smon pa la gnas pa\", \"english\": \"A person who relies on the Wish in the form of a prayer can rip out, from its roots, the obstacle created by the deed they've committed. There are a number of references we could use to demonstrate this fact. The young man named \\\"Child of the Friend\\\" wanted to travel across the sea to another land; his mother, for sheer love of the boy, would kneel and grasp him by his two legs, and was thus able to stop him. There came a point though where he was seized by violent anger at her, and he hit her on the head with his leg. Then he went to sea, but his ship went down in a storm. There was a near-hell at the edge of the ocean, and here the karma seed from disrespecting his mother was awakened. He heard a voice from the sky that said \\\"Kneel here, for the crown of knives is about to spin down upon your head.\\\" And he stood, making to himself a fervent prayer: May the karma of all those who have ever disrespected their mother in the past; or who are disrespecting their mother at this moment; or who may ever in the future disrespect their mother all ripen upon me alone, at this very moment. May no one else ever suffer the pain that I am about to undergo.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 0.985}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34900,
        "course": "P7",
        "seq": 11,
        "acip": "YAN LAG BCU GNYIS PO THAMS CAD KYANG SEMS GCIG LA BRTEN PAR GSUNGS PA LA, BRTEN NAS TZAM GYI SGRAS SEMS GTZO BOR GYUR PAR BSTAN TE BSHAD PA SNGA MA, DGAG PHYOGS DANG PHYI MA SGRUB PHYOGS NAS YIN NO DE'I PHYIR 'GRO BA RAB TU 'JUG PA, LA SEMS NYID GTZO BO'I RGYU YIN GYI SEMS LAS GZHAN PA NI GTZO BO'I RGYU MIN PAS MDO LAS SEMS TZAM ZHIG GTZO BOR BZHAG GI GZUGS NI MA YIN NO GAL TE GZUGS YOD PAR 'DOD MOD KYI STE KYANG GZUGS DE LA NI SEMS BZHIN DU 'GRO BA'I BYED PA PO NYID NI YOD PA MA YIN NO DES NA SEMS LAS GZHAN PA'I BYED PA PO BZLOG PA STE BKAG GI PHYI ROL GYI GZUGS NI BKAG PA MA YIN NO",
        "wylie": "yan lag bcu gnyis po thams cad kyang sems gcig la brten par gsungs pa la, brten nas tsam gyi sgras sems gtso bor gyur par bstan te bshad pa snga ma, dgag phyogs dang phyi ma sgrub phyogs nas yin no de'i phyir 'gro ba rab tu 'jug pa, la sems nyid gtso bo'i rgyu yin gyi sems las gzhan pa ni gtso bo'i rgyu min pas mdo las sems tsam zhig gtso bor bzhag gi gzugs ni ma yin no gal te gzugs yod par 'dod mod kyi ste kyang gzugs de la ni sems bzhin du 'gro ba'i byed pa po nyid ni yod pa ma yin no des na sems las gzhan pa'i byed pa po bzlog pa ste bkag gi phyi rol gyi gzugs ni bkag pa ma yin no",
        "english": "It is stated in scripture as well that each and every one of the twelve links in the chain of interdependence depends on a single thing: the mind. These references convey yet another meaning of the word \"only\" in the expression \"mind-only.\" Here the point is that mind is the main thing. The former scriptural references are putting their point in a negative way, and the latter references are putting their point in a positive way. Thus we can see that it's the mind which is the one single principal cause that sets all life into motion. The principal cause is not something other than the mind. Therefore when the sutras speak of \"mind only,\" they are making the point that mind is the main thing, and not matter. Although we do of course admit that physical matter exists, this matter is not the one prime creator of sentient beings in the way that mind is. Therefore all these scriptural references are denying that the opposite of the mind, something other than the mind, could be the creator. They are not though saying that there are no outside physical objects at all. The Asian Classics Institute Reading Two: How Emptiness Allows Karma to Work, According to the Middle-Way School",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"YAN LAG BCU GNYIS PO THAMS CAD KYANG SEMS GCIG LA BRTEN PAR GSUNGS PA LA, BRTEN NAS TZAM GYI SGRAS SEMS GTZO BOR GYUR PAR BSTAN TE BSHAD PA SNGA MA, DGAG PHYOGS DANG PHYI MA SGRUB PHYOGS NAS YIN NO DE'I PHYIR 'GRO BA RAB TU 'JUG PA, LA SEMS NYID GTZO BO'I RGYU YIN GYI SEMS LAS GZHAN PA NI GTZO BO'I RGYU MIN PAS MDO LAS SEMS TZAM ZHIG GTZO BOR BZHAG GI GZUGS NI MA YIN NO GAL TE GZUGS YOD PAR 'DOD MOD KYI STE KYANG GZUGS DE LA NI SEMS BZHIN DU 'GRO BA'I BYED PA PO NYID NI YOD PA MA YIN NO DES NA SEMS LAS GZHAN PA'I BYED PA PO BZLOG PA STE BKAG GI PHYI ROL GYI GZUGS NI BKAG PA MA YIN NO\", \"wylie\": \"yan lag bcu gnyis po thams cad kyang sems gcig la brten par gsungs pa la, brten nas tsam gyi sgras sems gtso bor gyur par bstan te bshad pa snga ma, dgag phyogs dang phyi ma sgrub phyogs nas yin no de'i phyir 'gro ba rab tu 'jug pa, la sems nyid gtso bo'i rgyu yin gyi sems las gzhan pa ni gtso bo'i rgyu min pas mdo las sems tsam zhig gtso bor bzhag gi gzugs ni ma yin no gal te gzugs yod par 'dod mod kyi ste kyang gzugs de la ni sems bzhin du 'gro ba'i byed pa po nyid ni yod pa ma yin no des na sems las gzhan pa'i byed pa po bzlog pa ste bkag gi phyi rol gyi gzugs ni bkag pa ma yin no\", \"english\": \"It is stated in scripture as well that each and every one of the twelve links in the chain of interdependence depends on a single thing: the mind. These references convey yet another meaning of the word \\\"only\\\" in the expression \\\"mind-only.\\\" Here the point is that mind is the main thing. The former scriptural references are putting their point in a negative way, and the latter references are putting their point in a positive way. Thus we can see that it's the mind which is the one single principal cause that sets all life into motion. The principal cause is not something other than the mind. Therefore when the sutras speak of \\\"mind only,\\\" they are making the point that mind is the main thing, and not matter. Although we do of course admit that physical matter exists, this matter is not the one prime creator of sentient beings in the way that mind is. Therefore all these scriptural references are denying that the opposite of the mind, something other than the mind, could be the creator. They are not though saying that there are no outside physical objects at all. The Asian Classics Institute Reading Two: How Emptiness Allows Karma to Work, According to the Middle-Way School\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 41832,
        "course": "SUBJ",
        "seq": 15,
        "acip": "BZLOG PA",
        "wylie": "bzlog pa",
        "english": "Practices For Preventing Harmful Spirits",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \"BZLOG PA\", \"wylie\": \"bzlog pa\", \"english\": \"Practices For Preventing Harmful Spirits\", \"course\": \"SUBJ\", \"source_wave\": \"r6-subjects-2026-08\", \"catalog_source\": \"ACIP Release 6 web subject browser (aciprelease.org mirror, acip-release6-wylie.zip)\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 36890,
        "course": "SVN",
        "seq": 430,
        "acip": "DE MED PAR RTOGS PA LAS 'KHOR BA'I RTZA BA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR, BDEN ZHEN DGAG BYA YIN GYI, BDEN PA DGAG BYA MIN TE, YE MED YIN PA'I PHYIR CES SMRA BA",
        "wylie": "de med par rtogs pa las 'khor ba'i rtsa ba bzlog pa'i phyir, bden zhen dgag bya yin gyi, bden pa dgag bya min te, ye med yin pa'i phyir ces smra ba",
        "english": "Now some people have said it like this- When we talk about something that we have to put a stop to, we're talking about the tendency of grasping to things as real-because realizing that it doesn't exist is described as the thing that prevents the very root of the cycle of pain. It isn't though that things being real could ever be what we have to put a stop to, since that never existed in the first place.",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \"DE MED PAR RTOGS PA LAS 'KHOR BA'I RTZA BA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR, BDEN ZHEN DGAG BYA YIN GYI, BDEN PA DGAG BYA MIN TE, YE MED YIN PA'I PHYIR CES SMRA BA\", \"wylie\": \"de med par rtogs pa las 'khor ba'i rtsa ba bzlog pa'i phyir, bden zhen dgag bya yin gyi, bden pa dgag bya min te, ye med yin pa'i phyir ces smra ba\", \"english\": \"Now some people have said it like this- When we talk about something that we have to put a stop to, we're talking about the tendency of grasping to things as real-because realizing that it doesn't exist is described as the thing that prevents the very root of the cycle of pain. It isn't though that things being real could ever be what we have to put a stop to, since that never existed in the first place.\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 37500,
        "course": "SVN",
        "seq": 1040,
        "acip": ",RE ZHIG NYES KUN 'BYUNG BA'I GNAS, ,MED NYID RNAM PAR BZLOG ZIN GYIS, ,RIGS PA'I GANG GIS YOD NYID DANG, ,BZLOG PAR 'GYUR BA MNYAN PAR GYIS,",
        "wylie": "re zhig nyes kun 'byung ba'i gnas, ,med nyid rnam par bzlog zin gyis, ,rigs pa'i gang gis yod nyid dang, ,bzlog par 'gyur ba mnyan par gyis",
        "english": "To start with, the idea That nothing at all exists- A concept which is indeed The source of every fault- Has already been disproven. Then listen to the reasoning, We use to deny as well That things could be as they seem.",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \",RE ZHIG NYES KUN 'BYUNG BA'I GNAS, ,MED NYID RNAM PAR BZLOG ZIN GYIS, ,RIGS PA'I GANG GIS YOD NYID DANG, ,BZLOG PAR 'GYUR BA MNYAN PAR GYIS,\", \"wylie\": \"re zhig nyes kun 'byung ba'i gnas, ,med nyid rnam par bzlog zin gyis, ,rigs pa'i gang gis yod nyid dang, ,bzlog par 'gyur ba mnyan par gyis\", \"english\": \"To start with, the idea That nothing at all exists- A concept which is indeed The source of every fault- Has already been disproven. Then listen to the reasoning, We use to deny as well That things could be as they seem.\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 37513,
        "course": "SVN",
        "seq": 1053,
        "acip": ",SNGON SKYE BA NI JI LTAR NA, ,PHYI NAS SLAR YANG BZLOG PAR 'GYUR, ,SNGON DANG PHYI MA'I MTHA' BRAL BA, ,'GRO BA SGYU MA BZHIN DU SNANG,",
        "wylie": "sngon skye ba ni ji ltar na, ,phyi nas slar yang bzlog par 'gyur, ,sngon dang phyi ma'i mtha' bral ba, ,'gro ba sgyu ma bzhin du snang",
        "english": "If things had, Earlier on, Come to a start, Then how could we say That, later on, They could come to a stop? Beings are without Any first beginning Or final ending; They make their appearance Like a magical illusion.",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \",SNGON SKYE BA NI JI LTAR NA, ,PHYI NAS SLAR YANG BZLOG PAR 'GYUR, ,SNGON DANG PHYI MA'I MTHA' BRAL BA, ,'GRO BA SGYU MA BZHIN DU SNANG,\", \"wylie\": \"sngon skye ba ni ji ltar na, ,phyi nas slar yang bzlog par 'gyur, ,sngon dang phyi ma'i mtha' bral ba, ,'gro ba sgyu ma bzhin du snang\", \"english\": \"If things had, Earlier on, Come to a start, Then how could we say That, later on, They could come to a stop? Beings are without Any first beginning Or final ending; They make their appearance Like a magical illusion.\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 37554,
        "course": "SVN",
        "seq": 1094,
        "acip": ",SDUG SNYAM PA LAS CHAGS PAR 'GYUR, ,DE LAS BZLOG PAS 'DOD CHAGS BRAL, ,SGYU MA'I SKYES BU LTAR DBEN PAR, ,MTHONG NAS MYA NGAN 'DA' BAR 'GYUR,",
        "wylie": "sdug snyam pa las chags par 'gyur, ,de las bzlog pas 'dod chags bral, ,sgyu ma'i skyes bu ltar dben par, ,mthong nas mya ngan 'da' bar 'gyur",
        "english": "Some think to themselves That something is attractive, And then develop attachment. Those who are the opposite Lose desire. Those who see beings As devoid, as though They were illusions, Come to nirvana.",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \",SDUG SNYAM PA LAS CHAGS PAR 'GYUR, ,DE LAS BZLOG PAS 'DOD CHAGS BRAL, ,SGYU MA'I SKYES BU LTAR DBEN PAR, ,MTHONG NAS MYA NGAN 'DA' BAR 'GYUR,\", \"wylie\": \"sdug snyam pa las chags par 'gyur, ,de las bzlog pas 'dod chags bral, ,sgyu ma'i skyes bu ltar dben par, ,mthong nas mya ngan 'da' bar 'gyur\", \"english\": \"Some think to themselves That something is attractive, And then develop attachment. Those who are the opposite Lose desire. Those who see beings As devoid, as though They were illusions, Come to nirvana.\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 37618,
        "course": "SVN",
        "seq": 1158,
        "acip": "DE NYID KYI PHYIR SLOB DPON GYI ZHAL SNGA NAS KYANG, ,GAL TE MNGON SUM LA SOGS PA'I, ,DON GYIS 'GA' ZHIG DMIGS NA NI, ,SGRUB PAR MA BZLOG PAR BYA NA DE, ,MED PHYIR NGA LA KLAN KA MED, ,CES GSUNGS SO,,]",
        "wylie": "de nyid kyi phyir slob dpon gyi zhal snga nas kyang, ,gal te mngon sum la sogs pa'i, ,don gyis 'ga' zhig dmigs na ni, ,sgrub par ma bzlog par bya na de, ,med phyir nga la klan ka med, ,ces gsungs so,,]",
        "english": "And this is why the Master himself has said, I have no debate with you, Whether you seek to prove Or disprove that these things Could be seen, in & of themselves, By a perception that was direct, Or any other kind.",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \"DE NYID KYI PHYIR SLOB DPON GYI ZHAL SNGA NAS KYANG, ,GAL TE MNGON SUM LA SOGS PA'I, ,DON GYIS 'GA' ZHIG DMIGS NA NI, ,SGRUB PAR MA BZLOG PAR BYA NA DE, ,MED PHYIR NGA LA KLAN KA MED, ,CES GSUNGS SO,,]\", \"wylie\": \"de nyid kyi phyir slob dpon gyi zhal snga nas kyang, ,gal te mngon sum la sogs pa'i, ,don gyis 'ga' zhig dmigs na ni, ,sgrub par ma bzlog par bya na de, ,med phyir nga la klan ka med, ,ces gsungs so,,]\", \"english\": \"And this is why the Master himself has said, I have no debate with you, Whether you seek to prove Or disprove that these things Could be seen, in & of themselves, By a perception that was direct, Or any other kind.\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 9273,
        "course": "TCS01",
        "seq": 336,
        "acip": ",STOBS DANG DPUNG GIS BZLOG PA'I SKABS MED CING, ,RDZAS SNGAGS NOR DANG MDZES MAS BSLUR MED PA'I, ,'JIGS RUNG DUS KYI CHAD PA RNAM PA LNGA, ,THOG LTAR 'BAB BO MKHAS PAS BAG YOD STEN,",
        "wylie": "stobs dang dpung gis bzlog pa'i skabs med cing, ,rdzas sngags nor dang mdzes mas bslur med pa'i, ,'jigs rung dus kyi chad pa rnam pa lnga, ,thog ltar 'bab bo mkhas pas bag yod sten",
        "english": "Watch yourself, if your are wise-- they will strike like lightning, the five terrible signs that your time has come. No strength can stop them, no army can stop them; nothing you own no secret mantra no money no beautiful girl cheats death. [Translator's note: \"Five signs\" here seems to refer to the string of hallucinations that follow the death process, as the five elements of the body collapse: a mirage, smoke, sparks like fireflies, a candle flame, and a cloudless sky.]",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS01\", \"acip\": \",STOBS DANG DPUNG GIS BZLOG PA'I SKABS MED CING, ,RDZAS SNGAGS NOR DANG MDZES MAS BSLUR MED PA'I, ,'JIGS RUNG DUS KYI CHAD PA RNAM PA LNGA, ,THOG LTAR 'BAB BO MKHAS PAS BAG YOD STEN,\", \"wylie\": \"stobs dang dpung gis bzlog pa'i skabs med cing, ,rdzas sngags nor dang mdzes mas bslur med pa'i, ,'jigs rung dus kyi chad pa rnam pa lnga, ,thog ltar 'bab bo mkhas pas bag yod sten\", \"english\": \"Watch yourself, if your are wise-- they will strike like lightning, the five terrible signs that your time has come. No strength can stop them, no army can stop them; nothing you own no secret mantra no money no beautiful girl cheats death. [Translator's note: \\\"Five signs\\\" here seems to refer to the string of hallucinations that follow the death process, as the five elements of the body collapse: a mirage, smoke, sparks like fireflies, a candle flame, and a cloudless sky.]\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 10354,
        "course": "TCS04Initiation",
        "seq": 217,
        "acip": "JI SRID DKYIL 'KHOR CHEN PO'I BYA BA RNAMS MA RDZOGS PA DE SRID DU BGEGS RNAM PAR BZLOG PA'I 'PHRIN LAS MDZOD CIG (,CES 'PHRIN LAS BCOL,)",
        "wylie": "ji srid dkyil 'khor chen po'i bya ba rnams ma rdzogs pa de srid du bgegs rnam par bzlog pa'i 'phrin las mdzod cig (,ces 'phrin las bcol,)",
        "english": "(Urge the Angels to Their holy deeds by saying:) Undertake please Your holy deeds, Of warding off all obstacles, Until our actions Here in the great mandala Reach their successful conclusion.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS04Initiation\", \"acip\": \"JI SRID DKYIL 'KHOR CHEN PO'I BYA BA RNAMS MA RDZOGS PA DE SRID DU BGEGS RNAM PAR BZLOG PA'I 'PHRIN LAS MDZOD CIG (,CES 'PHRIN LAS BCOL,)\", \"wylie\": \"ji srid dkyil 'khor chen po'i bya ba rnams ma rdzogs pa de srid du bgegs rnam par bzlog pa'i 'phrin las mdzod cig (,ces 'phrin las bcol,)\", \"english\": \"(Urge the Angels to Their holy deeds by saying:) Undertake please Your holy deeds, Of warding off all obstacles, Until our actions Here in the great mandala Reach their successful conclusion.\", \"repair_wave\": \"tcs-v25\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 10888,
        "course": "TCS04Initiation",
        "seq": 751,
        "acip": "BSRUNG BA'I DAM TSIG DANG, BZA' BA'I DAM TSIG DANG, GTOR MA'I CHO GA DANG, BSRUNG BA DANG, PHYIR BZLOG PA DANG, DKYIL 'KHOR BSGRUB PA DANG, DER SLOB MA BZHUGS PA'I MTSAN NYID NI SLOB DPON GYI LAS SU BYA BA'I RIM PA YIN NO,",
        "wylie": "bsrung ba'i dam tshig dang, bza' ba'i dam tshig dang, gtor ma'i cho ga dang, bsrung ba dang, phyir bzlog pa dang, dkyil 'khor bsgrub pa dang, der slob ma bzhugs pa'i mtshan nyid ni slob dpon gyi las su bya ba'i rim pa yin no",
        "english": "Here are the various works of a Diamond Master, which you must perform; these are your qualification: the Pledge to Protect; the Pledge to Partake; the Offering of Tormas; the Rites of Protection; the Rites of Defense; Building a Secret World; and Bringing Disiples into the Secret World.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS04Initiation\", \"acip\": \"BSRUNG BA'I DAM TSIG DANG, BZA' BA'I DAM TSIG DANG, GTOR MA'I CHO GA DANG, BSRUNG BA DANG, PHYIR BZLOG PA DANG, DKYIL 'KHOR BSGRUB PA DANG, DER SLOB MA BZHUGS PA'I MTSAN NYID NI SLOB DPON GYI LAS SU BYA BA'I RIM PA YIN NO,\", \"wylie\": \"bsrung ba'i dam tshig dang, bza' ba'i dam tshig dang, gtor ma'i cho ga dang, bsrung ba dang, phyir bzlog pa dang, dkyil 'khor bsgrub pa dang, der slob ma bzhugs pa'i mtshan nyid ni slob dpon gyi las su bya ba'i rim pa yin no\", \"english\": \"Here are the various works of a Diamond Master, which you must perform; these are your qualification: the Pledge to Protect; the Pledge to Partake; the Offering of Tormas; the Rites of Protection; the Rites of Defense; Building a Secret World; and Bringing Disiples into the Secret World.\", \"repair_wave\": \"tcs-v25\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 11721,
        "course": "TCS05",
        "seq": 42,
        "acip": "BSTEN BYA'I SLOB MA JI 'DRA BA YANG SGYU 'PHRUL DRA BA'I RIM PAR PHYE BA DANG PO LAS, DE DGE DANG SGOM LA DGA' BA DANG , ,RTAG TU BLA MA LA GUS DANG , ,RGYUN DU LHA RNAMS MCHOD BYED PA'I, ,YON TAN LDAN NA SLOB MA YIN, ,ZHES GSUNGS PA LTAR SHES PAR BYA LA SPANG BYA'I SLOB MA NI, SNYING RJE MED CING KHRO LA GDUG ,CES SOGS KYI SKYON DANG LDAN PA DANG 'DIR BSHAD PA'I YON TAN CAN LAS BZLOG PA RNAMS SU SHES PAR BYA'O,",
        "wylie": "bsten bya'i slob ma ji 'dra ba yang sgyu 'phrul dra ba'i rim par phye ba dang po las, de dge dang sgom la dga' ba dang , ,rtag tu bla ma la gus dang , ,rgyun du lha rnams mchod byed pa'i, ,yon tan ldan na slob ma yin, ,zhes gsungs pa ltar shes par bya la spang bya'i slob ma ni, snying rje med cing khro la gdug ,ces sogs kyi skyon dang ldan pa dang 'dir bshad pa'i yon tan can las bzlog pa rnams su shes par bya'o",
        "english": "What kind of qualities then should the prospective disciple possess?  The first of The Steps of the Web of Illusion answers, They should love good deeds, And enjoy meditation; They should always honor their Lama, And constantly be making offerings To the holy Angels; One who possesses these qualities Is one we call a disciple. We must acquaint ourselves with these good qualities, as well as the negative ones to be avoided in a disciple.  These can be understood as the opposite of the above: a person who has faults such as those described in the lines that say, They are wrong: They have no compassion, And are prone to anger. *** B. The Root Downfalls, Part I The Fourteen Root Downfalls The second part of the first class of the course covers the first of the fourteen root downfalls of the secret way, which is criticizing or disparaging our Lama.  We begin with general comments on the downfalls by Je Tsongkapa, from his Golden Harvest:",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS05\", \"acip\": \"BSTEN BYA'I SLOB MA JI 'DRA BA YANG SGYU 'PHRUL DRA BA'I RIM PAR PHYE BA DANG PO LAS, DE DGE DANG SGOM LA DGA' BA DANG , ,RTAG TU BLA MA LA GUS DANG , ,RGYUN DU LHA RNAMS MCHOD BYED PA'I, ,YON TAN LDAN NA SLOB MA YIN, ,ZHES GSUNGS PA LTAR SHES PAR BYA LA SPANG BYA'I SLOB MA NI, SNYING RJE MED CING KHRO LA GDUG ,CES SOGS KYI SKYON DANG LDAN PA DANG 'DIR BSHAD PA'I YON TAN CAN LAS BZLOG PA RNAMS SU SHES PAR BYA'O,\", \"wylie\": \"bsten bya'i slob ma ji 'dra ba yang sgyu 'phrul dra ba'i rim par phye ba dang po las, de dge dang sgom la dga' ba dang , ,rtag tu bla ma la gus dang , ,rgyun du lha rnams mchod byed pa'i, ,yon tan ldan na slob ma yin, ,zhes gsungs pa ltar shes par bya la spang bya'i slob ma ni, snying rje med cing khro la gdug ,ces sogs kyi skyon dang ldan pa dang 'dir bshad pa'i yon tan can las bzlog pa rnams su shes par bya'o\", \"english\": \"What kind of qualities then should the prospective disciple possess?  The first of The Steps of the Web of Illusion answers, They should love good deeds, And enjoy meditation; They should always honor their Lama, And constantly be making offerings To the holy Angels; One who possesses these qualities Is one we call a disciple. We must acquaint ourselves with these good qualities, as well as the negative ones to be avoided in a disciple.  These can be understood as the opposite of the above: a person who has faults such as those described in the lines that say, They are wrong: They have no compassion, And are prone to anger. *** B. The Root Downfalls, Part I The Fourteen Root Downfalls The second part of the first class of the course covers the first of the fourteen root downfalls of the secret way, which is criticizing or disparaging our Lama.  We begin with general comments on the downfalls by Je Tsongkapa, from his Golden Harvest:\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 11768,
        "course": "TCS05",
        "seq": 89,
        "acip": "SAm PU tA'I BRTAG PA GNYIS PA'I RAB BYED DANG PO LAS, ,GANG DANG GANG ZHIG SDUG PA'I RDZAS, ,RE BA MED PA'I SEMS KYIS NI, ,DE DANG DE NYID BLA MAR DBUL, ,BLA MA'ANG SNYING RJE LDAN PAS NI, ,SLOB MA'I ZHEN PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR, ,DE LA PHAN PA'I BSAM PAS GZUNG , ,ZHES GSUNGS SO,",
        "wylie": "saM pu Ta'i brtag pa gnyis pa'i rab byed dang po las, ,gang dang gang zhig sdug pa'i rdzas, ,re ba med pa'i sems kyis ni, ,de dang de nyid bla mar dbul, ,bla ma'ang snying rje ldan pas ni, ,slob ma'i zhen pa bzlog pa'i phyir, ,de la phan pa'i bsam pas gzung , ,zhes gsungs so",
        "english": "The first section of the second part of the secret book called Samputa says, Abandoning any hope of being acknowledged, Take any little beautiful thing you find And present it into your Lama's hands. The Lama too, out of sheer compassion, Should accept the gift, thinking that this Will be of benefit for the disciple, And help them prevent attachment. ***",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS05\", \"acip\": \"SAm PU tA'I BRTAG PA GNYIS PA'I RAB BYED DANG PO LAS, ,GANG DANG GANG ZHIG SDUG PA'I RDZAS, ,RE BA MED PA'I SEMS KYIS NI, ,DE DANG DE NYID BLA MAR DBUL, ,BLA MA'ANG SNYING RJE LDAN PAS NI, ,SLOB MA'I ZHEN PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR, ,DE LA PHAN PA'I BSAM PAS GZUNG , ,ZHES GSUNGS SO,\", \"wylie\": \"saM pu Ta'i brtag pa gnyis pa'i rab byed dang po las, ,gang dang gang zhig sdug pa'i rdzas, ,re ba med pa'i sems kyis ni, ,de dang de nyid bla mar dbul, ,bla ma'ang snying rje ldan pas ni, ,slob ma'i zhen pa bzlog pa'i phyir, ,de la phan pa'i bsam pas gzung , ,zhes gsungs so\", \"english\": \"The first section of the second part of the secret book called Samputa says, Abandoning any hope of being acknowledged, Take any little beautiful thing you find And present it into your Lama's hands. The Lama too, out of sheer compassion, Should accept the gift, thinking that this Will be of benefit for the disciple, And help them prevent attachment. ***\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 11804,
        "course": "TCS05",
        "seq": 125,
        "acip": ",'O NA SH'AN+TI PAS DGRA NAG 'GREL PAR, SMAD MI BYA ZHES BYA BA NI RTZOD PAS SUN DBYUNG BAR MI BYA STE 'GRO BA DAG NI RANG RANG GI 'DUN PA'I THEG PA TZAM LAS RIM GYIS 'JUS SHING BYANG CHUB PAR 'GYUR GYI BTZAN THABS SU 'DUN PA BZLOG PA NI SDANG BA 'PHEL BA'I RGYU TZAM YIN PAS BZHAG STE, ZHES GRUB MTHA' 'DZIN PA SUN 'BYIN PA LA SMOD PAR GSUNGS PA JI LTAR YIN SNYAM NA, SNGAGS SAM MTSAN NYID KYI THEG PA LA SKYON BKAL NAS BKA' MA YIN NO, ,ZHES SUN PHYUNG BA NA DE DAG GI GRUB MTHA' 'DZIN PA'I YANG 'DUN PA LDOG PA LA BSHAD PAS SKYON MED DO,",
        "wylie": "'o na shAn+ti pas dgra nag 'grel par, smad mi bya zhes bya ba ni rtsod pas sun dbyung bar mi bya ste 'gro ba dag ni rang rang gi 'dun pa'i theg pa tsam las rim gyis 'jus shing byang chub par 'gyur gyi btsan thabs su 'dun pa bzlog pa ni sdang ba 'phel ba'i rgyu tsam yin pas bzhag ste, zhes grub mtha' 'dzin pa sun 'byin pa la smod par gsungs pa ji ltar yin snyam na, sngags sam mtshan nyid kyi theg pa la skyon bkal nas bka' ma yin no, ,zhes sun phyung ba na de dag gi grub mtha' 'dzin pa'i yang 'dun pa ldog pa la bshad pas skyon med do",
        "english": "\"Well then,\" you may ask.  \"How does it fit in here when Shantipa, in his commentary to the Ebony Enemy, describes the act of disparaging here as overthrowing the belief of a particular school?  He says, When the wording of the vow here says that we should \"never disparage their beliefs,\" what it means is that we should not seek to overthrow others\" beliefs through argument.  The point is that beings reach enlightenment by being taken up gradually, without overextending themselves past the particular way which they find comfortable at any given time.  If instead we undertake some drastic step to try to change their minds, it only causes hard feelings--and so for the time being we should let it be.\" And yet there's no problem here.  What Shantipa is referring to here is where we say something negative about either the secret or open ways of Buddhism, overthrowing it in the sense of saying \"This is not the Word of the Buddha,\" and thus changing the person's mind: causing them to give up their desire to learn a particular school of Buddhism.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS05\", \"acip\": \",'O NA SH'AN+TI PAS DGRA NAG 'GREL PAR, SMAD MI BYA ZHES BYA BA NI RTZOD PAS SUN DBYUNG BAR MI BYA STE 'GRO BA DAG NI RANG RANG GI 'DUN PA'I THEG PA TZAM LAS RIM GYIS 'JUS SHING BYANG CHUB PAR 'GYUR GYI BTZAN THABS SU 'DUN PA BZLOG PA NI SDANG BA 'PHEL BA'I RGYU TZAM YIN PAS BZHAG STE, ZHES GRUB MTHA' 'DZIN PA SUN 'BYIN PA LA SMOD PAR GSUNGS PA JI LTAR YIN SNYAM NA, SNGAGS SAM MTSAN NYID KYI THEG PA LA SKYON BKAL NAS BKA' MA YIN NO, ,ZHES SUN PHYUNG BA NA DE DAG GI GRUB MTHA' 'DZIN PA'I YANG 'DUN PA LDOG PA LA BSHAD PAS SKYON MED DO,\", \"wylie\": \"'o na shAn+ti pas dgra nag 'grel par, smad mi bya zhes bya ba ni rtsod pas sun dbyung bar mi bya ste 'gro ba dag ni rang rang gi 'dun pa'i theg pa tsam las rim gyis 'jus shing byang chub par 'gyur gyi btsan thabs su 'dun pa bzlog pa ni sdang ba 'phel ba'i rgyu tsam yin pas bzhag ste, zhes grub mtha' 'dzin pa sun 'byin pa la smod par gsungs pa ji ltar yin snyam na, sngags sam mtshan nyid kyi theg pa la skyon bkal nas bka' ma yin no, ,zhes sun phyung ba na de dag gi grub mtha' 'dzin pa'i yang 'dun pa ldog pa la bshad pas skyon med do\", \"english\": \"\\\"Well then,\\\" you may ask.  \\\"How does it fit in here when Shantipa, in his commentary to the Ebony Enemy, describes the act of disparaging here as overthrowing the belief of a particular school?  He says, When the wording of the vow here says that we should \\\"never disparage their beliefs,\\\" what it means is that we should not seek to overthrow others\\\" beliefs through argument.  The point is that beings reach enlightenment by being taken up gradually, without overextending themselves past the particular way which they find comfortable at any given time.  If instead we undertake some drastic step to try to change their minds, it only causes hard feelings--and so for the time being we should let it be.\\\" And yet there's no problem here.  What Shantipa is referring to here is where we say something negative about either the secret or open ways of Buddhism, overthrowing it in the sense of saying \\\"This is not the Word of the Buddha,\\\" and thus changing the person's mind: causing them to give up their desire to learn a particular school of Buddhism.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 11995,
        "course": "TCS05",
        "seq": 316,
        "acip": "BLA MA'I DRUNG DU GZHAN GYIS PHYAG BYA BA SOGS BZLOG PA,",
        "wylie": "bla ma'i drung du gzhan gyis phyag bya ba sogs bzlog pa",
        "english": "If in our Lama's presence another person attempts to prostrate to us or anything of that nature, then we must stop them from doing so. B. The Mother Vows, Part One Our final group of tantric vows is the Mother Vows.  In this and the final class, we present the entire text of an extremely rare and secret commentary on these vows by the famed Mongolian sage, Kalka Damtsik Dorje (1781-1855).  His work is entitled The Shining Glory of Great Bliss: A Presentation of the Eight Pledges Spoken in the Root Tantra of Highest Bliss (bDe-mchog rtza-rgyud las gsungs pa'i dam-tsig brgyad gyi rnam-bzhag bde-chen dpal-'gyur). As we will see, this text draws large sections verbatim from Je Tsongkapa's own Cow that Produces Every Wish: An Extensive Explanation of the Practice of Realization for the Secret Teaching of the Conqueror, the Glorious Sphere of Highest Bliss (Heruka), found at ACIP digital text S5320. ***",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS05\", \"acip\": \"BLA MA'I DRUNG DU GZHAN GYIS PHYAG BYA BA SOGS BZLOG PA,\", \"wylie\": \"bla ma'i drung du gzhan gyis phyag bya ba sogs bzlog pa\", \"english\": \"If in our Lama's presence another person attempts to prostrate to us or anything of that nature, then we must stop them from doing so. B. The Mother Vows, Part One Our final group of tantric vows is the Mother Vows.  In this and the final class, we present the entire text of an extremely rare and secret commentary on these vows by the famed Mongolian sage, Kalka Damtsik Dorje (1781-1855).  His work is entitled The Shining Glory of Great Bliss: A Presentation of the Eight Pledges Spoken in the Root Tantra of Highest Bliss (bDe-mchog rtza-rgyud las gsungs pa'i dam-tsig brgyad gyi rnam-bzhag bde-chen dpal-'gyur). As we will see, this text draws large sections verbatim from Je Tsongkapa's own Cow that Produces Every Wish: An Extensive Explanation of the Practice of Realization for the Secret Teaching of the Conqueror, the Glorious Sphere of Highest Bliss (Heruka), found at ACIP digital text S5320. ***\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 12018,
        "course": "TCS05",
        "seq": 339,
        "acip": "YANG NA KH'A GDONG MA LA SOGS PA BRGYAD PO 'DI GUS PA DANG DAD PA 'PHEL BAR BYED PA YIN LA, DE YANG DNGOS GRUB RNAMS KYI RGYU YIN NO ZHES PA STE, DE YANG SNGAR BSHAD PA LTAR BLA MA DANG 'DI YI RNAL 'BYOR PA LA RTAG PAR TE RGYUN DU GUS PA NI DNGOS GRUB RNAMS KYI RGYU YIN LA, GUS PA DANG DAD PA GONG NAS GONG DU 'PHEL BAR BYED PA LA, ,BAR DU GCOD PA'I BGEGS NI SGO MTSAMS MAS BZLOG PA YIN PAS, SGO MTSAMS MA RNAMS DE DAG 'PHEL BA'I RGYU YIN NO,, ZHES PA'O,,",
        "wylie": "yang na khA gdong ma la sogs pa brgyad po 'di gus pa dang dad pa 'phel bar byed pa yin la, de yang dngos grub rnams kyi rgyu yin no zhes pa ste, de yang sngar bshad pa ltar bla ma dang 'di yi rnal 'byor pa la rtag par te rgyun du gus pa ni dngos grub rnams kyi rgyu yin la, gus pa dang dad pa gong nas gong du 'phel bar byed pa la, ,bar du gcod pa'i bgegs ni sgo mtshams mas bzlog pa yin pas, sgo mtshams ma rnams de dag 'phel ba'i rgyu yin no,, zhes pa'o",
        "english": "You can also read the lines as saying that Crowface and the rest of the eight Guardians cause your respect and devotion to come to increase; and that these emotions are the cause of every spiritual attainment.  The point here is that, as we noted before, the cause of every spiritual attainment is to always (meaning continually) feel respect for one's Lama and for deep practitioners who follow this secret teaching.  And the Guardians of the Gates can help us stop any hindrances which might prevent this respect and faith from growing ever higher; as such, they are causes that help these emotions increase.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS05\", \"acip\": \"YANG NA KH'A GDONG MA LA SOGS PA BRGYAD PO 'DI GUS PA DANG DAD PA 'PHEL BAR BYED PA YIN LA, DE YANG DNGOS GRUB RNAMS KYI RGYU YIN NO ZHES PA STE, DE YANG SNGAR BSHAD PA LTAR BLA MA DANG 'DI YI RNAL 'BYOR PA LA RTAG PAR TE RGYUN DU GUS PA NI DNGOS GRUB RNAMS KYI RGYU YIN LA, GUS PA DANG DAD PA GONG NAS GONG DU 'PHEL BAR BYED PA LA, ,BAR DU GCOD PA'I BGEGS NI SGO MTSAMS MAS BZLOG PA YIN PAS, SGO MTSAMS MA RNAMS DE DAG 'PHEL BA'I RGYU YIN NO,, ZHES PA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"yang na khA gdong ma la sogs pa brgyad po 'di gus pa dang dad pa 'phel bar byed pa yin la, de yang dngos grub rnams kyi rgyu yin no zhes pa ste, de yang sngar bshad pa ltar bla ma dang 'di yi rnal 'byor pa la rtag par te rgyun du gus pa ni dngos grub rnams kyi rgyu yin la, gus pa dang dad pa gong nas gong du 'phel bar byed pa la, ,bar du gcod pa'i bgegs ni sgo mtshams mas bzlog pa yin pas, sgo mtshams ma rnams de dag 'phel ba'i rgyu yin no,, zhes pa'o\", \"english\": \"You can also read the lines as saying that Crowface and the rest of the eight Guardians cause your respect and devotion to come to increase; and that these emotions are the cause of every spiritual attainment.  The point here is that, as we noted before, the cause of every spiritual attainment is to always (meaning continually) feel respect for one's Lama and for deep practitioners who follow this secret teaching.  And the Guardians of the Gates can help us stop any hindrances which might prevent this respect and faith from growing ever higher; as such, they are causes that help these emotions increase.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 12058,
        "course": "TCS05",
        "seq": 379,
        "acip": "RANG GI KUN SPYOD TSUL DANG MTHUN PA LA BRTZON PAS, RANG GI RDO RJE SPUN RANG GI KUN SPYOD DE LAS 'DAS PA NA, DGA' BA'I BSAM PAS THABS KYIS BZLOG PA BYA BA SOGS BLA MA BSTEN TSUL 'DI GNAD CHE ZHING , DNGOS GRUB MA LUS PA'I 'BYUNG GNAS YIN PA LA,",
        "wylie": "rang gi kun spyod tshul dang mthun pa la brtson pas, rang gi rdo rje spun rang gi kun spyod de las 'das pa na, dga' ba'i bsam pas thabs kyis bzlog pa bya ba sogs bla ma bsten tshul 'di gnad che zhing , dngos grub ma lus pa'i 'byung gnas yin pa la",
        "english": "Now suppose that we observe one of our diamond sisters or brothers transgressing this code of behavior in some way.  Assuming that we ourselves are making sincere efforts in this same code, and that our motivation is a joyful one, we must find some skillful means to try to dissuade them from doing what they are doing. *** Je Tsongkapa expands, in His commentary upon the Fifty Verses:",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS05\", \"acip\": \"RANG GI KUN SPYOD TSUL DANG MTHUN PA LA BRTZON PAS, RANG GI RDO RJE SPUN RANG GI KUN SPYOD DE LAS 'DAS PA NA, DGA' BA'I BSAM PAS THABS KYIS BZLOG PA BYA BA SOGS BLA MA BSTEN TSUL 'DI GNAD CHE ZHING , DNGOS GRUB MA LUS PA'I 'BYUNG GNAS YIN PA LA,\", \"wylie\": \"rang gi kun spyod tshul dang mthun pa la brtson pas, rang gi rdo rje spun rang gi kun spyod de las 'das pa na, dga' ba'i bsam pas thabs kyis bzlog pa bya ba sogs bla ma bsten tshul 'di gnad che zhing , dngos grub ma lus pa'i 'byung gnas yin pa la\", \"english\": \"Now suppose that we observe one of our diamond sisters or brothers transgressing this code of behavior in some way.  Assuming that we ourselves are making sincere efforts in this same code, and that our motivation is a joyful one, we must find some skillful means to try to dissuade them from doing what they are doing. *** Je Tsongkapa expands, in His commentary upon the Fifty Verses:\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 13405,
        "course": "TCS07",
        "seq": 619,
        "acip": "GRVA PA GZHAN GYI PHRAG DOG GIS DMAG DRANGS BYUNG BA SPRUL PA'I DMAG GIS PHYIR BZLOG PAS GUS SHING SLOB MAR GYUR, THANG LHA DANG SHAB KYI 'BA' RA NAG PO LA SOGS PA BAR CHAD BYED DU BYUNG BA'I LHA 'DRE RNAMS NYIN MO THOG DANG MTSAN MO TING NGE 'DZIN GYIS ZIL GYIS MNAN ZHING SROG SNGAGS BLANGS TE DAM TSIG LA BZHAG",
        "wylie": "grva pa gzhan gyi phrag dog gis dmag drangs byung ba sprul pa'i dmag gis phyir bzlog pas gus shing slob mar gyur, thang lha dang shab kyi 'ba' ra nag po la sogs pa bar chad byed du byung ba'i lha 'dre rnams nyin mo thog dang mtshan mo ting nge 'dzin gyis zil gyis mnan zhing srog sngags blangs te dam tshig la bzhag",
        "english": "At one point it happened that some other monks, out of envy towards Kyungpo Neljor, actually organized a group of armed men to do Him violence.  He routed the soldiers though by emanating an army of His own, which grained the monks' respect, and they became His disciples. In these days too a great many spirit beings of obstruction made their appearance—demons such as the Spirit of the Plains, and the Ebony Devil of Shab.  When they came in the daytime, Kyungpo Neljor blasted them with bolts of lightning; those who came at night He overwhelmed with the power of His meditation.  Then He used mantras to capture their life-force, and bound them to His service.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS07\", \"acip\": \"GRVA PA GZHAN GYI PHRAG DOG GIS DMAG DRANGS BYUNG BA SPRUL PA'I DMAG GIS PHYIR BZLOG PAS GUS SHING SLOB MAR GYUR, THANG LHA DANG SHAB KYI 'BA' RA NAG PO LA SOGS PA BAR CHAD BYED DU BYUNG BA'I LHA 'DRE RNAMS NYIN MO THOG DANG MTSAN MO TING NGE 'DZIN GYIS ZIL GYIS MNAN ZHING SROG SNGAGS BLANGS TE DAM TSIG LA BZHAG\", \"wylie\": \"grva pa gzhan gyi phrag dog gis dmag drangs byung ba sprul pa'i dmag gis phyir bzlog pas gus shing slob mar gyur, thang lha dang shab kyi 'ba' ra nag po la sogs pa bar chad byed du byung ba'i lha 'dre rnams nyin mo thog dang mtshan mo ting nge 'dzin gyis zil gyis mnan zhing srog sngags blangs te dam tshig la bzhag\", \"english\": \"At one point it happened that some other monks, out of envy towards Kyungpo Neljor, actually organized a group of armed men to do Him violence.  He routed the soldiers though by emanating an army of His own, which grained the monks' respect, and they became His disciples. In these days too a great many spirit beings of obstruction made their appearance—demons such as the Spirit of the Plains, and the Ebony Devil of Shab.  When they came in the daytime, Kyungpo Neljor blasted them with bolts of lightning; those who came at night He overwhelmed with the power of His meditation.  Then He used mantras to capture their life-force, and bound them to His service.\", \"repair_wave\": \"tcs-v25\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 13609,
        "course": "TCS08NaropaSadhana",
        "seq": 6,
        "acip": "DE'I RJES LA SNYING GAR AAm LAS ZLA BA'I DKYIL 'KHOR BSAMS LA, DER AOm AOm AOm SARBBA BUDDHA d'A KI N'I YE BADZRA WARnnA N'I YE BADZRA BEE RO TZA N'I YE H'Um H'Um H'Um PHAt PHAt PHAt SV'A H'A, ZHES BYA BA'I SNGAGS KYI PHRENG BA KHA DOG DMAR PO 'OD ZER SNA TSOGS 'PHRO BA'I LUGS LAS BZLOG PAR BSAM MO,,",
        "wylie": "de'i rjes la snying gar aaM las zla ba'i dkyil 'khor bsams la, der aoM aoM aoM sarbba buddha DA ki nI ye badzra warNNa nI ye badzra bee ro tsa nI ye hUM hUM hUM phaT phaT phaT svA hA, zhes bya ba'i sngags kyi phreng ba kha dog dmar po 'od zer sna tshogs 'phro ba'i lugs las bzlog par bsam mo",
        "english": "Next picture a letter am at your heart, and see it transform into a moon disk.  Upon the disk is a string of secret words: Om om om sarva Buddha Dakiniye, vajra varnaniye, vajra vairochaniye, hung hung hung, phet phet phet, sva ha.  The words are all made of crimson light.  A mass of light rays flies forth from words, and then returns.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS08NaropaSadhana\", \"acip\": \"DE'I RJES LA SNYING GAR AAm LAS ZLA BA'I DKYIL 'KHOR BSAMS LA, DER AOm AOm AOm SARBBA BUDDHA d'A KI N'I YE BADZRA WARnnA N'I YE BADZRA BEE RO TZA N'I YE H'Um H'Um H'Um PHAt PHAt PHAt SV'A H'A, ZHES BYA BA'I SNGAGS KYI PHRENG BA KHA DOG DMAR PO 'OD ZER SNA TSOGS 'PHRO BA'I LUGS LAS BZLOG PAR BSAM MO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i rjes la snying gar aaM las zla ba'i dkyil 'khor bsams la, der aoM aoM aoM sarbba buddha DA ki nI ye badzra warNNa nI ye badzra bee ro tsa nI ye hUM hUM hUM phaT phaT phaT svA hA, zhes bya ba'i sngags kyi phreng ba kha dog dmar po 'od zer sna tshogs 'phro ba'i lugs las bzlog par bsam mo\", \"english\": \"Next picture a letter am at your heart, and see it transform into a moon disk.  Upon the disk is a string of secret words: Om om om sarva Buddha Dakiniye, vajra varnaniye, vajra vairochaniye, hung hung hung, phet phet phet, sva ha.  The words are all made of crimson light.  A mass of light rays flies forth from words, and then returns.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 14228,
        "course": "TCS08ThousandAngels",
        "seq": 17,
        "acip": ",TSE RABS KUN TU RGYAL BA TZONG KHA PAS, ,THEG MCHOG BSHES GNYEN DNGOS SU MDZAD PA'I MTHUS, ,RGYAL BAS BSNGAGS PA'I LAM BZANG DE NYID LAS, ,SKAD CIG TZAM YANG BZLOG PAR MA GYUR CIG",
        "wylie": "tshe rabs kun tu rgyal ba tsong kha pas, ,theg mchog bshes gnyen dngos su mdzad pa'i mthus, ,rgyal bas bsngags pa'i lam bzang de nyid las, ,skad cig tsam yang bzlog par ma gyur cig",
        "english": "tserab kuntu gyalwa tsongkape tekchok she-nyen ngusu dzepay tu gyalwe ngakpay lamsang de-nyi le kechik tsamyang dokpar magyur chik Through all the string of my future lives May Tsongkapa the Victor come to me And serve himself as my spiritual guide In the teachings of the Greater Way.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS08ThousandAngels\", \"acip\": \",TSE RABS KUN TU RGYAL BA TZONG KHA PAS, ,THEG MCHOG BSHES GNYEN DNGOS SU MDZAD PA'I MTHUS, ,RGYAL BAS BSNGAGS PA'I LAM BZANG DE NYID LAS, ,SKAD CIG TZAM YANG BZLOG PAR MA GYUR CIG\", \"wylie\": \"tshe rabs kun tu rgyal ba tsong kha pas, ,theg mchog bshes gnyen dngos su mdzad pa'i mthus, ,rgyal bas bsngags pa'i lam bzang de nyid las, ,skad cig tsam yang bzlog par ma gyur cig\", \"english\": \"tserab kuntu gyalwa tsongkape tekchok she-nyen ngusu dzepay tu gyalwe ngakpay lamsang de-nyi le kechik tsamyang dokpar magyur chik Through all the string of my future lives May Tsongkapa the Victor come to me And serve himself as my spiritual guide In the teachings of the Greater Way.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 15208,
        "course": "TCS10",
        "seq": 409,
        "acip": "DE YANG N'A RO'I GZHUNG CHUNG LAS, ZHES PA'I SNGAGS PHRENG LUGS LAS BZLOG PA BSAM MO, ZHES DANG, LUGS LAS LDOG TSUL YANG DE'I MCHAN KUN SNYING GIS MDZAD PA LAS, GA YON SKOR DU ZHES 'BYUNG ZHING, RTZA RGYUD DANG RJE'I RGYUD 'GREL GNYIS KAR SNGAGS LUGS LDOG SPYI 'GRO LTAR 'BYUNG BA DANG,",
        "wylie": "de yang nA ro'i gzhung chung las, zhes pa'i sngags phreng lugs las bzlog pa bsam mo, zhes dang, lugs las ldog tshul yang de'i mchan kun snying gis mdzad pa las, ga yon skor du zhes 'byung zhing, rtsa rgyud dang rje'i rgyud 'grel gnyis kar sngags lugs ldog spyi 'gro ltar 'byung ba dang",
        "english": "On this point the Short Classics of Naropa says, \"Think of them in reverse, the string of secret words that read this way.\"  The meaning of the phrase \"in reverse\" here is explained in the Annotations of Kunga Nyingpo with the words \"in a circle to the left.\"  In both the secret root text and the commentary to the secret teaching by Tsongkapa the Great, the secret words are explained as going in reverse, in their normal order.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS10\", \"acip\": \"DE YANG N'A RO'I GZHUNG CHUNG LAS, ZHES PA'I SNGAGS PHRENG LUGS LAS BZLOG PA BSAM MO, ZHES DANG, LUGS LAS LDOG TSUL YANG DE'I MCHAN KUN SNYING GIS MDZAD PA LAS, GA YON SKOR DU ZHES 'BYUNG ZHING, RTZA RGYUD DANG RJE'I RGYUD 'GREL GNYIS KAR SNGAGS LUGS LDOG SPYI 'GRO LTAR 'BYUNG BA DANG,\", \"wylie\": \"de yang nA ro'i gzhung chung las, zhes pa'i sngags phreng lugs las bzlog pa bsam mo, zhes dang, lugs las ldog tshul yang de'i mchan kun snying gis mdzad pa las, ga yon skor du zhes 'byung zhing, rtsa rgyud dang rje'i rgyud 'grel gnyis kar sngags lugs ldog spyi 'gro ltar 'byung ba dang\", \"english\": \"On this point the Short Classics of Naropa says, \\\"Think of them in reverse, the string of secret words that read this way.\\\"  The meaning of the phrase \\\"in reverse\\\" here is explained in the Annotations of Kunga Nyingpo with the words \\\"in a circle to the left.\\\"  In both the secret root text and the commentary to the secret teaching by Tsongkapa the Great, the secret words are explained as going in reverse, in their normal order.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 16960,
        "course": "TCS13",
        "seq": 411,
        "acip": "'PHO BA NI, 'CHI LTAS BYUNG MA THAG NAS 'PHO BA BYED DU MI RUNG BAS 'CHI LTAS BYUNG NA 'CHI BA BLU BA'I THABS GANG ZAB LA 'BAD DGOS, 'CHI BLU BYAS KYANG MI BZLOG PA BYUNG NA, ZLA BA DRUG GI GONG ROL NAS 'PHO BA BYA CHOG ,",
        "wylie": "'pho ba ni, 'chi ltas byung ma thag nas 'pho ba byed du mi rung bas 'chi ltas byung na 'chi ba blu ba'i thabs gang zab la 'bad dgos, 'chi blu byas kyang mi bzlog pa byung na, zla ba drug gi gong rol nas 'pho ba bya chog",
        "english": "Here are some notes on the practice of transferring your mind.  Transferring your mind this way is not something you should be doing right after you get a sign that you are going to die.  Therefore when such a sign occurs you should put all your effort into carrying out the deepest practice you can find for ransoming death.  If you do complete one of these practices and are still unable to reverse your impending death, then six months before it comes you are allowed to do the transfer.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS13\", \"acip\": \"'PHO BA NI, 'CHI LTAS BYUNG MA THAG NAS 'PHO BA BYED DU MI RUNG BAS 'CHI LTAS BYUNG NA 'CHI BA BLU BA'I THABS GANG ZAB LA 'BAD DGOS, 'CHI BLU BYAS KYANG MI BZLOG PA BYUNG NA, ZLA BA DRUG GI GONG ROL NAS 'PHO BA BYA CHOG ,\", \"wylie\": \"'pho ba ni, 'chi ltas byung ma thag nas 'pho ba byed du mi rung bas 'chi ltas byung na 'chi ba blu ba'i thabs gang zab la 'bad dgos, 'chi blu byas kyang mi bzlog pa byung na, zla ba drug gi gong rol nas 'pho ba bya chog\", \"english\": \"Here are some notes on the practice of transferring your mind.  Transferring your mind this way is not something you should be doing right after you get a sign that you are going to die.  Therefore when such a sign occurs you should put all your effort into carrying out the deepest practice you can find for ransoming death.  If you do complete one of these practices and are still unable to reverse your impending death, then six months before it comes you are allowed to do the transfer.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 17059,
        "course": "TCS13",
        "seq": 510,
        "acip": "'PHRIN LAS BCOL BA NI, DUM SKYES MA RTEN 'DI LA BRTAN PAR BZHUGS NAS RNAL 'BYOR PA BDAG GI BAR CHAD DANG BGEGS RNAMS BZLOG PA'I 'PHRIN LAS MDZOD CIG ,",
        "wylie": "'phrin las bcol ba ni, dum skyes ma rten 'di la brtan par bzhugs nas rnal 'byor pa bdag gi bar chad dang bgegs rnams bzlog pa'i 'phrin las mdzod cig",
        "english": "(Next we exhort Her to Her holy deeds:) O Lady of Pieces, I ask You to come And remain steadfast Within this sacred object And undertake Your sacred work Of preventing any disturbances Or any kind of obstacles To what I the yogi will do.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS13\", \"acip\": \"'PHRIN LAS BCOL BA NI, DUM SKYES MA RTEN 'DI LA BRTAN PAR BZHUGS NAS RNAL 'BYOR PA BDAG GI BAR CHAD DANG BGEGS RNAMS BZLOG PA'I 'PHRIN LAS MDZOD CIG ,\", \"wylie\": \"'phrin las bcol ba ni, dum skyes ma rten 'di la brtan par bzhugs nas rnal 'byor pa bdag gi bar chad dang bgegs rnams bzlog pa'i 'phrin las mdzod cig\", \"english\": \"(Next we exhort Her to Her holy deeds:) O Lady of Pieces, I ask You to come And remain steadfast Within this sacred object And undertake Your sacred work Of preventing any disturbances Or any kind of obstacles To what I the yogi will do.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 17661,
        "course": "TCS14",
        "seq": 59,
        "acip": "DE'I 'OG TU RANG NYID ZHI BDE'I SEMS BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU, BYAMS PA DANG SNYING RJE DANG DE'I RTZA BA CAN GYI BYANG CHUB KYI SEMS LA RING DU SBYANGS NAS, BCOS MA MA YIN PA'I BYANG CHUB KYI SEMS SBYANG BAR BYA'O,,",
        "wylie": "de'i 'og tu rang nyid zhi bde'i sems bzlog pa'i phyir du, byams pa dang snying rje dang de'i rtsa ba can gyi byang chub kyi sems la ring du sbyangs nas, bcos ma ma yin pa'i byang chub kyi sems sbyang bar bya'o",
        "english": "After this, work to prevent any idea of concentrating only on your own happiness.  This is done by devoting much time to training yourself in thoughts of love, and compassion, and the Wish for enlightenment rooted in these two.  Work finally to bring this Wish to the point where it comes to you in a perfectly natural way, not something you have to try to feel artificially.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS14\", \"acip\": \"DE'I 'OG TU RANG NYID ZHI BDE'I SEMS BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU, BYAMS PA DANG SNYING RJE DANG DE'I RTZA BA CAN GYI BYANG CHUB KYI SEMS LA RING DU SBYANGS NAS, BCOS MA MA YIN PA'I BYANG CHUB KYI SEMS SBYANG BAR BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i 'og tu rang nyid zhi bde'i sems bzlog pa'i phyir du, byams pa dang snying rje dang de'i rtsa ba can gyi byang chub kyi sems la ring du sbyangs nas, bcos ma ma yin pa'i byang chub kyi sems sbyang bar bya'o\", \"english\": \"After this, work to prevent any idea of concentrating only on your own happiness.  This is done by devoting much time to training yourself in thoughts of love, and compassion, and the Wish for enlightenment rooted in these two.  Work finally to bring this Wish to the point where it comes to you in a perfectly natural way, not something you have to try to feel artificially.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 17814,
        "course": "TCS14",
        "seq": 212,
        "acip": "DE'I PHYIR RDO RJE GUR LAS, BDAG TU 'DZIN PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR, ,STONG PA RGYAL BA RNAMS KYIS GSUNGS, ,ZHES GANG ZAG DANG CHOS KYI BDAG TU 'DZIN PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU, SANGS RGYAS KYIS STONG PA NYID KYI LTA BA GTAN LA 'BEBS PA GSUNGS SO,,",
        "wylie": "de'i phyir rdo rje gur las, bdag tu 'dzin pa bzlog pa'i phyir, ,stong pa rgyal ba rnams kyis gsungs, ,zhes gang zag dang chos kyi bdag tu 'dzin pa bzlog pa'i phyir du, sangs rgyas kyis stong pa nyid kyi lta ba gtan la 'bebs pa gsungs so",
        "english": "This in fact is why The Canopy of Diamond says, The Victors spoke of emptiness To stop our tendency To grasp to some self-nature. The point here is that the Enlightened Ones have given Their presentation on the worldview of emptiness in order to help us stop our tendency of grasping to some self-nature of both the person, and the things that make a person up.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS14\", \"acip\": \"DE'I PHYIR RDO RJE GUR LAS, BDAG TU 'DZIN PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR, ,STONG PA RGYAL BA RNAMS KYIS GSUNGS, ,ZHES GANG ZAG DANG CHOS KYI BDAG TU 'DZIN PA BZLOG PA'I PHYIR DU, SANGS RGYAS KYIS STONG PA NYID KYI LTA BA GTAN LA 'BEBS PA GSUNGS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i phyir rdo rje gur las, bdag tu 'dzin pa bzlog pa'i phyir, ,stong pa rgyal ba rnams kyis gsungs, ,zhes gang zag dang chos kyi bdag tu 'dzin pa bzlog pa'i phyir du, sangs rgyas kyis stong pa nyid kyi lta ba gtan la 'bebs pa gsungs so\", \"english\": \"This in fact is why The Canopy of Diamond says, The Victors spoke of emptiness To stop our tendency To grasp to some self-nature. The point here is that the Enlightened Ones have given Their presentation on the worldview of emptiness in order to help us stop our tendency of grasping to some self-nature of both the person, and the things that make a person up.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 18870,
        "course": "TCS16KaliWang",
        "seq": 89,
        "acip": ",DE LA SOGS PA'I GDUG SEMS RNAMS, ,THAMS CAD SDANG BA'I DGRA LA BHYO, ,THAMS CAD GNOD PA'I BGEGS LA BHYO, (,ZHES KYIS BZLOG PA BYA,)",
        "wylie": "de la sogs pa'i gdug sems rnams, ,thams cad sdang ba'i dgra la bhyo, ,thams cad gnod pa'i bgegs la bhyo, (,zhes kyis bzlog pa bya,)",
        "english": "Bhyo to these and every other Evil force; Bhyo to these enemies who detest All of living kind; Bhyo to these obstructing spirits Who harm all living kind. With these words, we have performed the rite of stopping evil forces. The Mantras of the Goddesses",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS16KaliWang\", \"acip\": \",DE LA SOGS PA'I GDUG SEMS RNAMS, ,THAMS CAD SDANG BA'I DGRA LA BHYO, ,THAMS CAD GNOD PA'I BGEGS LA BHYO, (,ZHES KYIS BZLOG PA BYA,)\", \"wylie\": \"de la sogs pa'i gdug sems rnams, ,thams cad sdang ba'i dgra la bhyo, ,thams cad gnod pa'i bgegs la bhyo, (,zhes kyis bzlog pa bya,)\", \"english\": \"Bhyo to these and every other Evil force; Bhyo to these enemies who detest All of living kind; Bhyo to these obstructing spirits Who harm all living kind. With these words, we have performed the rite of stopping evil forces. The Mantras of the Goddesses\", \"repair_wave\": \"tcs2-v25-1\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 20186,
        "course": "TCS17",
        "seq": 324,
        "acip": "LUNG GNYIS PAS THABS SHES KHYAD PAR CAN GYIS ZIN PA'I NYON MONGS KYIS LOG PAR LTUNG BA NGAN 'GROR 'KHRID MI NUS PAR BSTAN PAS, THABS MI MKHAS PA LA MTHO RIS KYI GEGS BYED PA'I NYON MONGS KYIS, DE LAS BZLOG PA LA GEGS BYED MI NUS PAR BSTAN TO,,",
        "wylie": "lung gnyis pas thabs shes khyad par can gyis zin pa'i nyon mongs kyis log par ltung ba ngan 'gror 'khrid mi nus par bstan pas, thabs mi mkhas pa la mtho ris kyi gegs byed pa'i nyon mongs kyis, de las bzlog pa la gegs byed mi nus par bstan to",
        "english": "The second part of the same citation is meant to say that a mental affliction which is imbued with an exceptional form of method and wisdom could never lead us to \"fall wrong,\" meaning to take a birth in the realms of misery.  The point is that a mental affliction which would block someone who had no ability for unexpected spiritual means from reaching the higher realms could never block someone who was the opposite.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS17\", \"acip\": \"LUNG GNYIS PAS THABS SHES KHYAD PAR CAN GYIS ZIN PA'I NYON MONGS KYIS LOG PAR LTUNG BA NGAN 'GROR 'KHRID MI NUS PAR BSTAN PAS, THABS MI MKHAS PA LA MTHO RIS KYI GEGS BYED PA'I NYON MONGS KYIS, DE LAS BZLOG PA LA GEGS BYED MI NUS PAR BSTAN TO,,\", \"wylie\": \"lung gnyis pas thabs shes khyad par can gyis zin pa'i nyon mongs kyis log par ltung ba ngan 'gror 'khrid mi nus par bstan pas, thabs mi mkhas pa la mtho ris kyi gegs byed pa'i nyon mongs kyis, de las bzlog pa la gegs byed mi nus par bstan to\", \"english\": \"The second part of the same citation is meant to say that a mental affliction which is imbued with an exceptional form of method and wisdom could never lead us to \\\"fall wrong,\\\" meaning to take a birth in the realms of misery.  The point is that a mental affliction which would block someone who had no ability for unexpected spiritual means from reaching the higher realms could never block someone who was the opposite.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 20428,
        "course": "TCS17",
        "seq": 566,
        "acip": "DE NAS KUN TU BZANG PO'I SPYOD PAS SLOB DPON RANG NYID LA MI MOS PA'I 'GRO BA RNAMS KYI MA DAD PA BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I CHED DANG, MA 'ONGS PA'I SEMS CAN DMAN PA RNAMS KYI DON DU THUGS RJE CHEN PO'I SPRUL PA SPYAN DRANGS PA'I DGOS PA YONGS SU BSGRUB PA'I SLAD DANG, AOO dE BI SHA'I YUL NA GNAS PA'I SKYE BO RNAMS RDO RJE THEG PA LA GZUD PAR DGONGS PA BCAS KYI DON DU GRONG KHYER DU 'BYON PAR BZHED DE SPRUL PA'I BU DANG PU MO GNYIS SHIG KYANG SPRUL TO,,",
        "wylie": "de nas kun tu bzang po'i spyod pas slob dpon rang nyid la mi mos pa'i 'gro ba rnams kyi ma dad pa bzlog par bya ba'i ched dang, ma 'ongs pa'i sems can dman pa rnams kyi don du thugs rje chen po'i sprul pa spyan drangs pa'i dgos pa yongs su bsgrub pa'i slad dang, aoo De bi sha'i yul na gnas pa'i skye bo rnams rdo rje theg pa la gzud par dgongs pa bcas kyi don du grong khyer du 'byon par bzhed de sprul pa'i bu dang pu mo gnyis shig kyang sprul to",
        "english": "And then the Master decided to return to the city, for three reasons.  First of all, He wanted to put a stop to the lack of faith that people were feeling towards Him, by showing them the pureness of His actions.  He further wanted to accomplish the purpose for which the emanation of the Compassionate One had been invited, in order to help lower kinds of living beings in the future.  And then thirdly He had the idea that He could get the people of the country of Oddivisha to enter the diamond way.  He set off then for the city, bringing along with him a little boy and a little girl that He had emanated.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS17\", \"acip\": \"DE NAS KUN TU BZANG PO'I SPYOD PAS SLOB DPON RANG NYID LA MI MOS PA'I 'GRO BA RNAMS KYI MA DAD PA BZLOG PAR BYA BA'I CHED DANG, MA 'ONGS PA'I SEMS CAN DMAN PA RNAMS KYI DON DU THUGS RJE CHEN PO'I SPRUL PA SPYAN DRANGS PA'I DGOS PA YONGS SU BSGRUB PA'I SLAD DANG, AOO dE BI SHA'I YUL NA GNAS PA'I SKYE BO RNAMS RDO RJE THEG PA LA GZUD PAR DGONGS PA BCAS KYI DON DU GRONG KHYER DU 'BYON PAR BZHED DE SPRUL PA'I BU DANG PU MO GNYIS SHIG KYANG SPRUL TO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de nas kun tu bzang po'i spyod pas slob dpon rang nyid la mi mos pa'i 'gro ba rnams kyi ma dad pa bzlog par bya ba'i ched dang, ma 'ongs pa'i sems can dman pa rnams kyi don du thugs rje chen po'i sprul pa spyan drangs pa'i dgos pa yongs su bsgrub pa'i slad dang, aoo De bi sha'i yul na gnas pa'i skye bo rnams rdo rje theg pa la gzud par dgongs pa bcas kyi don du grong khyer du 'byon par bzhed de sprul pa'i bu dang pu mo gnyis shig kyang sprul to\", \"english\": \"And then the Master decided to return to the city, for three reasons.  First of all, He wanted to put a stop to the lack of faith that people were feeling towards Him, by showing them the pureness of His actions.  He further wanted to accomplish the purpose for which the emanation of the Compassionate One had been invited, in order to help lower kinds of living beings in the future.  And then thirdly He had the idea that He could get the people of the country of Oddivisha to enter the diamond way.  He set off then for the city, bringing along with him a little boy and a little girl that He had emanated.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 20537,
        "course": "TCS17",
        "seq": 675,
        "acip": "DE LTAR PHYI ROL GYI BUD MED MA RNYED NA YE RGYA LA BRTEN PAR BSHAD PA NI, YE RGYAS PHYI'I BUD MED BSTEN PA LTAR GYI BDE BA CHEN PO'I TING NGE 'DZIN BSKYED MI NUS PA YIN LA, PHYI'I BUD MED RNYED KYANG DE SPANGS NAS YE RGYA LA BRTEN PAR GSUNGS PA NI SNGA MA LAS BZLOG PA LA DGONGS SO,,",
        "wylie": "de ltar phyi rol gyi bud med ma rnyed na ye rgya la brten par bshad pa ni, ye rgyas phyi'i bud med bsten pa ltar gyi bde ba chen po'i ting nge 'dzin bskyed mi nus pa yin la, phyi'i bud med rnyed kyang de spangs nas ye rgya la brten par gsungs pa ni snga ma las bzlog pa la dgongs so",
        "english": "When they state this way that--if you can't find an outer woman, then you can practice with an imaginary one--it's with reference to the fact that practice with an imaginary partner doesn't have the capacity to bring on in us the meditation of great bliss in the same way that practice with an actual partner does.  To say though that you do find an outer woman, and then leave off practicing with Her, and practice instead with an imaginary woman, is to interpret the lines in a completely opposite way than what we've seen above.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS17\", \"acip\": \"DE LTAR PHYI ROL GYI BUD MED MA RNYED NA YE RGYA LA BRTEN PAR BSHAD PA NI, YE RGYAS PHYI'I BUD MED BSTEN PA LTAR GYI BDE BA CHEN PO'I TING NGE 'DZIN BSKYED MI NUS PA YIN LA, PHYI'I BUD MED RNYED KYANG DE SPANGS NAS YE RGYA LA BRTEN PAR GSUNGS PA NI SNGA MA LAS BZLOG PA LA DGONGS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de ltar phyi rol gyi bud med ma rnyed na ye rgya la brten par bshad pa ni, ye rgyas phyi'i bud med bsten pa ltar gyi bde ba chen po'i ting nge 'dzin bskyed mi nus pa yin la, phyi'i bud med rnyed kyang de spangs nas ye rgya la brten par gsungs pa ni snga ma las bzlog pa la dgongs so\", \"english\": \"When they state this way that--if you can't find an outer woman, then you can practice with an imaginary one--it's with reference to the fact that practice with an imaginary partner doesn't have the capacity to bring on in us the meditation of great bliss in the same way that practice with an actual partner does.  To say though that you do find an outer woman, and then leave off practicing with Her, and practice instead with an imaginary woman, is to interpret the lines in a completely opposite way than what we've seen above.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 20687,
        "course": "TCS17",
        "seq": 825,
        "acip": "LUGS LAS BZLOG PA YIS GNAS PA NI, BRTZON CHEN MA NAS RAB GTUM MA'I BAR GYI MAS YAR GNAS PA'O,,",
        "wylie": "lugs las bzlog pa yis gnas pa ni, brtson chen ma nas rab gtum ma'i bar gyi mas yar gnas pa'o",
        "english": "As for Ladies who are positioned in the reverse order, we have those from the Lady of Great Effort up to the Lady of Perfect Fire, going from bottom to top.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS17\", \"acip\": \"LUGS LAS BZLOG PA YIS GNAS PA NI, BRTZON CHEN MA NAS RAB GTUM MA'I BAR GYI MAS YAR GNAS PA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"lugs las bzlog pa yis gnas pa ni, brtson chen ma nas rab gtum ma'i bar gyi mas yar gnas pa'o\", \"english\": \"As for Ladies who are positioned in the reverse order, we have those from the Lady of Great Effort up to the Lady of Perfect Fire, going from bottom to top.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 21485,
        "course": "TCS18Commentary",
        "seq": 256,
        "acip": "KHRO BCU LTA BU BSGOMS NAS PHYIR BZLOG PA 1 DANG, BRIS NAS 'DOGS PAS PHYIR BZLOG 2 TSUL SHES PA DANG, BUM DBANG SNGON DU SONG BA'I GSANG BA BSKUR BA 3 DANG, SHES RAB YE SHES DANG, DBANG BZHI PA BSKUR BA 4 DANG, DGRA BO SRUNG MA DANG PHRAL NAS MNGON SPYOD BYED TSUL GYI KHA SBYOR DBYE BA 5 DANG,",
        "wylie": "khro bcu lta bu bsgoms nas phyir bzlog pa 1 dang, bris nas 'dogs pas phyir bzlog 2 tshul shes pa dang, bum dbang sngon du song ba'i gsang ba bskur ba 3 dang, shes rab ye shes dang, dbang bzhi pa bskur ba 4 dang, dgra bo srung ma dang phral nas mngon spyod byed tshul gyi kha sbyor dbye ba 5 dang",
        "english": "These are explained as follows: (1) Our Lama knows how to use a meditation such as that on the Ten Angels of Ferocity to force obstacles away; (2) They know the way to write a special spell on a piece of paper, and attach it to something, in order to force these same obstacles away; (3) They know how to impart the secret, beginning with the empowerment of the vase as a preliminary; (4) They know how to impart the empowerment of the Woman of Wisdom, and the fourth of the empowerments; and (5) They know how to \"split the capsule\"; which is to say, They know how to separate an evil person from their personal guardian, and part them from their life.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS18Commentary\", \"acip\": \"KHRO BCU LTA BU BSGOMS NAS PHYIR BZLOG PA 1 DANG, BRIS NAS 'DOGS PAS PHYIR BZLOG 2 TSUL SHES PA DANG, BUM DBANG SNGON DU SONG BA'I GSANG BA BSKUR BA 3 DANG, SHES RAB YE SHES DANG, DBANG BZHI PA BSKUR BA 4 DANG, DGRA BO SRUNG MA DANG PHRAL NAS MNGON SPYOD BYED TSUL GYI KHA SBYOR DBYE BA 5 DANG,\", \"wylie\": \"khro bcu lta bu bsgoms nas phyir bzlog pa 1 dang, bris nas 'dogs pas phyir bzlog 2 tshul shes pa dang, bum dbang sngon du song ba'i gsang ba bskur ba 3 dang, shes rab ye shes dang, dbang bzhi pa bskur ba 4 dang, dgra bo srung ma dang phral nas mngon spyod byed tshul gyi kha sbyor dbye ba 5 dang\", \"english\": \"These are explained as follows: (1) Our Lama knows how to use a meditation such as that on the Ten Angels of Ferocity to force obstacles away; (2) They know the way to write a special spell on a piece of paper, and attach it to something, in order to force these same obstacles away; (3) They know how to impart the secret, beginning with the empowerment of the vase as a preliminary; (4) They know how to impart the empowerment of the Woman of Wisdom, and the fourth of the empowerments; and (5) They know how to \\\"split the capsule\\\"; which is to say, They know how to separate an evil person from their personal guardian, and part them from their life.\"}"
      },
